ompt","system_tools","mcp_tools","mcp_instructions","mcp_tools_deferred","system_tools_deferred","custom_agents","memory_files","skills","messages","autocompact_buffer","compact_buffer","free_space"],rF=f(()=>u({name:o().describe('Display name of the row as the CLI renders it, e.g. "Messages" or "MCP tools (deferred)". Use `kind` (not this name) to classify the row.'),tokens:E().int(),kind:F(K7n).describe("What the row is: 'used' content occupies the window; 'free' is the remaining window; 'buffer' is the compaction reserve (autocompact or manual); 'deferred' rows are out-of-window tool schemas \u2014 listed for awareness, excluded from usage math.")}).describe("One row of the /context usage-by-category breakdown. Rows may carry zero tokens; renderers typically hide those.")),oF=f(()=>u({model:o().describe("Main-loop model the usage was computed for."),total_tokens:E().int().describe("Estimated tokens in use, unclamped \u2014 may exceed raw_max_tokens when over limit."),raw_max_tokens:E().int().describe("The window usage is measured against: the resolved autocompact window \u2014 the model's believed limit, or a smaller compaction-policy window (a configured value, or e.g. the 200K boundary on 1M-window models)."),percentage:E().int().describe("Rounded total_tokens / raw_max_tokens, 0-100+."),over_limit:u({tokens_over:E().int(),kind:F(["hard_limit","compaction_window"])}).optional().describe("Present when total_tokens exceeds raw_max_tokens. kind says how the window was resolved, not whether the API will accept the next request: 'hard_limit' means the window is the model's believed limit (the API will refuse past it); 'compaction_window' means a compaction-policy window, which may or may not coincide with the model's hard limit."),categories:C(rF()),mcp_tools:C(u({name:o().describe('Wire name, e.g. "mcp__linear__create_issue".'),server_name:o(),tokens:E().int()})),memory_files:C(u({path:o(),type:o().describe('Display label of the memory-file source, e.g. "Project" or "User".'),tokens:E().int()})),agents:C(u({agent_type:o(),source:o().describe("Raw source identifier, e.g. 'projectSettings', 'userSettings', 'plugin'. Built-in agents are excluded by the producer. Display labels are the renderer's concern."),tokens:E().int()})),skills:C(u({name:o(),source:o().describe("Raw source identifier, e.g. 'userSettings', 'plugin', 'syncedSkills'."),plugin_name:o().optional(),tokens:E().int()})).optional().describe("Omitted when no skills contribute tokens.")}).describe("Structured twin of the /context report \u2014 the data a client needs to render the context-usage card without parsing the markdown table. Evolves additively (new optional fields); a breaking reshape would ship as a sibling field, so consumers can trust the fields they know.")),mn=f(()=>u({type:T("assistant"),message:J0(),parent_tool_use_id:o().nullable(),error:go().optional(),uuid:p(),historical:M().optional().describe("@internal Replayed history rather than a live message (stamped by the Remote Control bridge when it flushes history to the session server)."),session_id:o(),request_id:o().optional(),refused_message_id:o().optional().describe("@internal Only on the assistant message that reports a refusal: the API msg_* id of the refused response (message.id on this message is a local UUID). Absent when the CLI has no well-formed id for it."),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Client uuid of the user message this turn is answering (submitMessage options.uuid), stamped on an assistant message each time that send changes \u2014 the turn's FIRST top-level assistant message (which may carry only a thinking block, or be a synthetic API-error message), and then, for a turn started by a synthetic (meta) prompt, the first assistant message after each queued user message folded in mid-turn (the fold takes the echo over); with --include-partial-messages the turn's first non-ping stream event is stamped too, independently (see SDKPartialAssistantMessage), so the same uuid may appear on both \u2014 either binds the reply to the send it answers without waiting for the result; the server keeps the first stamp it sees per uuid. A turn started by a typed prompt keeps that uuid for its whole turn, so it stamps once per frame kind. A meta turn's own uuid is stamped only when the host vouches it is the client event's own (on a hosted session, the uuid the session server persisted: delivered content such as a Slack owner ping, a Slack-bot observation or a client-injected synthetic turn), never for a prompt the CLI minted itself \u2014 except that a turn carrying resume_reason, such as the boot-time rescue turn re-running a turn a worker restart interrupted mid-way, stamps the interrupted turn's own last user prompt, the send it answers, where that can be vouched for (a turn that goes on from a permission answer or from in-flight tool calls may first re-send, unstamped, the assistant message that made those calls); either way a user message folded into a meta turn takes the echo over from it (the rescue turn absorbing messages sent while the session was down; a bot-observation turn absorbing a human's post), and the first reply frame of each kind after that fold carries the folded message's uuid \u2014 the first reply that message got. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model. Absent on every other frame of the turn, on subagent frames (parent_tool_use_id set), on turns that neither had a client uuid nor folded a user message in, and from older producers."),user_message_uuids:C(o()).optional().describe("Client uuids of every user message whose prompt this turn has consumed so far, in consumption order \u2014 all members of a prompt batch the host merged into this one turn (several messages sent close together run as one turn whose user_message_uuid is the LAST member's), then any user message folded into the turn before this frame \u2014 so a consumer that sent any of them can bind this reply to its own send by finding its uuid anywhere in the list. Always contains user_message_uuid; at most 64 entries. Present exactly when user_message_uuid is, on the same frames; absent from older producers (fall back to user_message_uuid)."),resume_reason:o().optional().describe("Why this frame's turn continues one that a worker restart interrupted. Such a turn is the automatic re-run of the interrupted turn (CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN), or one that goes on from the answer to the permission prompt the new worker took over, or from that turn's tool calls still in flight. The value is the host's CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_REASON when it set one (host_draining, checkpoint_restore, container_recreated, \u2026), else 'interrupted_turn'. Stamped on the same reply frames as user_message_uuid (which on such a turn names the interrupted turn's own last user prompt), so a consumer can tell its first reply from the interrupted attempt's. Absent on every other turn (also one that goes on from the answer to any other permission prompt), on thinking_tokens frames, and from older producers."),resumed_from_incomplete_thinking:T(!0).optional().describe("This turn continued the preceding truncated assistant turn inside its trailing signed thinking block (max-output-tokens recovery). Its thinking signatures are cumulative over that preceding thinking-only turn, so a history replayed through the bridge must carry this flag back for the normalizer to keep the run's prefix on the wire. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),supersedes:C(p()).optional().describe("Wire uuids of previously-delivered messages that this message replaces (refusal-fallback supersede). The list can include tombstoned tool_result frames from the refused leg, not only assistant frames. Evict the named messages on arrival and treat this frame as their canonical replacement. Idempotent with the end-of-turn model_refusal_fallback notice, whose retracted_message_uuids remains the complete audit record for the turn."),aborted:T(!0).optional().describe("True when this assistant message was truncated by an interrupt/abort before the stream completed: stop_reason was never received and the content may end mid-word. Absent on normally completed messages."),agent_id:o().optional().describe("ID of the subagent that produced this message: the `task_id` of its task events, unchanged when the subagent is resumed. Absent on main-thread messages."),subagent_type:o().optional().describe("Subagent type that produced this message."),task_description:o().optional().describe("Description of the subagent task that produced this message."),tool_use_meta:C(u({id:o(),display_name:o(),server_display_name:o().optional(),icon_url:o().optional(),server_id:o().optional(),server_id_stamp:o().optional(),tool_name:o().optional()})).optional().describe("@internal Display metadata for this message's tool_use blocks, keyed by block id. display_name is the MCP server's `tool.annotations.title` when provided, otherwise a readable transform of the wire name; server_display_name is the MCP server's own display name; icon_url is the MCP server's directory icon URL (claude.ai connectors only); server_id is the mcpsrv_ connector id of a server provisioned by claude.ai, set by the CLI from claude.ai's own connector listing, so a client may show the call as that connector's. server_id_stamp is the X-MCP-Server-ID header value, lower-cased, of an http entry in the dynamic scope (passed in at launch or by a later mcp_set_servers push). Claude Code Remote writes the connector's uuid there when the session has one, otherwise an id it generates; the CLI checks only its shape (at most 128 characters of letters, digits, '.', '_', ':' and '-'; anything else yields no stamp), not whose it is, since a --mcp-config or SDK-host entry can write anything. A client may look it up in its own server list, treat a miss as a server it does not know (never as an error), and show a hit as the entry's claim to be that server, not as proof. They are separate fields: a server provisioned by claude.ai carries server_id, a dynamic-scope http entry server_id_stamp. Both absent for a plugin's, a repo's, a settings or an agent entry. Neither is a permission grant. tool_name is the tool's name as its server lists it, without the mcp__<server>__ prefix. Omitted for blocks whose display label equals the wire name (built-in tools). Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),narration_block_indexes:C(E().int().nonnegative()).optional().describe("@internal Ascending zero-based indexes into this frame's `message.content` of the thinking blocks whose signature the server tagged block_kind 'narration' (server summaries of the prose between tool calls, not the model's own reasoning), so a renderer can label them as summaries without decoding the signature envelope. Fail-closed: an unparseable or legacy signature is not listed. A listed block can have empty thinking text when the request returned no thinking text; give those ordinary thinking treatment. Omitted when the frame has no such block, and by CLI versions before this field existed; treat unlisted thinking blocks as ordinary thinking. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),narration_hint:F(["hidden","faint"]).optional().describe("@internal Display-only, for this frame's narration_block_indexes blocks: how to draw their trailing ' \xB7 summarized' hint, by the same rule Claude Code's terminal UI uses for the model that produced the frame. 'hidden': draw no hint. 'faint': draw ' \xB7 summary'. Omitted: draw ' \xB7 summarized'. Sent only to the VS Code extension and only on frames that have such a block; older CLI versions never send it. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),thinking_duration_ms:E().int().positive().optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal How long the thinking block in this frame took to stream, in whole milliseconds (at least 1): from its content_block_start to its content_block_stop, so the wait for the response's first token is not counted. For display only. Sent only on a frame whose one content block is a thinking block that was streamed. Older CLI versions never send it, and a CLI that rebuilt its history from SDK frames does not send it when it replays that history, so be ready to show the block without a duration. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),thinking_display:o().optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal The thinking.display that Claude Code put in the API request body for the response that streamed this frame's thinking block. It has the same source and meaning as `thinking_display` on a stream event. Sent only on a frame whose one content block is a thinking block that was streamed, and only when the body had a display. Older CLI versions never send it, and a CLI that rebuilt its history from SDK frames does not send it when it replays that history. An absent key states nothing. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),timestamp:o().optional().describe("ISO timestamp of when this content block finished on the originating process. One API assistant turn may produce several assistant messages sharing a message.id, each with its own timestamp. Uses the originating host's clock, so it's for display only; do not order messages by this field. Older emitters omit it; consumers should fall back to receive time."),is_meta:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal True when the message was synthesized by the loop (not a model response)."),context_usage:oF().optional().describe("Structured twin of the /context report, carried on the synthetic assistant message that delivers the markdown table. Present only on /context results from CLIs new enough to attach it; the markdown in message.content remains the canonical fallback. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),usage_report:gWo().optional().describe("Structured twin of the /usage report, carried on the synthetic assistant message that delivers its text: the session totals, the plan's usage rows and extra-usage spend, for remote clients that render a card from data. Present only on /usage results from CLIs new enough to attach it and from claude.ai-subscriber sessions; the text in message.content remains the canonical fallback. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),local_command_source:o().optional().describe("@internal The originating system local_command row's wire-form content (escaped frames), carried on the loop-synthesized local-command twin. Round-tripped so toInternalMessages rebuilds the internal system row on a bridge/SDK history replay \u2014 replay then matches the live session, where that row is model-visible \u2014 instead of dropping the output or replaying the twin's decoded display text. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content`."),local_command_run:u({command:o(),args:o(),plugin:o().optional()}).optional().describe("@internal On the local-command twin, the run that printed the row: the command's name as `command.run` carried it (no slash), its arguments as the echo shows them (`***` when the command marks them sensitive) and, for a command a plugin registered, that plugin's name, or, for a row whose text plugins' `command.run` hooks answered or retold, the names the text is led by (untrusted text; absent for the binary's own command in its own words). Present when a `local` command's dispatch printed the row or a `command.run` hook answered it, absent on a never-ran notice. A surface that draws the hooks engine's `CommandOutput` site asks ui_render with these, the twin's text and its uuid as instance_id; toInternalMessages round-trips it onto the rebuilt row. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content`."),local_command_outcome:u({kind:F(eo),suggestion:o().optional().describe("unknown: the registered command closest to the typed name, no slash.")}).optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal On the local-command twin, what the row reports, so a host can offer a fix instead of relaying the text: 'unavailable_headless' (an interactive-panel command this session cannot open), 'unknown' (no command has the typed name), 'failed' (the command ran and failed), 'restart_required' (the change applies after Claude Code restarts). Absent when the row reports none of these; new kinds are additive, so treat an unknown one as absent. toInternalMessages round-trips it onto the rebuilt row. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content`."),is_virtual:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Display-only: rendered in the UI but filtered before API send."),batch_tool_uses:C(Le({id:o(),name:o()})).optional().describe("@internal `{id, name}` of the original Batch* tool_use block(s) for a message whose content was decomposed into synthetic v1 tool_use blocks. Round-tripped so a replayed history reassembles the batch block on the wire (not N synthetics). Input is reassembled from the synthetics in `message.content` \u2014 never carried here. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content` \u2014 so it is not replayed to the model."),wire_tool_inputs:fe(o(),oe()).optional().describe("@internal `tool_use.input` exactly as the API produced it, keyed by tool_use id, for a message whose `message.content` carries the client-normalized input. Round-tripped so a replayed history echoes each earlier tool call back to the API as the API emitted it (the server may hold its own copy of the turn). Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content`."),wire_ingest_context:fe(o(),oe()).optional().describe("@internal What the client-side input normalization read from process state (the working directory a leading `cd` was stripped against, the stamp applied to a Slack send) for the inputs in `wire_tool_inputs`, keyed by the same tool_use ids. Round-tripped with `wire_tool_inputs` so a replayed history can verify each recorded input against the normalized one exactly. Wrapper-level sibling \u2014 never inside `message.content`."),is_api_error_message:M().optional().describe("@internal True when this assistant message wraps an API error (from internal AssistantMessage.isApiErrorMessage)."),api_error_status:E().int().optional().describe("@internal HTTP status code of the API error when is_api_error_message is true."),api_error:F(["max_output_tokens","dlp_request_denied","claude_code_version_too_old","safety_monitor_blocked","effort_requires_thinking","advisor_incompatible","tool_history_mismatch","autocompact_thrashing","pdf_too_large","pdf_password_protected","media_removed","no_response","tls_untrusted_ca","gateway_content_type","provider_credentials","gateway_signin_required","gateway_session_expired","api_key_auth_disabled","org_disabled_credential","invalid_credential_header","model_requires_usage_credits","long_context_credits_required","consent_unanswered","no_allowed_fallback","model_substitution_disabled","field_not_granted","usage_limit_reached"]).optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal Typed kind of the API error when is_api_error_message is true, for consumers that key on the cause instead of the message text (the text stays the fallback and may change). Absent when the error has no typed kind; new values are added over time, so treat an unknown value as absent. dlp_request_denied: the request was refused by the organization's data-loss-prevention policy \u2014 terminal for that request body; a consumer replaying history must not re-send it. claude_code_version_too_old: the API refused the request because this Claude Code build is below a required minimum version \u2014 the selected model's minimum, or an organization-policy floor; only the message text says which and names the versions, and the remedy is updating Claude Code. safety_monitor_blocked: a server-side safety monitor blocked the model's response for this turn; terminal for the turn, so a consumer replaying history must not re-send the prompt that led to it. effort_requires_thinking: the model refuses the requested effort level while thinking is off; api_error_params.effort names the level. advisor_incompatible: the configured advisor model cannot be paired with this request's model. tool_history_mismatch: the API rejected the conversation's tool_use/tool_result pairing (a tool_use without its result, an orphaned tool_result, a duplicate tool_use id); re-sending the same history fails the same way, and rewinding the conversation is the remedy. autocompact_thrashing: the context refilled to the limit right after each automatic compaction, repeatedly, so the turn stopped; something in context is too large for the window. pdf_too_large: a PDF is over the API's page or size limit. pdf_password_protected: a PDF is encrypted. media_removed: the API refused an image or a document in the conversation, so Claude Code leaves it out of the requests that follow. The frame comes either before the answer of a retry that already left it out, or as the error that ended the turn (the result then repeats this value). api_error_params.media_reason says why, and api_error_params.media which kind of block. Other kinds (pdf_too_large, pdf_password_protected) and some untyped errors also leave the block out, so the absence of this value does not mean that nothing was left out. no_response: no response arrived before the first-byte deadline on any attempt, usually a proxy or gateway that holds responses until they complete. tls_untrusted_ca: the API's TLS certificate comes from an authority this process does not trust, usually a TLS-inspecting proxy or a gateway signed by a private CA. gateway_content_type: a proxy between Claude Code and Amazon Bedrock rewrote Bedrock's event-stream response. provider_credentials: a third-party cloud provider (api_error_params.provider) refused the request's credentials or access; api_error_params.remedy says what fixes it. gateway_signin_required: the Cloud gateway provider is configured and nobody is signed in to it; no request was sent. gateway_session_expired: the Cloud gateway session expired; no request was sent; api_error_params.remedy is gateway_token when the token came from the environment, which signing in does not replace. api_key_auth_disabled: the organization turned off API key authentication and the active credential is an API key. org_disabled_credential: the ANTHROPIC_API_KEY environment variable belongs to a disabled organization and overrides the account sign-in. invalid_credential_header: a credential or header value (from the environment, a helper, or the saved sign-in) cannot be sent as an HTTP header; caught before the request was sent. model_requires_usage_credits: the model runs on usage credits for this account, and credits are off or the included allowance is used up. long_context_credits_required: a request over the 200K-token context boundary needs usage credits. consent_unanswered: the model now uses usage credits and the prompt to confirm went unanswered; nothing was sent. no_allowed_fallback: switching off the model was needed (the usage-credits prompt was declined) and the model policy allows no other model. model_substitution_disabled: switching off the model was needed and CLAUDE_CODE_NO_MODEL_FALLBACK forbids it. field_not_granted: the organization is not allowed to send a field in the request, and the message text says what to do. usage_limit_reached: the request was refused by the account's own usage limit, a plan limit (session, weekly or per-model) or a spend cap on usage credits; api_error_params.rate_limit_info says which and when it resets. The other refusals that share error rate_limit never carry it: an organization policy that turned the service off (no limit was reached, and no reset clears it), a temporary server throttle, a capacity switch-off, an entitlement refusal shown in the server's own words, or a 429 on an API key or a third-party cloud provider."),api_error_params:u({effort:o().optional(),provider:F(["bedrock","anthropicAws","mantle","anthropicGoogleCloud","vertex","foundry","gateway"]).optional(),remedy:F(["refresh_command","refresh_credentials","adc","gateway_token","host_managed","model_access"]).optional(),media:F(["image","document"]).optional().catch(void 0),media_reason:F(["unprocessable","unsupported_by_model","media_budget"]).optional().catch(void 0),rate_limit_info:Xjr().optional().catch(void 0)}).optional().describe("@internal Parameters of api_error, present only for the kinds that have any. effort: the effort level the API refused (effort_requires_thinking). media (media_removed): the kind of block the API refused; absent for media_budget. media_reason (media_removed): unsupported_by_model \u2014 the API said that the model does not accept PDF documents; media_budget \u2014 the request's images and documents together exceed the API's limit, and the requests that follow may leave out blocks of either kind; unprocessable \u2014 every other refusal, a gateway's report of a model's refusal among them. provider: the API provider whose credentials failed (provider_credentials, gateway_session_expired). remedy (provider_credentials, gateway_session_expired): refresh_command \u2014 a configured refresh command (awsAuthRefresh / gcpAuthRefresh) refreshes the credentials; refresh_credentials \u2014 refresh the provider's credentials by hand, no command is configured; adc \u2014 refresh the Google application default credentials or the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS key file; gateway_token \u2014 replace the gateway token supplied through ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN or ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS; host_managed \u2014 the host application owns these credentials; model_access \u2014 the credentials work but the model is not enabled for this account and region (Amazon Bedrock). rate_limit_info (usage_limit_reached): what the refused request's own response said about the limit, in the shape rate_limit_event carries. It is read from the same values as the message text, so its status, limit and reset are the text's: status is rejected and isUsingOverage false, rateLimitType names the limit, resetsAt is when it resets, and overageStatus, overageResetsAt, overageDisabledReason and limitScope say whose spend cap it is (the person's own cap arrives as org_level_disabled_until, as on rate_limit_event); no other member is sent. It is that refusal's record and is never updated, so compare resetsAt with the clock before calling the limit current."),api_error_code:o().optional().describe("@internal The server's error.details.error_code for this API error, copied through when it is an identifier (^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}$) and dropped otherwise. Carries server gate codes this build has no api_error value for, so a host can key on a new gate without a Claude Code release. Absent when the response carried no code."),api_error_origin:F(["first_party"]).optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal first_party when this API error was built from a response Anthropic's own API sent: the request used the firstParty provider, went to https://api.anthropic.com on the default port and was answered from there, not redirected, over TLS verified with no added CA (NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS), no verification override and no unix-socket tunnel. Absent in every other case: an ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL or WIF base_url override, a gateway, a third-party provider, a connection error, an error Claude Code raised itself, and any frame replayed from the transcript, SDK input or a remote session. Treat absence as unknown origin. It says which endpoint answered, not that the message text is the server's verbatim, and a CA installed in the system trust store can still intercept. An invalid-request message (400 or 422) can quote request values, so it is stamped only when it is one of the known Anthropic wordings, and no message is stamped that quotes the request's model id; render stamped text as plain text. New values may be added; treat an unknown value as absent."),error_details:o().optional().describe("@internal Raw API error message \u2014 preserves details (e.g. prompt-too-long token counts) that user-facing content discards."),advisor_model:o().optional().describe("@internal Advisor model that produced this message, when applicable."),attribution_agent:o().optional().describe("@internal Attribution stamp: agent name parsed from querySource (see messageAttribution.ts). May overlap with subagent_type."),attribution_skill:o().optional().describe("@internal Skill that produced this message."),attribution_plugin:o().optional().describe("@internal Plugin that produced this message."),attribution_mcp_server:o().optional().describe("@internal MCP server that produced this message."),attribution_mcp_tool:o().optional().describe("@internal MCP tool that produced this message.")}).describe("An assistant message. While a response streams the CLI emits one assistant message per completed content block, so several consecutive assistant messages can share message.id and each carries just that block in message.content; on those, message.stop_reason is null and message.usage is not final \u2014 the turn's stop reason and total usage arrive on the result message. parent_tool_use_id is non-null when the message was produced inside a subagent started by that tool_use."));var aF=f(()=>u({type:T("rate_limit_event"),rate_limit_info:Xjr(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Rate limit event emitted when rate limit info changes.")),yy=f(()=>u({tool_name:o(),tool_use_id:o(),tool_input:fe(o(),oe())})),iF=f(()=>u({id:o(),name:o(),input:fe(o(),oe())})),Ry=f(()=>F(WA).describe("Why the query loop terminated. Unset when the loop was bypassed (local slash command).")),vy=f(()=>u({spawned:E().int().describe("Subagents actually started (one tengu_agent_tool_selected each); a refused or failed launch is not counted."),requested:u({background:E().int(),foreground:E().int(),unset:E().int()}).describe("Spawns by the run_in_background value the model passed; all count as unset while the parameter is not offered (background tasks disabled, or subagents forced into the background: in a terminal, the desktop app or the VS Code extension, or with CLAUDE_CODE_FORK_SUBAGENT=1)."),started_in_background:E().int().describe("Spawns that started in the background after defaults and session settings (tengu_agent_tool_selected.is_async); the rest blocked the spawning tool call."),by_type:fe(o(),E().int()).describe("Spawns by agent type."),max_depth:E().int().describe("Deepest spawn: 1 = started by the main thread, 2 = by a depth-1 subagent."),spawned_by_subagents:E().int().describe("Spawns made from inside another subagent (depth > 1)."),completed:E().int(),failed:E().int(),killed:u({parent:E().int(),user:E().int(),system:E().int()}).describe("Stopped before finishing: parent = by another agent through TaskStop; system = by Claude Code itself (the --max-budget-usd halt, the sweep of background subagents when an SDK or IDE client interrupts, or -p giving up on a background subagent still running at its wait ceiling); user = any other stop, including a foreground subagent cancelled just as it finished (counted here, not as completed)."),refused:u({depth_limit:E().int(),concurrency_limit:E().int(),budget:E().int()}).describe("Agent tool calls turned down because one of these limits was reached (other denials, such as an unknown agent type, are not counted). depth_limit stays near zero in practice: a subagent at the nesting limit is normally not offered the tool at all.")}).describe("@internal Subagents started through the Agent tool in this session, as running totals (forked skills, workflows, teammates and other internal agents are not counted; two sessions in one process keep separate counts). Cumulative like modelUsage: read the latest result rather than summing across results; a resumed session starts fresh and a mid-session /clear zeroes it, though a background subagent that outlives the /clear still records its outcome, so completed, failed and killed can then exceed spawned. A result that was held back while background subagents finished carries the counts as of when it is written to the stream, as do its total_cost_usd, duration_api_ms and modelUsage (and usage where that is a running total; a per-turn main-loop usage keeps its turn-end value). Omitted by hosts without a local Agent tool and on crash/startup-error results; all zeros until the Agent tool starts or refuses a subagent. A remote (isolation: remote) launch counts as spawned but never reports an outcome. Per-subagent detail is on the task_started / task_notification events.")),ky="Per-model totals for every model call made through the query pipeline during this query() call \u2014 main loop, Task subagents, sidechains, and internal calls such as compaction and Workflow agents. Cumulative across turns in streaming-input sessions: each result carries the running total so far, so read the latest result rather than summing across results. Internal helper calls outside the query pipeline (e.g. the permission classifier, token-count probes) are excluded; crash/startup-error results may carry zeroed usage, a resumed or forked session continues from the totals its transcript saved, when it has them (so the first result already carries the earlier turns), and a mid-session /clear resets the running total. The correct field for token/cost accounting; treat it as an estimate, not a billing statement.",Cy="@internal Running totals of the subagents the Agent tool started this session; see SDKSubagentStats.",zy="@internal How many times a safety system has stopped one of this session's model calls made through the query pipeline (main loop, subagents, forked skills, compaction and other internal calls), as a running total since the session started in this process; two sessions in one process keep separate counts. Counted: a response that ends on stop_reason refusal, whether Claude Code then shows it, retries it or re-sends it to another model; a refusal that the API itself answered on a fallback model; output that the API's content filter stopped for good (the stop ends the call); and, where the build can recognise it (public builds cannot), a server-side safety monitor's block (api_error safety_monitor_blocked). It counts these events, not calls: one call can add a refusal answered on a fallback model and then that model's own refusal. 0 means that none of these was seen; a kind of stop that this version does not know is not counted. Never sum across results. It is never saved, so a resumed session starts at 0 again, and a mid-session /clear zeroes it: over a stored log that spans a restart, a resume or a /clear, any result above 0 means a stop happened, and a later 0 does not cancel it. Present, 0 included, on every result that carries a turn's measured totals. Absent on the zeroed results that stand in for a crashed, cut or never-started turn, on Remote Control's results and from older producers, where it says nothing: absent is not 0. A call still running when a result is written (a background subagent's) shows on the next result; a result held back while background subagents finished carries the count as of when it is written to the stream, never less than it was built with. Not counted inside the pipeline: a stop of the API's content filter that Claude Code answers by streaming the request again (so 0 does not rule one out), an organization's data-loss-prevention denial (api_error dlp_request_denied), a block by a cloud provider's own guardrail, an advisor's refusal inside a reply that ends normally. Not counted at all: model calls outside the query pipeline (e.g. the permission classifier, token-count probes) and work in other processes.",Oy="User-initiated sends still waiting in the command queue when this result was produced. Greater than 0 means at least one more user turn (and result) follows without further input, barring cancellation; 0 means none is pending, or the session is ending (end_session or a shutdown latched mid-turn discards the backlog). Queued sends may coalesce into fewer turns, so this counts pending sends, not remaining results. System-generated queue entries are not counted. Absent on fatal startup results and on surfaces without a command queue.",Ny="Cumulative estimated cost in USD for this query() call, covering the same query-pipeline calls as modelUsage and sharing its lifecycle: cumulative across turns in streaming-input sessions \u2014 each result carries the running total so far, so read the latest result rather than summing across results. Crash/startup-error results may carry zeroed values, a resumed or forked session continues from the total its transcript saved, when it has one (so the first result already carries the earlier turns; maxBudgetUsd counts only the spend since this query() call started or last /clear), and a mid-session /clear resets the running total. An estimate, not a billing statement.",Iy="Delivery sequence of this result within the run: how many results the run numbered before this one, starting at 0, in the order the process writes them. A result held back while background work finishes is numbered when it is finally written, not when its text was produced; a result whose write fails still consumes its number, so a gap in a stream-json sequence means a result was lost. Distinct from num_turns, which counts model round-trips within one turn. Numbered by the process that hosts the run (`claude -p`, stream-json): a local client relaying a cloud session passes the cloud session's numbering through and its own locally built error results carry none; the in-process engine surface does not number yet. Absent from older producers.",Py="@internal The host of a multi-tenant worker shut the worker down while this turn ran. The shutdown was the first thing to abort the turn, and the turn is left for the session's next worker. The other frames of the ending, and terminal_reason, are the same as for a Stop from a client. Read this member to tell the two apart. A success result can have it, when the shutdown falls in a Stop hook. A result without it can also be the result of a shutdown. A shutdown that makes the turn write no interrupt marker does not set it, and older producers never set it.",lF=f(()=>u({phase:F(["setup","run"]).describe("@internal 'setup' when the session process never started, 'run' when it exited."),exit_code:E().int().nullable().optional(),signal:o().nullable().optional()}).describe("@internal How a self-hosted runner's session process ended, on the result the runner synthesizes for it.")),dF=f(()=>F(["org_pin_api_key_conflict","provider_not_allowed","org_verify_failed","org_pin_mismatch","managed_settings_invalid","remote_settings_required_unavailable","gateway_signin_required","gateway_access_denied","proxy_invalid","temp_dir_unusable","cwd_unavailable","shell_tool_missing","session_held_by_background","worktree_resume_refused","worktree_unverified","cli_version_too_old","bypass_root","org_config_required_unavailable","org_config_refused"]).describe("Why Claude Code refused to start, so a host can offer the fix instead of a retry. org_pin_api_key_conflict: managed settings pin a first-party or Cloud gateway sign-in, and an Anthropic API key or auth token is configured instead. provider_not_allowed: managed settings list the API providers this machine may use (allowedProviders), and the session is set up for one that is not listed. org_verify_failed: the sign-in's organization could not be verified against the pin (network, or a revoked token). org_pin_mismatch: the sign-in belongs to an organization the pin does not allow. managed_settings_invalid: managed policy settings could not be read, the pin names no organization, or managed model settings (deniedModels, or an availableModels list matched exactly) block the default model and leave no allowed model to use instead. remote_settings_required_unavailable: managed settings the organization requires could not be loaded. gateway_signin_required: the Cloud gateway ended this sign-in. gateway_access_denied: the Cloud gateway refused managed settings for this account. proxy_invalid: a proxy setting is not a complete URL. temp_dir_unusable: the per-user temp directory is unsafe or could not be created. cwd_unavailable: the working directory was deleted, moved or cannot be read. shell_tool_missing: Windows has no shell tool: Git Bash is missing, and PowerShell is missing or turned off by CLAUDE_CODE_USE_POWERSHELL_TOOL. session_held_by_background: the conversation to resume or continue is running as a background session. worktree_resume_refused: the resume was refused because the session's worktree failed its safety checks or the resume was launched from inside it; errors says whether a re-run continues without the worktree. worktree_unverified: the session's worktree could not be verified right now; retrying may succeed. cli_version_too_old: this Claude Code version is below the minimum Anthropic requires. bypass_root: bypass permissions mode was requested while running as root. org_config_required_unavailable: the organization requires its policy limits and managed settings before a session starts, and they could not be loaded (network, proxy, or an Anthropic error); retrying may succeed. org_config_refused: the organization requires its policy limits and managed settings, and Anthropic refused them for this sign-in (expired or revoked session, or the organization does not allow Claude Code for this account); signing in again or an admin's change is the fix, not a retry.")),cF=f(()=>u({type:T("result"),subtype:T("success"),duration_ms:E().int(),duration_api_ms:E().int(),ttft_ms:E().int().optional(),ttft_stream_ms:E().int().optional(),time_to_request_ms:E().int().optional(),time_to_request_phases_ms:fe(o(),E().int()).optional().describe("@internal How time_to_request_ms was spent, in integer ms per phase, summing to it exactly: system_prompt, resume_other (settling, before the turn's own prompt, tool calls that an earlier run left open or the session's client handed over, see turn_start_resume_kind; excluding four parts of it that have keys of their own: resume_store_confirm, writing a handed-over turn's lines to the transcript and waiting for the store to confirm them; resume_staged_files_wait, that turn's wait for files its client is still staging; resume_connector_wait, an orphaned permission's wait for its tool's MCP server to connect; turn_start_tool_run, the calls' permission checks, a person's time at a prompt included, and their runs, which are work the user asked for, not preparation; older producers report these five as one key, turn_start_resume), process_user_input (excluding four parts of it that have keys of their own: input_images, input_attachments, input_command, input_hooks), transcript_persist, tool_pool_refresh, system_prompt_rebuild (a second assembly of the system prompt, run when the tool pool re-read after the message was handled differs from the turn-start pool; older producers report it inside system_prompt), init_message, engine_pickup, memory_recall_wait, memory_context_wait, message_prep, model_config_wait, autocompact, query_setup, auto_mode_context_wait (the wait at dispatch for the auto-mode context, on a request that started one), model_gates, tool_schema_build, message_normalization, client_creation, request_build, then at most one of redrive_reactive_compact, redrive_fallback_model, redrive_non_streaming and redrive_other (present when the first model request of the turn never began streaming, or was answered without streaming, so time_to_request_ms runs to the send time of a later request: the time between the two sends, named for the reason; after a reply without streaming it includes that reply and any tool calls), and other (the remainder no phase covers). A phase that did not run on the turn is absent; other is always present. Present where time_to_request_ms is, on CLAUDE_CODE_REMOTE sessions and hosts that set CLAUDE_CODE_EMIT_STARTUP_TIMING; absent elsewhere and from older producers. See src/utils/turnRequestTiming.ts."),turn_start_resume_kind:F(FA).optional().describe("@internal What the turn was given to settle before its own prompt (the resume_ phases and turn_start_tool_run in time_to_request_phases_ms say how long that took): orphaned_permission (a permission answer whose pending request was lost, as when a worker restarts, so the tool call is settled with that answer), turn_handoff (a turn in progress that the session's client handed over), deferred_tool_use (a call a PreToolUse hook deferred), or none (resume_other is still reported, at about 0). A turn given more than one reports the first in that order. Present where time_to_request_phases_ms is."),handoff_intake_phases_ms:fe(o(),E().int()).optional().describe("@internal On a turn that the session's client handed over (turn_start_resume_kind is turn_handoff), how long its turn_handoff control request waited from its arrival on the session server's SSE stream (--sdk-url) until the input loop read it, in integer ms on the monotonic clock, split into parts that frame_intake_phases_ms explains: startup_wait, control_requests_ahead and user_frames_ahead, each absent when 0, and before_read (the rest), always present. The values sum to the wait. Present only on results that carry turn_start_resume_kind. Absent on a claude-code-server tenant, when the host had handled too much since the arrival to say what the request waited behind, and from older producers."),process_turn_index:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Number of earlier turns of this CLI process that sent a main-conversation model request: 0 is the process's first turn to reach the model, not the conversation's first. Turns stopped or failed after sending count but carry no value, so values can skip. Where one process hosts several sessions, each has its own count, from 0 each time the process starts hosting it. Present where time_to_request_phases_ms is."),time_to_request_cpu_ms:E().int().optional().describe("@internal CPU time (user plus system) the CLI process used from turn start until it sent the turn's first main-conversation model request. Normally that is the time_to_request_ms window; if that request was abandoned for a new call (not an ordinary retry), time_to_request_ms runs on to the new one and this does not. Near time_to_request_ms, the window was mostly computing; near 0, mostly waiting. It adds up all threads, so it can exceed the window, and leaves out child processes, such as a command a tool runs. Unrelated work counts, such as the request that names the session (usually in its first turn). On a virtual machine, time the host spends restoring memory can count as CPU time, so a high value alone does not mean JavaScript work. Present where time_to_request_phases_ms is, unless the runtime reports no resource usage or the process hosts other sessions too."),time_to_request_loop_lag_ms:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The longest single stall of the session's thread between turn start and the turn's first main-conversation model request, measured as how late a 50 ms timer fired: the real stall can be up to 50 ms longer, so one under 50 ms can read 0, and a few ms is timer noise. JavaScript ran without a break, or the thread waited inside a blocking call (a synchronous file read or child process), or the whole process was paused; time_to_request_cpu_ms below this value means one of the last two, though on a virtual machine a pause while the host restores memory can look like CPU time. Any work on the thread counts: the request that names the session (usually in its first turn), or another session's where several share the thread. Only the first two minutes are measured. Present where time_to_request_phases_ms is."),time_to_request_major_faults:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Major page faults (waits for memory to be read from disk) by the process in the time_to_request_cpu_ms window; Windows also counts faults needing no disk read. On a virtual machine, memory the host restores is not counted: the machine never sees those faults. Present where time_to_request_cpu_ms is."),process_rss_mib_turn_start:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Resident memory of the CLI process at turn start, in whole MiB, as its kernel counts it (VmRSS in /proc/self/status). From the process's second turn on, it shows what stayed resident through the idle time since the turn before. Child processes, such as a command a tool runs or an MCP server, are left out. On a virtual machine it is the guest kernel's count, not what the host holds for the machine. Sent only by a worker that Anthropic hosts (never by a self-hosted runner, a BYOC environment, a session that Claude Desktop starts or a local run), and only with telemetry on. On such a worker it is present, always with process_rss_mib_result, where process_turn_index is, on Linux (not WSL), unless /proc/self/status cannot be read or lacks VmRSS, or the process hosts other sessions too."),process_anon_mib_turn_start:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The anonymous part of process_rss_mib_turn_start (RssAnon): memory with no file behind it, such as heaps, stacks and code the JavaScript engine compiles while running. Mapped files, such as the executable, are left out. Present where process_rss_mib_turn_start is, if the kernel gives it."),process_rss_mib_result:E().int().optional().describe("@internal As process_rss_mib_turn_start, read again after the turn, just before this result is built: the level the turn leaves behind."),process_anon_mib_result:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The anonymous part of process_rss_mib_result; see process_anon_mib_turn_start. Present where process_rss_mib_result is, if the kernel gives it."),process_peak_rss_mib:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The most memory the process has had resident from its start until this result (VmHWM), in whole MiB. It is not the peak of this turn alone: a later turn repeats an earlier peak unless it went higher. Present where process_rss_mib_result is, if the kernel gives it."),process_cpu_ms_between_turns:E().int().optional().describe("@internal CPU time (user plus system, all threads, without child processes) the CLI process used from the end of its previous turn to this turn's start: mostly what it costs while no turn runs. A turn ends after its last frame and its turn-end hooks. Work it left running in the background counts here, as do this turn's steps ahead of turn start, such as re-reading the tool list. To get CPU time per second, add these up over many results and divide by the sum of their process_wall_ms_between_turns, which always comes with it. Present where process_cpu_ms_turn is, from the process's second turn of any kind on (process_turn_index can still be 0), unless the previous turn was abandoned midway and so never ended."),process_wall_ms_between_turns:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The time that passed in the process_cpu_ms_between_turns stretch, by a clock that never runs backward. Time while the process is stopped counts; time while its virtual machine is paused may not."),process_cpu_ms_turn:E().int().optional().describe("@internal CPU time, counted as in process_cpu_ms_between_turns, from this turn's start until just before this result is built, so what the turn does after that is in neither stretch. The stretch includes the waits for the model and for tools. It always comes with process_wall_ms_turn. Sent only by the workers named under process_rss_mib_turn_start. On them it is present where process_turn_index is, unless the runtime reports no resource usage or the process hosts other sessions too."),process_wall_ms_turn:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The time that passed in the process_cpu_ms_turn stretch, by the same clock as process_wall_ms_between_turns."),input_attachments_detail:u({slowest_producer:o().optional(),slowest_producer_ms:E().int().optional(),cpu_ms:E().int().optional(),major_faults:E().int().optional(),changed_files_reread:E().int().optional(),touch_compare:u({same:E().int().optional(),differs:E().int().optional(),via_outputs_link:E().int().optional(),partial_view:E().int().optional(),already_compared:E().int().optional(),link_on_path:E().int().optional(),leaf_link:E().int().optional(),nlink:E().int().optional(),not_regular:E().int().optional(),oversize:E().int().optional(),enoent:E().int().optional(),other:E().int().optional()}).optional()}).optional().describe("@internal What the input_attachments phase of time_to_request_phases_ms did on the turn, to tell a wait inside one attachment producer from a process that was busy. The producers are the calls of maybe() in getAttachments; their labels are a closed list, and a producer's time includes the computing of the others that run beside it. slowest_producer is the label of the one that took longest from its start to its result, among those that began within the phase, and slowest_producer_ms is that time; a background agent's pass that begins within the phase is timed with the turn's own (a new process's first turn has none), and its re-reads are not counted. Where slowest_producer_ms is well above cpu_ms that producer most likely waited (on a file, the network or a child process); a time_to_request_loop_lag_ms near slowest_producer_ms says instead that the thread itself stood still about that long (a blocking call, or a paused process). Where it is not well above cpu_ms the label tells little. cpu_ms and major_faults are time_to_request_cpu_ms's and time_to_request_major_faults's counters over this phase alone and are read the same way: cpu_ms far below the phase's time means the process was not computing; at or above it means computing, without saying whose. Where cpu_ms is absent, the phase's own time is what there is to compare slowest_producer_ms with. changed_files_reread is how many files the changed_files producer set out to read again in full because their modification time was later than the one recorded when the conversation last wrote or edited them (0 when it read none again, whether or not any file was a candidate; absent when the conversation's record of files was empty or the producer did not run). Present on turns where the input_attachments phase started, unless nothing of it is known. cpu_ms and major_faults are absent wherever time_to_request_cpu_ms is absent, and when the turn's first model request went out before the phase ended. touch_compare counts how the changed_files producer's compares ended in the main thread's pass (a subagent's are not counted). A compare checks a touched file against what the conversation last saw (its size, then its bytes), before deciding whether to read it again. same: the text had not changed, so it was not read again. differs: it had, so it was read again. partial_view and already_compared: the file was not compared (the conversation has seen only part of it or an older copy, or it was compared once already since the conversation last read or wrote it) and was read again. The rest are why a compare declined and the file was read again: link_on_path (the file could not be shown to be the one its path names: on Linux a symbolic link on the way, or a landing through the outputs link that the read check did not judge; or the file swapped or removed meanwhile), leaf_link (the open met a symbolic link: at the file, or on macOS anywhere on the way), nlink (the file has a second hard link), not_regular, oversize, enoent (the file was gone) and other (anything else, errors included). via_outputs_link counts those of same and differs that were reached through the hosted container's outputs link, from /mnt/user-data/outputs to /mnt/attach/outputs. Whole numbers; a zero count is left out, and touch_compare is absent when nothing was counted."),resume_store_confirm_detail:u({write_ms:E().int(),ahead_ms:E().int(),post_ms:E().int(),other_ms:E().int(),posts:E().int(),failed_posts:E().int(),rows_ahead:E().int(),rows:E().int()}).optional().describe("@internal How the resume_store_confirm phase of time_to_request_phases_ms was spent. Present where that phase is, in a session whose transcript goes to a hosted store: on the result of a handed-off turn whose lines were confirmed and that went on to a model request. A wait that ends its turn (the lines not stored, or the turn stopped meanwhile) is therefore not in the measure. The four _ms values sum to that phase exactly. write_ms: the transcript write, until the carried lines are in the queue of the lane that uploads transcript rows (POST /worker/internal-events, one POST out at a time); a POST that is out during the write is counted in posts and rows, but that part of its time is in write_ms only. The other three split the wait for that lane to empty. ahead_ms: a POST was out that held only rows queued before the hand-off's write began, one already out at that moment included. post_ms: any other POST was out, timed by the client from before the body is encoded to the response, each retry included. other_ms: no POST was out (a backoff between retries, picking and sizing the next batch, a busy event loop), plus rounding, any POST still out when the wait was cut short, and the time of POSTs after the 64th. posts: POST attempts that ended between the start of the write and the end of the wait; failed_posts: those that did not go through. rows_ahead: rows the lane had accepted and the store had not yet confirmed when the write began, queued or in a POST already out; rows written earlier but kept back for a compaction's summary count from when they are queued. rows: rows queued since then that a POST delivered, the carried lines among them."),user_message_uuid:o().optional(),user_message_uuids:C(o()).optional(),resume_reason:o().optional(),local_command:o().optional(),request_sent_wall_ms:E().optional(),first_request_input_tokens:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Input tokens of the model call whose send time is request_sent_wall_ms: input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens from that call's message_start. Lets a consumer group turn latency by prompt size. Present only with request_sent_wall_ms, and only when the sum is above zero; absent from older producers."),user_message_server_received_wall_ms:E().optional().describe("@internal The server_received_wall_ms of the user message that user_message_uuid names, copied unchanged, so the session server can measure from its own receipt to request_sent_wall_ms (two machines' clocks) without keeping the receipt time. Present only with request_sent_wall_ms, and only when that message carried a positive finite number there; absent from older producers."),first_content_frame_ms:E().int().optional(),first_stream_post_ms:E().int().optional(),first_stream_post_ack_ms:E().int().optional(),first_stream_post_queue_wait_ms:E().int().optional(),first_stream_post_queued_behind:F(Zr).optional(),first_stream_post_wall_ms:E().optional(),frame_received_wall_ms:E().optional().describe("@internal Date.now() (epoch ms) when the triggering send's frame arrived on a session reading its input from the session server's SSE stream (--sdk-url), before the input loop read it. With frame_enqueued_wall_ms and turn_started_wall_ms it splits the stretch between the server's persist and request_sent_wall_ms into transit, the input loop's handling of the frame, its wait on the command queue, and the turn's own work. Present only together with request_sent_wall_ms, and only when the transport recorded the receipt; absent on a local stdin host and from older producers."),frame_enqueued_wall_ms:E().optional().describe("@internal Date.now() (epoch ms) when the input loop put the triggering send's frame on the command queue. Present exactly when frame_received_wall_ms is."),turn_started_wall_ms:E().optional().describe("@internal Date.now() (epoch ms) when the turn's clock started: the anchor duration_ms, ttft_ms, ttft_stream_ms, time_to_request_ms, first_content_frame_ms, first_stream_post_ms and first_stream_post_ack_ms are measured from. Present exactly when frame_received_wall_ms is."),frame_intake_phases_ms:fe(o(),E().int()).optional().describe("@internal How the triggering send's frame spent the time from frame_received_wall_ms to frame_enqueued_wall_ms, in integer ms per step: before_read (until the input loop read the frame; a frame received more than once counts from the earliest receipt no earlier copy already took), and, carved out of before_read where the loop could tell them apart, startup_wait (the part before the input loop existed, the worker still starting), control_requests_ahead (the part the loop spent handling control requests before it read the frame) and user_frames_ahead (the part it spent on earlier user frames, each from its read until the loop moved on, at its delivery or its parking), so that before_read keeps only the rest (frames handled without being noted, and everything when no split could be made); then dedup, flag_settle, receive_hook, attachments, admission_wait (held behind a pending model switch, a pending MCP server-set change, start-up work the worker still owed its first turn, or an earlier held frame), admit_check (the host's decision whether to queue the frame, when it had to be awaited), and other (the rest). Steps that did not run are absent; before_read and other are always present. The values sum to frame_enqueued_wall_ms minus frame_received_wall_ms, unless other or before_read came out negative and was set to 0. Present exactly when frame_received_wall_ms is, except from older producers."),turn_start_phases_ms:fe(o(),E().int()).optional().describe("@internal How the time from frame_enqueued_wall_ms to turn_started_wall_ms was spent, in integer ms per step of the host's work through its message queue. Each step is where the host was at the time, which for part of the stretch may be an earlier message's turn. The steps: idle (the host was not working through its queue), earlier_turn (an earlier turn still running, including its work after its result), after_turn (the host's work after an earlier turn ended and before it took the next message: recording the outcome, writing the transcript and acknowledgements, and releasing the queue; also its handling of a crash), background_wait (waiting on background tasks before looking at the queue again), entry_joins (other waits when the host starts on its queue, before it may take a message), sdk_mcp_update (the claude -p host updating its SDK MCP servers when it starts on its queue), loop_top (reloading plugins that changed, at the start of each pass over the queue), session_start_wait (waiting for the plugins' first session.start hook, and taking the message off the queue), first_command (the claude -p host's waits for its plugin registry and a Projects mount before the first message it takes each time it starts on its queue), mcp_prewait (waiting for MCP servers still connecting, before that same first message), repo_wait (a claude-code-server session waiting for its repository before a turn), turn_joins (before every turn: starting the memory re-check, and waiting for a Projects mount and for SDK or newly added MCP servers), tool_pool (building the turn's tools), commit (marking the message taken and setting up its turn), after_arm (waiting for a cleared permission prompt's record to be written, and the host's other work once the turn is set up), turn_gate (work the host requires before every turn), turn_open (the host's turn-start hook and other turn setup), engine_setup (getting the engine for the turn; on the claude -p host's first turn, building its session), tool_reread (re-reading tools, MCP clients and settings for the turn), cwd (switching to the turn's working directory), and other (the rest: rounding, and the wall clock running ahead of the monotonic one). Values are rounded so that they add up: each is within 1 ms of its step's time, a step that comes out as 0 is absent, and other is always present. The values sum to turn_started_wall_ms minus frame_enqueued_wall_ms, except that they can exceed it, usually by 1 ms from rounding and by more if the wall clock stepped back. Present only when frame_received_wall_ms is, and absent from older producers."),turn_start_control_requests_ms:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Of the time from frame_enqueued_wall_ms to turn_started_wall_ms, the integer ms in which the claude -p host's message reader was held by control requests (such as mcp_set_servers or set_permission_mode) instead of reading the next message; for a request handled in the background, only its start counts. The host can keep working toward the turn meanwhile, so this overlaps turn_start_phases_ms and is not part of its sum. Absent when 0. A claude-code-server tenant does not time control requests, so its results never have this field."),first_text_post_ms:E().int().optional(),first_text_post_queue_wait_ms:E().int().optional(),first_text_post_queued_behind:F(Zr).optional(),first_text_post_wall_ms:E().optional(),time_to_request_from_spawn_ms:E().int().optional(),warm_spare_claimed:M().optional(),flag_fetch_kick:o().optional().describe("@internal One of FLAG_FETCH_KICKS, a string here so that list can change: whether this process started its start-up feature-flag fetch before the wait for it, or why not: the same value as system:init's startup_timing.flag_fetch_kick. started_in_init_* and started_after_init_*: it did, inside init() or right after. not_asked_warm: the flag cache was warm and nothing asked for an early start. settings_pending_*: settings that could change the fetch were still to be applied. launch_excluded: not a launch that starts it early. not_eligible: interactive, or no wait is budgeted. An _empty or _warm ending is the flag cache at that moment. Present only where time_to_request_from_spawn_ms is, and only when start-up judged it."),time_origin_ms:E().optional(),is_error:M(),api_error_status:E().int().nullable().optional(),api_error_code:o().optional().describe("@internal The api_error_code of the API error that ended the turn (see SDKAssistantMessage.api_error_code): the server's error.details.error_code when it is an identifier. Absent when the turn did not end on an API error or the response carried no code."),api_error_origin:F(["first_party"]).optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal The api_error_origin of the API error that ended the turn (see SDKAssistantMessage.api_error_origin): first_party when Anthropic's own API sent the response. Absent when the turn did not end on an API error or the origin is anything else; treat an unknown value as absent."),api_error:mn().shape.api_error.describe("@internal The api_error of the API error that ended the turn (see SDKAssistantMessage.api_error). Absent when the turn did not end on an API error or the error has no typed kind; treat an unknown value as absent."),num_turns:E().int(),result:o(),stop_reason:o().nullable(),total_cost_usd:E().describe(Ny),usage:Sy(),modelUsage:fe(o(),Bt()).describe(ky),subagent_stats:vy().optional().describe(Cy),safety_stops:E().int().nonnegative().optional().describe(zy),permission_denials:C(yy()),queued_turn_count:E().int().optional().describe(Oy),structured_output:oe().optional(),deferred_tool_use:iF().optional(),terminal_reason:Ry().optional(),interrupted_by_shutdown:T(!0).optional().describe(Py),result_index:E().int().nonnegative().optional().describe(Iy),fast_mode_state:$t().optional(),fast_mode_disabled_reason:Xt().optional(),origin:fo().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),hF=f(()=>u({type:T("result"),subtype:F(["error_during_execution","error_max_turns","error_max_budget_usd","error_max_structured_output_retries"]),duration_ms:E().int(),duration_api_ms:E().int(),is_error:M(),num_turns:E().int(),stop_reason:o().nullable(),total_cost_usd:E().describe(Ny),usage:Sy(),modelUsage:fe(o(),Bt()).describe(ky),subagent_stats:vy().optional().describe(Cy),safety_stops:E().int().nonnegative().optional().describe(zy),permission_denials:C(yy()),queued_turn_count:E().int().optional().describe(Oy),errors:C(o()),runner_exit:lF().optional().describe("@internal Set by the self-hosted runner on the result it synthesizes when the session process fails to start or exits abnormally (src/self-hosted-runner/failureResult.ts buildFailureEvents)."),startup_failure_reason:dF().optional().describe("Set on the zeroed error_during_execution result a stream-json run writes before exiting on a known startup failure; errors carries the same text as stderr. Failures that used to end with stderr alone write that result only when the host sets CLAUDE_CODE_STARTUP_FAILURE_RESULTS. Absent on every other result, on startup failures without a known cause, and from older producers."),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Client uuid of the user message that triggered this turn (submitMessage options.uuid), echoed back so a consumer can link this error result to the send it answers \u2014 the same join key the success variant echoes, carried alone (error turns have no request_sent_wall_ms to report). A delivery-failure result from the remote-session client echoes the failed send's queue key, which is client-minted when the host sent no uuid of its own. A synthetic/scheduled (meta) turn's own uuid is echoed only when the host vouches it is the client event's own (delivered content such as a Slack owner ping or a Slack-bot observation); a meta turn that folded queued user messages in mid-turn, vouched or not, echoes the LAST of them. Absent on turns that neither had a client uuid nor folded a user message in, on session-scoped failures with no single triggering send (a crashed worker's zeroed result), and from older producers."),user_message_uuids:C(o()).optional().describe("Client uuids of every user message whose prompt this turn consumed, in consumption order \u2014 all members of a prompt batch the host merged into this one turn (several messages sent close together run as one turn whose user_message_uuid is the LAST member's), then any queued user message folded into the running turn between tool rounds, once taken off the queue \u2014 so a consumer that sent any of them can bind this result to its own send by finding its uuid anywhere in the list. Always contains user_message_uuid; at most 64 entries; can be longer than the list on the turn's first reply frame. Present when a headless turn that ran echoes user_message_uuid; absent on delivery-failure and zeroed results and from older producers (fall back to user_message_uuid)."),resume_reason:o().optional().describe("Why this turn continued one that a worker restart interrupted. Such a turn is the automatic re-run of the interrupted turn (CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN), or one that goes on from the answer to the permission prompt the new worker took over, or from that turn's tool calls still in flight. The value is the host's CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_REASON when it set one (host_draining, checkpoint_restore, container_recreated, \u2026), else 'interrupted_turn'. Present on such a headless turn's result, success or error, with or without an echo (a turn whose opening user prompt could not be vouched for still carries the reason); absent on every other turn (also one that goes on from the answer to any other permission prompt), on the Remote Control bridge's per-turn synthetic results, and from older producers."),terminal_reason:Ry().optional(),interrupted_by_shutdown:T(!0).optional().describe(Py),result_index:E().int().nonnegative().optional().describe(Iy),fast_mode_state:$t().optional(),fast_mode_disabled_reason:Xt().optional(),origin:fo().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),uF=f(()=>Ue([cF(),hF()]).describe(`The outcome of a turn. The CLI emits exactly one result message per turn, after that turn's assistant, user and stream_event messages; treat it as the turn-complete signal (informational system messages such as task notifications, session state changes or prompt suggestions may still follow it). subtype "success" carries the final assistant text in result \u2014 or, with is_error true, the error text when the turn ended on an API error; the error subtypes say why the turn stopped early. In single-prompt (non-streaming-input) mode the process exits after the turn.`)),_n=f(()=>u({file:o().optional().describe("Path to the settings file that failed to parse or validate."),path:o().describe("Dot-notation path to the field with the error, or empty string for whole-file errors."),message:o().describe("Human-readable error message.")}).describe("A settings file parse or validation error. When a settings.json file fails to parse (invalid JSON, JSON comments, schema mismatch), the file is skipped and any rules it contained \u2014 including permission allow/deny lists \u2014 are not applied.")),bo="The plugin's version as declared in its plugin.json manifest, emitted verbatim (plugin-author-controlled \u2014 validate before trusting). Omitted when the manifest declares no version.";function My(){return u({id:o(),view_url:o(),device:Br("status",[u({status:T("bound"),device_id:o(),display_name:o().optional()}),u({status:T("unbound"),reason:o(),message:o()})]),directory_sync:u({state:F(["off","seeding","armed","stopped"]),reason:o().optional(),message:o().optional(),first_upload:o().optional(),synced_files:E().int().nonnegative().optional(),other_window:M().optional(),muted:M().optional(),started_from_upload:M().optional(),direction:F(["two_way","upload_only"]).optional().catch(void 0),file_mode:F(["container_sync","device_tools","unspecified"]),file_mode_source:F(["stored"])}),client_version:T(1),not_applied:C(u({name:o(),kind:F(["lost","kept","preference"])})).optional(),host:u({handle:o(),working_dir:o(),platform:o(),cli_version:o().optional()}).optional(),serving:u({state:F(["on","off","pending","revoked"]).catch("off"),reason:o().optional().catch(void 0),policy:F(["parity","interim"]).catch("interim"),channel:F(["session","bridge_only"]).catch("bridge_only")}).optional(),connection:F(["connecting","live","reconnecting"]).optional().catch(void 0),calls:u({live:C(u({call_id:o(),tool:o(),state:F(["running","asked"]).catch("running"),since:E(),ask_id:o().optional(),lapses_at:E().optional()})),recent:C(u({call_id:o(),tool:o(),ended:F(["completed","denied","withdrawn","refused","failed"]).catch("failed"),code:o().optional(),at:E()}))}).optional(),notices:C(u({key:o(),text:o()})).optional().catch(void 0)})}var pF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("init"),agents:C(o()).optional(),apiKeySource:RK(),startup_timing:fe(o(),oe()).optional().describe("@internal Cold-start telemetry for hosted (CCR) sessions: named startup phases and resume-hydration counters (StartupTiming in src/utils/startupTiming.ts). Absent elsewhere."),betas:C(o()).optional(),claude_code_version:o(),cwd:o(),tools:C(o()),mcp_servers:C(u({name:o(),status:o(),source:o().optional().describe("Where the server definition came from \u2014 same values as McpServerStatus.source (sdk | plugin | a config scope). Absent on CLIs that predate the field.")})),model:o(),permissionMode:de(),slash_commands:C(o()),terminal_slash_commands:C(o()).optional().describe("Subset of slash_commands whose UX is bound to the local terminal (e.g. exit, statusline). Phone/remote UIs should hide these from command menus; desktop surfaces may keep them. Present only when non-empty; absent on CLIs that predate the field, and on sessions where no advertised command carries the tag."),output_style:o(),skills:C(o()),plugins:C(u({name:o(),path:o(),source:o().optional().describe('@internal Plugin source identifier in "name\\@marketplace" format. Sentinels: "name\\@inline" for --plugin-dir, "name\\@skills-dir" for .claude/skills plugins, "name\\@synced" for plugins synced from claude.ai, "name\\@builtin" for built-in plugins.'),version:o().optional().describe(bo)})),plugin_errors:C(u({plugin:o(),type:o(),message:o(),path:o().optional().describe("Present only when a --plugin-dir, SDK `plugins` or synced directory entry did not load at all: the path of that entry, resolved against the cwd (for a directory, the value its `plugins[]` row would have carried; for a .zip, the archive; a --plugin-url entry carries none). `plugin` is then the positional `inline[N]` / `synced[N]` tag, so a host that mounts several directories pairs the error to its own by this path.")})).optional().describe("Plugin load-time errors. A plugin that did not load (an unmet dependency, a --plugin-dir entry that failed) is absent from `plugins[]`; a plugin that loaded without one of its components keeps its row and gets an entry here too. `plugin` is `name@marketplace`, or the positional `inline[N]` / `synced[N]` tag for a directory entry that failed before it had a name; `type` is a category from an open set (path-not-found, generic-error, manifest-validation-error, dependency-unsatisfied, hook-load-failed, \u2026) \u2014 treat a value you do not recognize as a generic failure; `message` is display text. The key is omitted when there are no errors; CI can fail on `(plugin_errors?.length ?? 0) > 0`. A session whose frames are persisted server-side (a Remote Control worker) always omits this key \u2014 plugin diagnostics stay in the local log there, so an omitted key does not assert a clean load."),plugin_warnings:C(u({plugin:o(),type:o(),message:o()})).optional().describe("@internal Plugin authoring feedback (e.g., a default folder shadowed by a manifest key). When `plugin` matches an entry in `plugins[]`, that plugin loaded and the warning is advisory; warnings with a synthetic `plugin` source (no matching `plugins[]` entry, e.g. workspace-level suppression notices) describe content that did NOT load. The key is omitted when there are no warnings. On connections that persist frames server-side (the local bridge-worker lane) this key is always omitted \u2014 plugin diagnostics stay in the local log, so an omitted key there does NOT assert a clean load."),mcp_server_errors:C(u({name:o(),type:o(),message:o()})).optional().describe("@internal MCP server config entries from --mcp-config that failed validation and were skipped (e.g. a `url` entry with no `type`). Affected servers are absent from `mcp_servers[]`. `type` is a stable category from an open set \u2014 currently unknown_type, url_missing_type, invalid_config, reserved_name, or (Remote Control child only) bridge_carrier_foreign_entry, bridge_carrier_not_http, bridge_carrier_url_mismatch, bridge_carrier_no_ingress_origin, bridge_carrier_no_session_id; treat values you do not recognize as a generic skip. The key is omitted when there are no errors; CI can fail on `(mcp_server_errors?.length ?? 0) > 0`. On connections that persist frames server-side (the local bridge-worker lane) this key is always omitted \u2014 the skipped-entry detail stays in the local log, so an omitted key there does NOT assert every entry validated."),fast_mode_state:$t().optional(),fast_mode_disabled_reason:Xt().optional(),footer_indicator:Co().optional().describe(`@internal The terminal's configured "\u25C6 <text>" footer pill \u2014 see SDKFooterIndicator. Absent when nothing is configured.`),effort:F(["low","medium","high","xhigh","max"]).nullable().optional().describe("The effort level the session will send on its next request \u2014 after env overrides, session state, org caps and model-support downgrades; the same value get_settings reports as applied.effort. null when no effort parameter will be sent (a model without effort, CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL=unset, or an internal numeric budget). Present on Remote Control bridge init frames (terminal- and Desktop/VS Code-hosted sessions); absent on hosts that do not publish it and on CLIs that predate the field. Re-emitted inits carry the current value \u2014 the newest frame wins."),per_turn_effort_active:M().optional().describe("@internal Whether per-turn effort is active for this frame's `model`. true: an effort change keeps the prompt cache, because the effort is sent inside the conversation, at the turn where it changes. false: the effort is sent only as the top-level parameter, so a change rewrites the cached prefix. Mid-session the CLI reports only a change to false (system/per_turn_effort_changed), and true comes only from an init. After a fresh process, or after the conversation's refusals are cleared (for example by /clear or a compaction), a true is provisional: a refusal on any later request sends system/per_turn_effort_changed before the retry, so a host keeps listening for it all session. Absent means unknown. Sent by the stream-json (Agent SDK) host."),view_mode:F(["focus","default"]).optional().describe("Whether the session's transcript is in focus view ('focus': the model is told the user sees only its final message per turn, so clients may collapse each turn to the prompt and the final response; 'default' otherwise). Toggled by /focus, including over Remote Control. Present on Remote Control bridge init frames and on the per-turn init of headless stream-json runs (`claude rc` workers, -p, the SDK's subprocess transport); absent on hosts that do not publish it (the in-process engine) and on CLIs that predate the field. Re-emitted inits carry the current value \u2014 the newest frame wins."),capabilities:C(o()).optional().describe("Protocol capabilities this CLI supports, so SDK consumers can feature-detect instead of version-sniffing. Open set \u2014 ignore unknown values; check each capability for exactly the behavior you use. 'interrupt_receipt_v1' = the interrupt control_response success payload carries still_queued (uuids of async user messages that survive the interrupt). 'interrupt_cancel_queued_v1' = the interrupt control_request honors cancel_queued:true (queued and pending-dispatch commands are cancelled alongside the abort, listed on the response's cancelled field; still_queued is then empty \u2014 including any uuid that was mid-fold at the interrupt instant, since this request also aborts and the fold never delivers it \u2014 except that a client driving a hosted session lists there what it can no longer recall: a send already in flight to that session, or the first prompt the session was created with). 'queued_notifications' = the CLI accepts inbound queued_notification stream messages and drains them via ReadNotifications (the cloud session backend reads this from the persisted init event to decide whether it may send them). Absent on older CLIs."),analytics_disabled:M().optional().describe("@internal True when the CLI has analytics/telemetry disabled (privacy level, DO_NOT_TRACK, or 3P provider). IDE clients use this to hide per-message thumbs feedback UI since the rating event would be a no-op."),product_feedback_disabled:M().optional().describe("@internal True when the org's allow_product_feedback policy is false (ZDR/HIPAA). IDE clients use this to hide feedback surfaces (thumbs, session survey) whose events the CLI would drop at the proxy boundary anyway."),memory_paths:u({auto:o().optional(),team:o().optional()}).optional().describe("@internal Absolute directory paths for the auto-memory and team-memory stores. Lets SDK renderers classify Read/Write/Edit tool calls on these paths as memory operations without re-implementing CLI path detection."),scratchpad_path:o().optional().describe("@internal Absolute path of this session's scratchpad directory, for clients that read files from the session. Omitted when the scratchpad is disabled, and from the redacted init sent over the Remote Control bridge and by the hosted worker."),original_cwd:o().optional().describe("@internal Absolute path of the folder the session's file permission checks are anchored at, for clients that read files from the session. Unlike cwd it does not follow the shell: it moves only on /cd, on entering or leaving a worktree, and on a session restore. Omitted on the lanes that redact system/init's local paths: the init sent over the Remote Control bridge and by the claude-code-server tenant worker. Omitted too in a session that has no directory, where cwd is the empty string, and, on every lane, for an organization that is under HIPAA or whose policy the session fetches but does not hold."),additional_directories:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Absolute paths of the folders added to the session (--add-dir, the SDK additionalDirectories option, settings, a grant or a host's request made mid-session), in no particular order; an empty array when there are none. Re-read for every init message. Left out: a folder that only a settings file the repository delivers adds (.claude/settings.json, and .claude/settings.local.json unless git is known not to track it), so that a branch cannot widen what a client fetches; and an entry not stored as an absolute path. Entries are not all symlink-resolved, and one can be the symlinked spelling of the start folder. It grants nothing: the CLI's own check decides every read. Absent means not told, not none: omitted on the lanes that redact system/init's local paths and for an organization that is under HIPAA or whose policy the session fetches but does not hold, like original_cwd, and by a host that has not wired it. A session that has no directory still sends it."),worker_epoch:E().int().min(1).optional().describe("@internal This cloud worker's life (CLAUDE_CODE_WORKER_EPOCH): a new number each time the session's worker is started. A claude --cloud client that latched an acknowledged epoch (remote_tools_announce) re-registers only when this differs. Emitted on workers where the account's remote-tools gate (tengu_violin_wood) is on, and by every claude-code-server worker whatever that gate reads; absent outside cloud workers and on CLIs that predate the field."),powershell_path:o().nullable().optional().describe("@internal Windows only: the absolute path of the PowerShell binary this session runs PowerShell commands with (PowerShell 7 when installed, including Microsoft Store installs, else Windows PowerShell 5.1), or null when none was found. Lets a desktop host open its own terminal with the same shell instead of re-implementing the detection. Present on stream-json (Agent SDK) init frames; absent on other platforms, wherever the init ambient is redacted (the Remote Control bridge), and on CLIs that predate the field."),cloud_session:My().optional().describe("@internal Only on init frames written by the headless stream-json client of a cloud-hosted session (agent loop in the cloud container; this machine may be bound to it as a device): a snapshot per frame, absent in every other mode; no user, assistant or result frame precedes the first such init (informational system frames may; so may a worker's result that no init preceded, after all that was held ahead of it; on a re-attach so may frames of a turn already in progress, other clients' prompts, and the echo of this host's own prompt when the session already had it or it joins a turn in progress). `id` is the cloud session id, always in its `session_\u2026` spelling (the producer normalizes the service's `cse_\u2026` spelling of the same session); hosts store it and pass it back verbatim to re-attach. The frame-level `session_id` is instead the cloud worker's id, a uuidv5 of the session id in its `cse_` form: the same for the life of the cloud session across worker restarts and re-attaches until a /clear in the session, and passed through by the client unchanged. When `device.status` is `unbound`, `reason` is one of the client's unbound-reason keys and `message` its phrase: one line, at most 200 characters, no ANSI or trailing newline, worded to follow `Laptop tools unavailable: `. `directory_sync`: see docs/cloud-session-block.md. `not_applied`, when present, lists what the host passed that this session does not apply \u2014 `name` is a flag as `--flag`, a key inside --settings / --managed-settings as `settings.<key>` / `managed-settings.<key>`, or a field of the opening initialize as `initialize.<field>` (at most 40, lost first, each one line of about 64 characters at most); `kind` says what it costs: `lost` (the session does without something this invocation asked for \u2014 the informational warning names these), `kept` (an attach: the existing session keeps what it was created with) or `preference` (a display or host preference nobody would miss \u2014 the informational notice names these two), so a host may show them itself once or never; absent when nothing was left unapplied. `host`, when present, is what this machine announces as to the session: `handle` is the name the model addresses it by (and a client shows), `working_dir` the absolute directory served calls run in (that machine's separators), `platform` its platform word, `cli_version` its Claude Code version; each one line of at most 200 characters; present once this machine has built an announce for the session (bound, with something or an explicit nothing to serve), absent otherwise and on clients that predate it. `serving`, when present, says whether this machine serves its tools (Bash, Read, Write, Edit) to the session over the session's channel: `state` is `pending` while bound and about to announce, `on` once the session holds this machine's tools, `off` when it serves nothing, `revoked` when it stopped mid-session; `reason` (present when `state` is not `on` and the cause is known) is one of the client's serving-off keys \u2014 `not_bound` (see `device.reason`), `external_build`, `windows`, `muted` (Anthropic's emergency switch turned serving off; it comes back when the switch does), `switch_off`, `launched_from_home`, `channel_off`, `egress`, `policy_org` (the organization does not allow remote sessions), `flag_off`, `flag_stale`, `announce_refused`, `announce_unverified`, `superseded` \u2014 an open set: render an unrecognized value as a generic not-serving state; `policy` is the serving policy in effect (`parity`, or the stricter `interim` fallback); `channel` is `session`, or `bridge_only` when the session channel is switched off on this machine and served calls (if any) ride only the legacy device bridge, whose state this block does not describe. Absent on clients that predate it: feature-detect the key. `connection`, when present, is this client's stream to the session: `connecting` before it first connects, `live` while it is up, `reconnecting` while it is lost and being re-dialled (this machine is offline to the session meanwhile). `calls`, when present (once this machine has served a call this attach), is what it serves right now and how its last calls ended, by id and tool name only \u2014 never a call's input or output: `live` lists, oldest first, each call admitted here and not yet ended, `state` `running` while this machine executes it or `asked` while it waits on the session's approval (`ask_id` names the question; `since` is when it entered that state and `lapses_at` about when the session gives an unanswered question up, both epoch milliseconds on this machine's clock \u2014 the session's own timer decides); `recent` lists, newest last, at most 20 calls that ended here, `ended` being `completed`, `denied` (the session said no), `withdrawn` (the session took the question back unanswered \u2014 its patience ran out or the turn moved on), `refused` or `failed`, with `code` the result's disposition, refusal or failure word when it has one, and `at` when. `notices`, when present, is what this machine's serving has told the person during this attach that a host should show, at most 8: `key` is the notice's stable name (an open set \u2014 `device-mcp-consent`: this machine has MCP servers a cloud session could use and none are offered until the person allows it; `device-mcp-reconsent`: a server it offers changed the tools it lists and is withheld until re-approved, or this machine's record of what its servers list could not be read and none are offered \u2014 `text` says which; `device-mcp-policy`: a deny list in this machine's Claude Code settings lists remote-devices, so none of its MCP servers are offered; `device-mcp-dropped`; `served-hooks-not-run`; `served-hook-errors`; `served-bash-launched-from-home`; `served-tools-muted` / `served-tools-unmuted`; `announce_unverified`) and `text` its one line (at most 200 characters, no ANSI or newline) \u2014 this machine's own words with names from its own configuration, to be shown as plain text; written only by a serve-only helper (`--attach-serve`) and absent when nothing was said. `calls`, `connection` and `notices` are pushed between inits on `cloud_session_delta` frames. `client_version` rises only on a breaking change to this block \u2014 warn and continue on a higher value, never refuse; `not_applied[].kind`, `serving.state`, `connection`, `calls[].state` / `ended` and reason keys may gain values without it changing, so treat unrecognized values neutrally (an unrecognized `serving.state` as not serving \u2014 this schema itself parses an unrecognized `serving.state` as `off`, `policy` as `interim`, `channel` as `bridge_only`)."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Session metadata the CLI emits at the start of each turn, normally ahead of every other message of that turn: session_id, model, working directory, tools, MCP servers, slash commands, permission mode, and the capabilities list for feature detection.")),mF=f(()=>u({type:T("stream_event"),event:Z0(),parent_tool_use_id:o().nullable(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),ttft_ms:E().int().optional(),thinking_display:o().optional().describe("@internal The thinking.display Claude Code put in the API request body for the response this event belongs to. If anything rewrites the request on its way to the API, this value still states what Claude Code sent, not what the API received. Two responses of one turn can differ. Set on `message_start`, on a thinking block's `content_block_start` and on `thinking_delta` events when the body had a display; absent on every other event, on Remote Control (interactive terminal) sessions and from older producers. An event that the API did not send as it stands can lack the key. The key states which display the text was requested with, and nothing about how to show it. An absent key states nothing: neither which display was requested, nor that none was."),api_message_id:o().optional().describe("@internal The API's id (`msg_\u2026`) of the response this event belongs to: `event.message.id` on its `message_start`, `message.id` on its assistant messages. Not a message `uuid`. Set on every event from a response's `message_start` to its `message_stop`, so that a consumer that missed the `message_start` can still tell responses apart. Two responses can share an id, as when a `message_start` is replayed; the key does not tell those apart. Absent on pings, when the `message_start` had no id or an empty one, on Remote Control (interactive terminal) sessions and from older producers. The events that Claude Code produces itself to close a response cut short carry it too. Events that a plugin produced or rewrote, and those that Claude Code builds in their place or to close them, can lack it. An absent key states nothing about which response the event belongs to."),abandoned_blocks:u({api_message_id:o().describe("The API's id (`msg_\u2026`) of the response that was cut short: `event.message.id` on its `message_start`, `message.id` on its assistant messages. Not a message `uuid`. Should two responses share an id, the one meant is the one this `message_stop` closes."),from_block_index:E().int().nonnegative().describe("Blocks of the response at this index and above have no assistant message and never will. It is an `event.index` as on the response's `content_block_*` events, not a count of assistant messages. 0 means the whole response. Can be higher than every index the consumer has seen, and then there is nothing to remove.")}).optional().describe("@internal Set on a `message_stop` that Claude Code produced itself, after it stopped reading the response that `message_stop` closes because the API's stream failed, stalled or ended early. That response's content blocks at index `from_block_index` and above have no assistant message and will never get one, so a consumer that drew or stored them from stream events, and holds no assistant message for them, can remove them, even a block that has had its `content_block_stop`. Nothing is stated about lower blocks: some may have no assistant message, and what follows may repeat or replace them. What follows can be, among others, a retry (a new `message_start`), the same request without streaming (assistant messages under another id, with no `message_start` before them), an assistant message that reports the error, or nothing. A plugin can add content blocks of its own to a response, and those do get assistant messages under the same id, emitted before this event. Absent on every other `message_stop` (the API's own, one produced for a response that had arrived whole, one where Claude Code cannot be sure), on Remote Control (interactive terminal) sessions and from older producers. An absent key states nothing: a block with no assistant message yet may still get one."),text_runs_joined:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Set on the `message_start` of a response when CLAUDE_CODE_WEBSEARCH_CITATIONS is on in a non-interactive session. Each run of adjacent text blocks of that response gets one assistant message with one text block, which holds the joined text. Each entry of its `citations`, except a `web_search_result_location`, has `span_start` and `span_end`: the place of the cited block in the joined text, in Unicode code points, end exclusive. The `content_block_*` events still show each block of the run by its own index. A response that came without streaming has no `message_start`, and it is joined too."),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Client uuid of the user message this turn is answering (submitMessage options.uuid), stamped on a non-ping stream event each time that send changes: the turn's FIRST non-ping stream event (normally the frame that triggers the turn's initial ack), and, for a turn started by a synthetic (meta) prompt, the first non-ping stream event after each queued user message folded in mid-turn takes the echo over (see SDKAssistantMessage.user_message_uuid for the rule) \u2014 so a consumer can bind the reply stream to the send it answers without waiting for the result. A turn started by a typed prompt stamps only its first non-ping stream event; independently, its first complete assistant message is stamped as well (see SDKAssistantMessage.user_message_uuid), so the same uuid may appear on both. Absent on every other stream event of the turn, on turns that neither had a client uuid nor folded a user message in, and from older producers."),user_message_uuids:C(o()).optional().describe("Client uuids of every user message whose prompt this turn has consumed so far, in consumption order \u2014 all members of a prompt batch the host merged into this one turn (several messages sent close together run as one turn whose user_message_uuid is the LAST member's), then any user message folded into the turn before this frame \u2014 so a consumer that sent any of them can bind this reply to its own send by finding its uuid anywhere in the list. Always contains user_message_uuid; at most 64 entries. Present exactly when user_message_uuid is, on the same frames; absent from older producers (fall back to user_message_uuid)."),resume_reason:o().optional().describe("Why this frame's turn continues one that a worker restart interrupted. Such a turn is the automatic re-run of the interrupted turn (CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN), or one that goes on from the answer to the permission prompt the new worker took over, or from that turn's tool calls still in flight. The value is the host's CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_REASON when it set one (host_draining, checkpoint_restore, container_recreated, \u2026), else 'interrupted_turn'. Stamped on the same reply frames as user_message_uuid (which on such a turn names the interrupted turn's own last user prompt), so a consumer can tell its first reply from the interrupted attempt's. Absent on every other turn (also one that goes on from the answer to any other permission prompt), on thinking_tokens frames, and from older producers.")}).describe("An incremental streaming event for the assistant message being generated, emitted only when partial messages are requested (--include-partial-messages). The complete assistant message still follows as its own message.")),_F=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("compact_boundary"),compact_metadata:u({trigger:F(["manual","auto"]),pre_tokens:E().int(),post_tokens:E().int().optional(),cumulative_dropped_tokens:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Running total of context tokens compaction has removed so far, across this and every earlier compaction. "+"Each contribution is approximately pre_tokens \u2212 post_tokens."),duration_ms:E().int().optional(),user_context:o().optional().describe('@internal User-provided focus text for manual "summarize from here".'),messages_summarized:E().int().optional().describe("@internal Count of messages the compaction summarized."),precomputed:M().optional().describe("@internal The summary was generated in the background at the autocompact threshold and swapped in when prompt-too-long fired; duration_ms measures user-wait from that point."),pre_compact_discovered_tools:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Deferred-tool names discovered before this compaction. extractDiscoveredToolNames reads this back on the next turn so the tool-schema filter keeps including them after the tool_reference-carrying messages were summarized away."),pre_compact_artifact_read_versions:C(u({slug:o(),ver:o()})).optional().describe("@internal The artifact versions the conversation held before this compaction (slug is the artifact id, ver the version read or published), most recently changed first; empty when it held none. A resumed session replays these so it can update those artifacts without reading them again."),preserved_segment:u({head_uuid:p(),anchor_uuid:p(),tail_uuid:p()}).optional().describe("Relink info for messagesToKeep. Loaders splice the preserved segment at anchor_uuid (summary for suffix-preserving, boundary for prefix-preserving partial compact) so resume includes preserved content. Unset when compaction summarizes everything (no messagesToKeep)."),preserved_messages:u({anchor_uuid:p(),uuids:C(p()),all_uuids:C(p()).optional().describe("@internal Unfiltered messagesToKeep UUIDs. uuids is the on-disk subset (messages recordTranscript writes); all_uuids is the in-memory superset including non-loggable messages an in-process surface still holds for the next turn's API input. Absent from older producers.")}).optional().describe("Ordered messagesToKeep UUIDs. Supersedes preserved_segment \u2014 "+"readers look up each UUID directly and relink uuids[i] to uuids[i-1] (uuids[0] to anchor_uuid) instead of walking the parentUuid chain. Unset when compaction summarizes everything.")}),logical_parent_uuid:p().nullable().optional().describe("@internal uuid of the last pre-compact message \u2014 the backpointer "+"forkSession follows across the compaction break. Distinct from the session-file chain parent (which is the post-compact summary). Absent from older producers."),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),historical:M().optional().describe("@internal Replayed history rather than a live message (stamped by the Remote Control bridge when it flushes history to the session server).")})),gF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("status"),status:eF(),permissionMode:de().optional(),compact_result:F(["success","failed"]).optional(),compact_error:o().optional(),compact_output_chars:E().int().optional().describe("@internal On the 'compacting' statuses of an automatic compaction (absent on a typed /compact): characters of the summary streamed so far (text and tool-input deltas, not thinking). Returns to 0 at each new attempt at the summary (a request retried inside an attempt adds to what the dropped try streamed), and stays 0 while waiting for a summary written in the background. A rising count shows that the summary is being written; a still one proves nothing, and it is not a share of the final length, which is not known in advance."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),Eo=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("post_turn_summary"),summarizes_uuid:o(),status_category:o(),status_detail:o(),needs_action:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Background post-turn summary emitted after each assistant turn. summarizes_uuid points to the assistant message this summarizes.")),So=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_summary"),detail:o().nullable(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Mid-turn progress line from the debounced classifier. Mirrors external_metadata.task_summary so non-CCR consumers (desktop LocalSessionManager) see the same live phrase. detail is null on the idle clear.")),To=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("session_metadata"),metadata:u({artifacts:C(u({url:o(),title:o().optional(),favicon:o().optional(),kind:T("frame"),updated_at:o()})).nullable().optional()}),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The same external_metadata patch a cloud worker sends the session server, for the keys a local host needs: today `artifacts` (the session's published-artifact list, whole list on each change, null once empty). Lets an SDK host keep the per-session summary a cloud session gets, without reading transcript rows.")),fF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("informational"),content:o(),level:F(["info","notice","suggestion","warning"]).describe("Render level. 'info' shows only in transcript mode; 'notice' renders in inactive gray; 'suggestion' and 'warning' are more prominent."),tool_use_id:o().optional().describe("Dedupes progress messages for the same tool use."),prevent_continuation:M().optional().describe("When true, execution stops after this message (e.g. a Stop hook denied continuation)."),tag:o().optional().describe("Opaque feature tag on a line a host may treat specially; absent on ordinary lines. Hosts ignore values they do not know."),prompt_submit_outcome:u({kind:F(YA).optional().catch(void 0),no_verdict_kind:F($A).optional().catch(void 0).describe("Sent with kind 'no_verdict', and says why: 'remote_run_failed' (the hook runs on another machine, and the run failed or gave no usable answer), 'remote_run_timed_out' (the wait for that run timed out; the hook may never have been sent), 'interrupted' (the turn was aborted during that wait), 'remote_runner_refused' (Claude Code does not send this hook to that machine; a retry does not help), 'unsupported_in_cloud_session' (a cloud session cannot run this type of hook), 'output_distrusted' (Claude Code could not prove that it read all of the hook's output), 'callback_timed_out' (a callback hook did not answer in time), 'hook_failed' and 'hook_timed_out' (for this hook a failure blocks), 'hooks_never_ran' (an internal fault: the hooks did not run over the submitted text), 'personal_config_refused' (the session restricts personal config, and part of the person's own settings did not load or is off), 'rewrite_unplaceable' (a plugin's code module changed the prompt's text, and Claude Code has no place to put the change)."),prompt_kept:M(),reason:o().optional()}).optional().catch(void 0).describe("@internal Present on the line that ends a submitted prompt before a model call. It says how the prompt ended, so a host can write its own message or count endings and does not match `content`. kind: 'blocked_by_hook' (a hook answered with a block), 'stopped_by_hook' (a hook answered continue:false), 'dropped_by_hook' (a plugin's code module dropped the prompt), 'not_passed_on' (a plugin's code module answered and did not pass the prompt on), 'no_verdict' (no hook gave a verdict, and Claude Code ended the prompt itself). prompt_kept: true when the prompt stays in the conversation, where the model reads it on the next turn. Do not infer it from kind. reason: the hook's text as Claude Code got it, with nothing added here; a model writes it for a prompt or agent hook. It is never sent with no_verdict. It is absent when Claude Code got no text, and for a plugin's code module that blocks on the UserPromptSubmit hook event. ANSI codes are removed, control and invisible characters are replaced, line feeds stay, and long text is cut. It is untrusted and can quote the prompt, so render it as plain text. A cloud session can run the hook on another machine: its tool container, where any program can write reason, or the person's own machine. reason is then what that machine sent, for a block and for a stop. The person's own machine puts a label before the stderr of an exit 2, or before 'No stderr output' when there is none. Either machine makes the block itself when it could not read all of the hook's output, and the person's own machine can do so when its timeout fires. Each reads 'blocked_by_hook', with that machine's sentence as reason. Claude Code's own block for an SDK host that reconnected before its hook answered reads the same way, with Claude Code's sentence as reason. New values can appear: treat an unknown value as absent, and ignore reason beside an unknown kind. A slash command that runs as a fork does not send this key when a hook ends it. A follow-up that a hook blocks while a turn runs carries this key too, so read it as the end of a turn only when the next result has num_turns 0."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Generic text banner emitted by the loop \u2014 non-error status lines, hook feedback (e.g. a UserPromptSubmit hook's block reason), slash-command output. Hosts render `content` as plaintext at the given level.")),QJ=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("permission_retry"),content:o(),commands:C(o()).describe("Display names of the commands that were allowed."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when tool execution retries after a permission-mode change allowed previously-denied commands. REPL renders a 'retrying with <commands>' banner. From internal SystemMessage 'permission_retry'.")),JJ=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("stop_hook_summary"),hook_count:E().int(),hook_infos:C(u({command:o(),prompt_text:o().optional(),duration_ms:E().int().optional()})),hook_errors:C(o()),hook_additional_context:C(o()).optional().describe("Non-error feedback from hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext \u2014 kept separate from hook_errors so the sanctioned feedback channel is not labeled an error. Absent in sessions persisted before this field existed."),prevented_continuation:M(),stop_reason:o().optional(),has_output:M(),level:F(["info","notice","suggestion","warning"]),tool_use_id:o().optional(),hook_label:o().optional(),total_duration_ms:E().int().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Summary of Stop/SubagentStop hook execution at turn end \u2014 which hooks ran, their output, and whether any prevented continuation. From internal SystemMessage 'stop_hook_summary'.")),ZJ=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("memory_saved"),written_paths:C(o()),team_count:E().int().optional(),verb:o().optional().describe('Renders as "<verb> N memories". Defaults to "Saved".'),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Confirmation that the memory subsystem wrote to the listed paths. REPL renders a '<verb> N memories' banner. From internal SystemMessage 'memory_saved'.")),e8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("agents_killed"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when background agents are terminated (e.g. on interrupt). REPL renders an 'agents killed' banner. From internal SystemMessage 'agents_killed'.")),bF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("instruction_size_warning"),total_chars:E().int().describe("Combined length, in characters, of the instruction files in the session prompt: CLAUDE.md files, rules files and the files they import. Always over total_limit_chars."),total_limit_chars:E().int().describe("The recommended limit for total_chars on the turn's model, in characters."),file_count:E().int().describe("Number of files counted in total_chars. When it is 1, word the warning for a single file rather than a total."),largest_chars:E().int().optional().describe("Length of the largest file, in characters. Present only when the warning should mention it: there are several files and this one by itself is over total_limit_chars."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The session's instruction files add up to more than the recommended limit. Numbers only: the host words the warning in the person's language. Sent to Claude Desktop's Code tab alone, at most once per process, on a conversation's first turn after system/init; never stored in the transcript. Safe to ignore: a host that does not recognize this subtype shows nothing.")),t8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("away_summary"),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Summary of what happened while the user was away (background tasks completed, notifications accumulated). From internal SystemMessage 'away_summary'.")),s8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("thinking"),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Rendered thinking content (the text itself, not the running token estimate \u2014 that is SDKThinkingTokensMessage). From internal SystemMessage 'thinking'.")),wo=f(()=>u({type:T("transcript_mirror"),filePath:o(),entries:C(oe())}).describe("@internal Emitted after each successful local transcript write. The parent peels these off the stdout stream and batches them to the SessionStore adapter. Not exposed to public SDK consumers.")),EF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("mirror_error"),error:o(),key:u({projectKey:o(),sessionId:o(),subpath:o().optional()}),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when SessionStore.append() rejects or times out for a transcript-mirror batch after bounded retry (3 attempts with short backoff; timeouts are not retried). The batch is then dropped; this surfaces the failure so consumers are not silent on data loss.")),SF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("api_retry"),attempt:E().int(),max_retries:E().int(),retry_delay_ms:E().int(),error_status:E().int().nullable(),error:go(),no_response:u({waited_ms:E().int(),retry_wait_ms:E().int()}).optional().describe("Present only when the API sent no response headers within the first-byte window (CLAUDE_STREAM_FIRST_BYTE_TIMEOUT_MS): waited_ms is how long the failed attempt waited for headers, retry_wait_ms how long the retry will wait for them. For this cause max_retries is its own cap (normally one retry), not the session budget."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when an API request fails with a retryable error and will be retried after a delay. error_status is null for connection errors (e.g. timeouts) that had no HTTP response.")),TF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("control_request_progress"),request_id:o().describe("request_id of the in-flight control_request this progress belongs to."),status:F(["started","api_retry"]),attempt:E().int().optional(),max_retries:E().int().optional(),retry_delay_ms:E().int().optional(),error_status:E().int().nullable().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Progress for a long-running client-originated control_request (currently only side_question), correlated by request_id. status 'started' means the worker accepted the request and launched the work; 'api_retry' carries the same retry counters as SDKAPIRetryMessage and is present only for that status.")),wF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("model_refusal_fallback"),trigger:T("refusal"),direction:F(["retry","revert","sticky"]),scope:F(["session","local"]).optional().describe("'session': the main thread fell back and the session model is swapped. 'local': a subagent / side-question (/btw) / background fork fell back \u2014 only that response came from the fallback model and the session model is unchanged. Absent from older CLIs (treat as 'session')."),original_model:o(),fallback_model:o(),request_id:o().nullable(),api_refusal_category:o().nullable().optional().describe("The refusal category ('cyber', 'bio', \u2026): stop_details.category from the refused API response (client lane), or the fallback block's server-gated trigger.category (server lane). Open string \u2014 new categories ship on the wire ahead of schema updates. null when neither source carried a category (normal, not an error). Absent when emitted by an older CLI."),saw_cyber_refusal:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present when ANY hop of this banner's multi-hop episode was a cyber refusal \u2014 not only the origin hop that api_refusal_category describes (the banner keeps first-hop category semantics for display). Re-arm evidence for the CLI's cyber-exclusion header on session restore; never rendered, not part of the public SDK contract. Absent on cyber-free episodes and when emitted by an older CLI."),api_refusal_explanation:o().nullable().optional().describe("stop_details.explanation from the refused API response (client lane only \u2014 the server-lane trigger carries no explanation). Unstable human prose \u2014 display only, never parse. null/absent when the response carried none, and always null on server-lane banners."),retracted_message_uuids:C(o()).optional().describe("Wire uuids of the messages this fallback retracted \u2014 the refused partial as the consumer received it (one uuid per normalized SDK message; multi-block messages carry per-block derived uuids) plus any tombstoned tool_results. Emitted AFTER the retraction, so this is a resolution-time eviction signal: remove these messages from transcript state on receipt. Eviction is idempotent \u2014 unknown or already-removed uuids are a no-op. Absent when emitted by an older CLI."),refused_user_message_uuid:o().nullable().optional().describe("UUID of the user message the refused request was for \u2014 the rewind target and composer prefill for edit-and-retry. This is the message's own uuid as delivered on the replay ack (not a per-block normalized uuid). null when the refused turn was not human-authored (e.g. a background task notification or auto-continuation \u2014 nothing to edit-and-retry) or otherwise cannot be identified; absent from older CLIs."),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('Emitted when the primary model ends the stream with stop_reason "refusal" and the turn is retried once on a fallback model (direction: "retry"). When `scope` is "session" (or absent \u2014 older CLIs), the swap is made persistent for the session; when `scope` is "local", only that subagent/side-question response came from the fallback model and the session model is unchanged. "revert" and "sticky" are retained in the enum for SDK-consumer compat and are no longer emitted.')),AF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("model_refusal_no_fallback"),original_model:o(),request_id:o().nullable(),api_refusal_category:o().nullable().optional(),api_refusal_explanation:o().nullable().optional(),refused_user_message_uuid:o().nullable().optional(),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('Emitted when the model ends the stream with stop_reason "refusal" and no retry runs: no fallback model is configured, or per-category routing declined the retry (the mapped fallback target is unresolvable, or CLAUDE_CODE_REFUSAL_FALLBACK_CATCH_ALL is explicitly disabled and the refusal category has no fallback map entry). The structured counterpart to detecting stop_reason "refusal" on the assistant error frame. Not emitted when the retry ran or the user declined the retry dialog (model_refusal_fallback covers the retry case). Absent from older CLIs.')),n8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("model_fallback"),trigger:F(["model_not_found","permission_denied","overloaded","server_error","last_resort","model_blocked"]),original_model:o(),fallback_model:o(),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('@internal Emitted when the current turn is switched to the configured fallback model because the primary model failed (trigger "model_not_found": model retired/unknown; "permission_denied": org lacks access; "overloaded": repeated 529s; "server_error": retryable 5xx pivot; "last_resort": non-retryable error on the primary; "model_blocked": primary disabled by the per-model kill switch). Turn-scoped \u2014 the primary is re-tried on the next user turn. Not yet in the public SDKMessage union.')),r8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("model_consent_fallback"),choice:F(["consent","switch_default","cancelled"]).describe("The consent-prompt answer (or no-dialog collapse) that produced the swap. 'consent' appears here only when the gate could not honor it (e.g. usage credits did not end up provisioned \u2014 the loop never enables billing from a bare wire reply)."),original_model:o(),original_model_name:o().optional().describe(`The name the CLI's usage-credit copy uses for original_model ("Fable 5"), for a host that renders its own notice; "Fable" when absent.`),fallback_model:o(),persisted_as_default:M().describe("True when the decline also rewrote the saved default model (explicit switch_default with the consent-gated model as the saved default)."),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a pre-send model consent gate swaps the session off the requested model (consent declined, dismissed, or given without the required entitlement ending up provisioned). Currently emitted by the Fable 5 usage-credit gate (`fable_overage_consent_prompt`). Session-scoped \u2014 the swap persists for the session, and additionally as the saved default when persisted_as_default is true. Absence of this message after the consent dialog resolves means the session stayed on the requested model. Not yet in the public SDKMessage union.")),o8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("file_snapshot"),content:o(),snapshot_files:C(u({key:o().describe("Identifier for the file type (e.g. 'plan', 'todo')."),path:o().describe("Original file path (for debugging)."),content:o()})),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Snapshot of session files (plan, todo) captured for rewind. From internal SystemMessage 'file_snapshot'.")),a8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("scheduled_task_fire"),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a scheduled task (cron) fires. content is the render text. From internal SystemMessage 'scheduled_task_fire'.")),yF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("peer_message_hold"),state:F(["held","released","dropped"]).describe("'held' when the receive-side policy parks the message instead of queueing it (once per message per hold cause \u2014 a re-announcement under a different cause emits again); then at most one of 'released' (the policy now accepts it: it enters the queue \u2014 its command_lifecycle 'queued' and, when it drains, its user replay echo follow under message_uuid) or 'dropped' (it will never reach the model in this session; see outcome)."),message_uuid:o().optional().describe("The parked command's uuid \u2014 the value its later command_lifecycle frames and user replay echo carry. For lane 'bridge'/'stdin' it is the id the host (or its bridge) supplied on the inbound message; for lane 'socket' it was chosen by the SENDING session, so a host must correlate it only within peer_message_hold / peer replay frames, never against its own prompts' lifecycle."),lane:F(["bridge","stdin","socket"]).describe("Which ingress delivered it: the Remote Control bridge, the host's own stdin stream, or the local cross-session socket."),from:o().describe("The sender's address as the envelope will show it (the cross-session-message `from`) \u2014 an address-shaped token with control and invisible code points scrubbed; empty when the sender supplied none or an unshaped one. Sender-asserted on the socket lane: a label, not an identity proof."),from_name:o().optional().describe("The sender's display name when it supplied one, normalized and scrubbed the same way. A claim, like `from`."),cause:F(["explicit-setting","managed-setting","repo-setting","invalid-setting","bypass-default","mode-unknown","mode-mismatch","no-mode-asserted"]).optional().describe("state 'held' only: why the policy parked it. 'mode-mismatch' / 'no-mode-asserted' are the permission-mode parity holds (the sender runs in a different permission class, or asserted none while this session bypasses permissions); the *-setting causes are a standing crossSessionInbound:'hold'."),outcome:F(["expired","refused","dropped","discarded"]).optional().describe("state 'dropped' only: 'expired' \u2014 no one approved it before the approval deadline (a headless host has no approval surface, so every parity hold ends this way unless the mode changes first); 'refused' \u2014 the policy turned to refuse while it waited; 'dropped' \u2014 released or approved but the ingress guard (rate limit, duplicate, queue cap) discarded it; 'discarded' \u2014 the session ended with it still parked."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A cross-session (peer) message this session's receive-side policy HELD rather than queued, and how that hold resolved. Lets a host show the human that a message arrived but has not reached the model (and may never) instead of nothing at all; the sending session is told separately over its own transport where one exists. Informational only \u2014 there is no host-side approval through this frame. Emitted on the stdout stream in -p/SDK sessions.")),i8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("turn_duration"),duration_ms:E().int(),budget_tokens:E().int().optional().describe("Output tokens spent this turn toward the token budget."),budget_limit:E().int().optional().describe("Turn token-budget ceiling."),budget_nudges:E().int().optional().describe("Budget-nudge count this turn."),message_count:E().int().optional().describe("In-memory message count at turn end; used by resume-consistency telemetry to detect write\u2192load round-trip drift."),pending_background_agent_count:E().int().optional().describe("Background Agent-tool runs still in flight when the turn finished."),pending_workflow_count:E().int().optional().describe("Workflow-tool runs still in flight when the turn finished."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Per-turn wall-clock duration plus budget and pending-background-work counts. REPL renders the 'Done in Ns' / 'Waiting for N agents' line. From internal SystemMessage 'turn_duration'.")),l8=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("api_error"),error:u({message:o(),status:E().int().optional(),request_id:o().optional(),formatted:o().describe("Human-readable display string for the error."),connection:u({code:o(),message:o(),is_ssl_error:M()}).nullable().describe("errno/SSL code extracted from the cause chain; null when absent."),is_network_down:M(),rate_limits:u({resets_at:E().int().optional(),rate_limit_type:o().optional()}).nullable().describe("Quota-429 headers surfaced by the retry banner; null when not a quota 429.")}).describe("Plain-data snapshot of the APIError \u2014 the class instance cannot cross the wire."),retry_in_ms:E().int(),retry_attempt:E().int(),max_retries:E().int(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Retryable-API-error frame carrying the plain-data error snapshot and retry counters. REPL renders the retry banner from this. Wire twin is SDKAPIRetryMessage ('api_retry'). From internal SystemMessage 'api_error'.")),RF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("local_command_output"),content:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Output from a local slash command (e.g. /voice, /usage). Displayed as assistant-style text in the transcript."));var vF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("hook_started"),hook_id:o(),hook_name:o(),hook_event:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),kF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("hook_progress"),hook_id:o(),hook_name:o(),hook_event:o(),stdout:o(),stderr:o(),output:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),CF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("hook_response"),hook_id:o(),hook_name:o(),hook_event:o(),output:o(),stdout:o(),stderr:o(),exit_code:E().int().optional(),outcome:F(["success","error","cancelled"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),zF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("plugin_install"),status:F(["started","installed","failed","completed"]),name:o().optional(),error:o().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Headless plugin installation progress (CLAUDE_CODE_SYNC_PLUGIN_INSTALL). started/completed bracket the whole install; installed/failed carry a per-marketplace name.")),OF=f(()=>u({type:T("tool_progress"),tool_use_id:o(),tool_name:o(),parent_tool_use_id:o().nullable(),elapsed_time_seconds:E().int(),task_id:o().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),heartbeat:M().optional(),subagent_type:o().optional(),subagent_retry:u({agent_id:o(),attempt:E().int(),max_retries:E().int(),retry_delay_ms:E().int(),error_status:E().int().nullable(),error_category:o()}).optional()})),NF=f(()=>u({type:T("auth_status"),isAuthenticating:M(),output:C(o()),error:o().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),IF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("files_persisted"),files:C(u({filename:o(),file_id:o()})),failed:C(u({filename:o(),error:o()})),processed_at:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),Ao="True for tasks that are not activity (every skip_transcript task, plus every live-update watcher, requested or auto-started); hosts should exclude them from activity indicators.",Dy="@internal True for a run of a resumed subagent (local_agent) that the tool call which resumed it waits for, as SendMessage does when background tasks are disabled; its task_started, if one is sent, names that call in tool_use_id. The run has ended by the time that call returns or throws, even when the wait is aborted. Its report goes into that call's result, so expect no follow-up turn when it ends. A later resume that nothing waits for starts a new run without the field.",xy="task_id of the subagent (local_agent) task whose agent launched this subagent, shell, monitor or workflow task. Absent when the launcher is the main thread, has no task_id of its own (a teammate, an agent inside a workflow), or is no longer tracked. A resumed subagent keeps its original parent. The parent may be a foreground or already-ended task, so treat an unknown id as no parent.",Wt="Id of one run of the task: equal on every task_* event, background_tasks_changed entry and saved notification (its origin.runId) of that run. A resumed task keeps its task_id and gets a new one. The run ids of one task sort, by plain string comparison, in the order the runs opened; a frame that names a run sorting before the one a reader shows is late. Nothing else about the format is promised. Absent for a task this process did not register, and from CLIs that predate it.",PF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_notification"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Wt),tool_use_id:o().optional(),status:F(["completed","failed","stopped"]),reason:F(["worker_restart"]).optional().describe("Machine-readable cause, set only when the task did not end through an ordinary completion, failure, or stop. 'worker_restart': the worker process restarted and the resumed process found the task orphaned (always with status 'stopped')."),output_file:o(),summary:o(),usage:u({total_tokens:E().int(),tool_uses:E().int(),duration_ms:E().int()}).optional(),resource_links:C(Ty()).optional().describe("CLI-owned: for a backgrounded MCP task (task_type mcp_task) that completed, the `resource_link` content blocks of its final result \u2014 the files it returned by reference \u2014 collected from the raw result before the CLI renders it as the text the model reads. A backgrounded task's tool_result is the placeholder text and its real result arrives as this notification, so this is where a host learns which files that tool call produced; join to the originating call via tool_use_id. Same fields and caps as tool_use_result.resourceLinks (at most 50 links, 64 KiB serialized), absent when the result had none or the task is any other type. Never populated from the server's _meta."),handback:F(Kjr).optional().describe("@internal Present on a 'completed' notification of a subagent that reports through the SubagentHandback tool: how its report reached its caller, with the values and meaning of `handback` on AgentToolCompletedOutput. Left out when the subagent failed, was stopped, is waiting on agents it started, or is waiting on its own background work with nothing delivered yet; for such a subagent, `summary` is then not a report. When present, `summary` holds harness notes written for the model. Any notification can be followed by a later one for the same task_id when the agent is resumed or finishes its wait; the later one replaces what the earlier one showed."),handback_report:qjr().optional().describe("@internal The report a 'send' or 'flagged' hand-back delivered, the same shape as `handbackReport` on AgentToolCompletedOutput: when present, render it instead of `summary`, with its `warning`, if it has one, above its `text`. Absent for 'withheld'; if it is absent for another value too, show `summary`."),skip_transcript:M().optional(),ambient:M().optional().describe(Ao),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),MF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_started"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Wt),tool_use_id:o().optional(),description:o(),subagent_type:o().optional().describe("Subagent type for Task tool subagents."),is_backgrounded:M().optional().describe("Whether the task was registered in the background (true) or in the foreground with the spawning tool call blocking on it (false). A resumed subagent is always registered in the background. A later move to the background arrives as task_updated patch.is_backgrounded. Set for local_agent and local_bash tasks."),awaited:M().optional().describe(Dy),spawn_depth:E().int().optional().describe("Nesting depth of a spawned subagent (local_agent) task: 1 for a top-level spawn, N+1 when spawned from inside a depth-N agent. Not set on other tasks."),parent_task_id:o().optional().describe(xy),task_type:o().optional(),workflow_name:o().optional().describe("meta.name from the workflow script (e.g. 'spec'). Only set when task_type is 'local_workflow'."),prompt:o().optional(),skip_transcript:M().optional().describe("Ambient/housekeeping task. Consumers should hide this from the inline transcript; it may still appear in a tasks panel."),ambient:M().optional().describe(Ao),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),DF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_updated"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Wt),patch:u({status:F(["pending","running","completed","failed","killed","paused"]).optional(),description:o().optional(),end_time:E().int().optional(),total_paused_ms:E().int().optional(),error:o().optional(),is_backgrounded:M().optional()}).describe("Wire-safe subset of TaskState fields that changed. Excludes abortController, messages, result. Clients merge into their local task map."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),xF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("background_tasks_changed"),tasks:C(u({task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Wt),task_type:o(),subagent_type:o().optional().describe(`Agent type of a local_agent task, such as "general-purpose" or a custom agent's name; "main-session" for a backgrounded main session. Not set on other tasks.`),description:o(),parent_task_id:o().optional().describe(xy),ambient:M().optional().describe(Ao),awaited:M().optional().describe(Dy)})).describe("Every live background task after the change. REPLACE semantics: swap your set for this payload."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("The full set of live background tasks, emitted whenever membership changes (start, completion, kill, a foreground agent being backgrounded) or an entry's `ambient` flag or `parent_task_id` changes. A level signal, unlike the task_started/task_notification edge bookends: consumers that only need 'is background work running' should replace their set with each payload rather than pairing edges, so a missed bookend cannot wedge a stale running indicator. Ordering relative to the bookends for the same transition is unspecified (in practice a task that starts in the background joins the level before its task_started, and a finishing task leaves it after its task_updated and task_notification), so do not correlate it with the edge stream. The level is per-process: nothing is emitted at startup, so consumers must reset to the empty set whenever the session's CLI process (re)starts and let the next membership change repopulate it. A host that re-initializes an already-running process (a repeated `initialize` control request, e.g. after reconnecting) is sent a snapshot of the current set right behind the success response to that request, even when it is empty, so it need not wait for a change; CLIs that predate this send nothing there.")),LF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("feedback_draft_queued"),draft_id:o().describe("UUID of the on-disk draft file under ~/.claude/feedback/drafts/ \u2014 the identity for discard and submit."),draft_type:F(["bug","idea","missing_capability"]),title:o().describe("Sanitized one-line summary from the SendFeedback tool call."),details_preview:o().describe("First ~200 chars of the sanitized details, for the host UI's three-line card preview. The full draft stays on disk and is read when the user opens the review form."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted by the SendFeedback tool after it writes a local draft to ~/.claude/feedback/drafts/, so a connected host UI can surface a review card without polling the filesystem. Carries only the card's display fields; nothing is sent anywhere without the user's explicit action on the review form.")),UF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("session_state_changed"),state:F(["idle","running","requires_action"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Mirrors notifySessionStateChanged. 'idle' fires after heldBackResult flushes and the bg-agent do-while exits \u2014 authoritative turn-over signal.")),VF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("session_title_changed"),title:o().describe("The session's current name. Control, bidirectional and zero-width characters are replaced with single spaces, the ends are trimmed, and the name is at most 200 Unicode code points. When another session already has the name, a suffix can make it unique."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The session's name, for a host that shows it, such as an IDE editor tab. A headless (print mode) session sends it at startup when the session already has a name, and again after each change to the name (not an AI-generated title), including a rename the host itself sent with `rename_session`. A name that another process writes to the transcript arrives when this process next reads the end of its transcript: after about 32 KB of its own transcript writes, or at a compaction. A cleared name is not sent. The same name can arrive more than once, for example when it is set again, so a host should treat a repeated name as no change. The frame can arrive before the system/init message that first names its session_id, for example at startup or after /clear. A host that learns the session id from system/init can hold the name until then.")),jF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("worker_shutting_down"),reason:o().describe("Short snake_case reason set by the host CLI (not user input), e.g. 'host_exit', 'remote_control_disabled'."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted by the bridge on opt-in graceful worker teardown (only when the teardown caller supplied a reason), before the heartbeat stops, so remote clients can show why the worker went away instead of waiting for heartbeat timeout. Absence is NOT a dead-host signal: handoffs (/update, /teleport, respawn), auto-disable, mode transitions, and internal fatal-error paths emit nothing by design. A dead host (battery, OOM, kill -9) never reaches teardown and never sends this either. NOTE: this event lands in the durable per-session event stream \u2014 a session that is later resumed may carry historical instances mid-stream. Clients MUST treat it as a live-tail signal only (honored when no further activity follows), not a one-shot session-lifetime fact.")),qF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("turn_handoff_available"),v:T(1),tools:C(o()),worker_epoch:E(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),staged_files:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, when this worker starts a handed-off turn's calls only after every stage_file control request it received earlier has finished or failed, or a wait limit has passed; a worker that omits it may start them while files are still downloading. Exception: a request with filestore_path set (a synced file) is not waited for, so a call that names that file may start before it is synced."),no_query_first:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal True when user messages with shouldQuery false that reach this worker before a turn_handoff request are appended, and acknowledged, before the handed-off turn produces any output. Exception: a request that continues a turn whose leading lines an earlier request appended; that turn goes first. A worker that omits it may run the turn first."),shared_message_id:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal True when this worker accepts a turn_handoff request in which an assistant message repeats the message id of the assistant message before it, as the messages member of that request describes. A worker that omits it refuses such a request (invalid_handoff), so a client gives each message an id of its own there."),relay_marker:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, only when this worker uses the relay_marker member of a turn_handoff request; a client sends that member to no other worker."),home_files:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, only on a worker that takes a stage_file control request whose mount_path is /uploads/.home/<path> as a file for /home/claude/<path>: it writes the file there, answers ok with a noop (already_present, path_occupied, refused_name, home_files_off, unchecked_announcement, resent_announcement) and writes nothing, or answers an error. Any other worker treats that path as an ordinary upload."),file_names:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, only when this worker uses the file_names member of a turn_handoff request; a client sends that member to no other worker."),carried_writes:T(!0).optional().describe(`@internal Present, and true, only on a worker that writes the files of the Write calls a turn_handoff request brings already answered, where their paths are under /home/claude. Before it appends a turn that has calls to run, it writes each such call's content to its path, replacing nothing. If it cannot write them all, it appends and runs nothing: the request is refused with an error that starts "invalid_handoff: carried_writes_", or it is accepted and the turn ends in a result with num_turns 0. Any other worker writes no file for these calls.`),hydrates_carried_lines:T(!0).optional().describe(`@internal Present, and true, only on a worker that rebuilt the conversation from the session's stored transcript before it read any request, and would not have started without it. A client may send this worker again a turn_handoff request that an earlier worker accepted, if the request names no calls (tool_use_ids empty), is not stopped, and its messages hold no tool call. A request whose turn had not ended when an earlier worker with this key stopped is left unacknowledged, so the session service sends it to this worker by itself; a second request for the same turn is answered "duplicate" while the first waits or runs. The worker appends only the messages of the turn that its conversation lacks and, once the store has confirmed every message of the turn, sends the turn's last message back as an assistant event (the echo); a turn it already holds whole is echoed on the same condition. The request can still be accepted, or refused ("invalid_handoff:"), with no echo, and the turn may then not be stored. That happens, for example, when the conversation has moved on past the part of the turn it holds, when a rewind has removed the turn, when a compaction has summarised part of it (or all of it, if the compaction was this worker's own), when a Stop clears the queue first, or when the store does not confirm the messages. A request sent again waits again for messages not yet confirmed. When a delivery finds that the store refused a message of the turn, it sends no echo and removes the turn from the conversation, and the same request sent again to that worker gets no echo. If another row has followed the turn, the turn stays, also with no echo. Exception: after 512 later refused rows the worker forgets a refusal and reads the message as confirmed. Confirmed means that the store answered the upload with success. A turn that an earlier worker's compaction summarised whole is appended a second time. The key promises nothing for a turn that holds a tool call, or whose first message is stored with no answer but with another row behind it (a hook's output, say): the worker may already have continued such a turn itself when it started. A worker without the key may have started with none of the turn's messages.`)}).describe("@internal Emitted once by a cloud worker that accepts the turn_handoff control request, after it registers, carrying what it wrote to external_metadata.turn_handoff: the version, the tools whose calls it would accept, the worker life (worker_epoch) announcing it, whether handed-off calls wait for files still being staged (staged_files), whether user messages that make no model call are appended before a handed-off turn (no_query_first), whether two assistant messages of a turn may share a message id (shared_message_id), and relay_marker, home_files, file_names, carried_writes and hydrates_carried_lines when set. Lets a session client learn the capability from the event stream instead of reading worker state. Durable in the stream: a reader keeps the entry with the newest worker_epoch it has seen and ignores older ones; a worker life that does not accept turn_handoff emits nothing (its registration writes turn_handoff: null).")),GF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_invalidate"),event:T("ui.render").describe("@internal The render event whose version moved; the engine versions `ui.render` as one event, so no component or instance is named."),prompts_screened:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present while a mod may change what is recorded of a typed line (a hook on `prompt.submit`, `command.run`, `session.append` or, for a line sent to the session, `session.receive`; mods not yet known; or an organization policy that lists a plugin or cannot be read): what the `prompts_screened_v1` capability says on `system/init`, said as soon as a plugin load or reload makes it so. Absent says nothing: the next `system/init` does."),instances:C(u({surface:o().describe("@internal The surface that drew the instance."),component:o().describe("@internal The component of the instance."),instance_id:o().describe("@internal The engine's requestId of the instance, as the ui_render that drew it named it.")})).optional().describe("@internal Present when a plugin wrote state (`$.state.set`) that these instances read while they were drawn, or a `Client` drawn in them failed and its plugin heard it (`ui.fault`), and nothing else moved: only they are stale, and the surface may re-ask them alone. Absent, every mounted instance may be stale, as before. A surface that ignores the field re-asks everything and stays correct."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('@internal Fire-and-forget push from the hooks engine when a plugin called `$.ui.invalidate("ui.render")` or a plugin loaded or reloaded: every tree a remote surface drew through ui_render may be stale. The surface re-asks each mounted instance; the engine dedups the evaluations. Bumps from one plugin fold to at most one per 100 ms. With `instances`, a state write or a `Client` that failed made only those stale.')),BF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_log"),plugin:o().describe("@internal The calling plugin's registry name, as the hooks engine knows it (never plugin-settable)."),text:o().describe("@internal The line the plugin gave: be ready for up to 10000 characters of it and then an ellipsis. Plain text: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session for one `$.ui.log(text)` line: what the REPL appends to its transcript as a dim notice under the plugin's name. Not sent to the model. Lines are not counted across the session: a plugin's lines are the plugin's to write, however long the session runs; ones logged before the stream opened are delivered first, in order.")),HF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_toast"),plugin:o().describe("@internal The calling plugin's registry name, as the hooks engine knows it (never plugin-settable)."),text:o().describe("@internal The line the plugin gave: be ready for up to 10000 characters of it and then an ellipsis. Plain text: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),timeout_ms:E().int().describe("@internal How long the REPL would keep it on the notification bar: the plugin's `timeoutMs`, or the engine's default (4000)."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session for one `$.ui.toast(text)`: what the REPL shows on the notification bar under the prompt for a few seconds, under the plugin's name. Be ready for a plugin's toasts at no fixed rate; nothing counts how many a plugin has shown.")),KF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_status"),plugin:o().describe("@internal The calling plugin's registry name, as the hooks engine knows it (never plugin-settable). One status line per plugin."),text:o().nullable().describe("@internal The plugin's pinned line, replacing its previous one; null removes it. Be ready for up to 10000 characters of it and then an ellipsis. Plain text: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session for one `$.ui.status(text)`: the line the REPL pins under the prompt beside the engine's own notices until the plugin's next call replaces or clears it.")),FF=/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/,pe=f(()=>o().regex(FF).describe("@internal A pane's id as the plugin opened it (`$.ui.open({ id })`): 1 to 64 of letters, digits, `_`, `-`. What a ui_render for `Pane` carries as `instance_id`.")),WF=f(()=>u({id:pe(),title:o().describe("@internal The pane's tab label: the `title` its latest open gave, else its id. Plugin-authored text the engine bounded and freed of control characters: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),plugin:o().describe("@internal The registry name of the plugin that opened it, as the engine knows it; a `$.ui.scroll` or `$.ui.focus` of the pane is that plugin's alone."),close_on_escape:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Escape while the pane holds the keyboard, or at an idle empty composer, closes it as the person's close does (send ui_close); absent, Escape only returns the keyboard."),hold_toasts:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal The surface holds its transient toasts while this pane is the one shown, and shows them once it closes."),rows:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The body rows its content asked for while seated inline; a request the person's own sizing overrides."),columns:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The body columns its content asked for while docked; a request the person's own sizing overrides.")}).describe("@internal One placed pane as the engine holds it (plugins/functionHooks/panes/): which, its tab, its opener, and the manners and size its latest open asked for.")),yo=f(()=>u({panes:C(WF()).describe("@internal The placed open panes in open order (an open appends, a close removes); a pane waiting unplaced is not listed. The surface draws one container for them: tabs when several, the shown one visible."),shown_id:pe().nullable().describe("@internal The pane the surface shows: the last opened or focused, never displacing one the person holds; null with none open."),focused_id:pe().nullable().describe("@internal The pane the person gave the keyboard (ui_pane_focus); null while the composer has it."),focus_requested_id:pe().nullable().describe("@internal A standing `$.ui.open({ focus: true })` the surface has yet to judge: honour it (focus the pane, send ui_pane_focus with its id) only while the composer is empty and idle and no dialog is up, else refuse it (ui_pane_focus with null).")}).describe("@internal The session's panes as a remote surface draws them: what ui_panes answers and the ui_panes push carries.")),YF=f(()=>yo().extend({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_panes"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session whenever the pane roster a remote surface draws changes: a plugin opened, retitled or closed a pane, the person showed, focused or closed one, a placing client attached. Deduplicated: an unchanged roster is not pushed. A pane opened before the stream armed produces none; read the roster with ui_panes on mount.")),$F=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_scroll"),client_id:o().describe("@internal The client drawing the site, as it attached."),component:F(["Pane","AbovePrompt"]).describe("@internal Which site: a pane's body or the band."),instance_id:o().describe("@internal The pane's id, or `above-prompt` for the band."),offset:E().int().describe("@internal The first content row the window is to show, 0 at the top, clamped to the tree as the surface last reported it."),follow_end:M().optional().describe("@internal True: keep the window on the tree's last rows through the draws to come, until the person or a plugin next moves it."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push when a plugin's `$.ui.scroll` (through the `ui.scroll` chain) moved the window of a site a remote surface draws: the surface scrolls the site's body there. The person's own scrolls (ui_scroll requests) are never pushed back.")),XF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_focus"),client_id:o().describe("@internal The client drawing the site, as it attached."),component:F(["Pane","AbovePrompt"]).describe("@internal Which site: a pane's body or the band."),instance_id:o().describe("@internal The pane's id, or `above-prompt` for the band."),plugin:o().describe("@internal Whose element takes the ring: its `press.plugin`."),key:o().describe("@internal The element's `props.key`, as ui.press names it."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push when a plugin's `$.ui.focus` (through the `ui.focus` chain) moved the focus ring of a site a remote surface draws onto one of its Buttons, Inputs or Selects: the surface focuses that element if it still draws it. The person's own moves (ui_focus requests) are never pushed back.")),QF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("commands_changed"),commands:C(Ft()),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Fire-and-forget push of the full slash-command list after a mid-session change (e.g. skills discovered dynamically as the agent works in a subdirectory). Clients should REPLACE their cached command list with this payload; supportedCommands() tracks the latest push, so a re-fetch returns the same fresh list.")),JF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("notification"),key:o(),text:o(),priority:F(["low","medium","high","immediate"]),color:o().optional(),timeout_ms:E().int().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Loop-side text notification. Mirrors the interactive REPL notification queue (key/priority/timeout). JSX notifications are not emitted on this channel.")),ZF=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_progress"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Wt),tool_use_id:o().optional(),description:o(),subagent_type:o().optional().describe("Subagent type for Task tool subagents."),usage:u({total_tokens:E().int(),tool_uses:E().int(),duration_ms:E().int()}),last_tool_name:o().optional(),summary:o().optional().describe("A one-line status for the task's row. For a local_agent task it is the model-generated progress summary (only when the agentProgressSummaries option is on); for a backgrounded mcp_task it is the MCP server's own bounded status message, emitted once per change without any option. Render it when present regardless of task type."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),eW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("thinking_tokens"),estimated_tokens:E().int(),estimated_tokens_delta:E().int(),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Client uuid of the user message that triggered this turn (submitMessage options.uuid), stamped on every thinking_tokens frame of a headless (stream-json / Agent SDK) turn so a consumer can attribute thinking progress to the send it answers before any reply frame arrives. On a synthetic/scheduled (meta) turn it names the last user message folded into the turn so far once one has been, else the turn's own uuid when the host vouches it is the client event's own (delivered content such as a Slack owner ping). Absent on turns that neither had a client uuid nor folded a user message in, on Remote Control (interactive terminal) sessions, and from older producers."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Live thinking-token estimate, digested from thinking_delta.estimated_tokens during the redacted-thinking phase (where the API otherwise streams only pings). estimated_tokens is the running total for the current thinking block; estimated_tokens_delta is the increment carried by this frame. Approximate progress for spinners/pills, not the authoritative billed output_tokens.")),tW=f(()=>u({type:T("tool_use_summary"),summary:o(),preceding_tool_use_ids:C(o()),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),timestamp:o().optional().describe("@internal ISO timestamp when the summary was created on the originating process. From internal ToolUseSummaryMessage.timestamp.")})),sW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("memory_recall"),mode:F(["select","synthesize"]).describe("How memories were surfaced: 'select' returns full file bodies chosen by the parallel selector; 'synthesize' returns a Sonnet-authored paragraph distilled from many tiny memories."),memories:C(u({path:o().describe("Absolute path to the memory file, a synthesis sentinel of the form `<synthesis:DIR>` when mode is 'synthesize', or an https URL when scope is 'organization'."),scope:F(["personal","team","organization"]),content:o().optional().describe("The surfaced memory body. Always present for 'synthesize' mode and 'organization' scope (neither has an on-disk path to lazy-load from); absent for file-backed 'select' entries (renderers lazy-load from path).")})),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('Emitted when the memory recall supervisor surfaces relevant memories into the turn. Mirrors the CLI relevant_memories attachment so SDK renderers can show "Recalled from memory" inline.')),nW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("elicitation_complete"),mcp_server_name:o(),elicitation_id:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when an MCP server confirms that a URL-mode elicitation is complete.")),rW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("permission_denied"),tool_name:o(),tool_use_id:o(),agent_id:o().optional().describe("Subagent ID when the denied tool call originated inside a subagent. Mirrors can_use_tool for host-side routing."),decision_reason_type:o().optional().describe("Discriminator from PermissionDecisionReason (e.g. 'classifier', 'asyncAgent', 'mode', 'rule')."),decision_reason_code:F(eer).optional().describe("@internal A closed-set code for a reason a host can act on, beside decision_reason_type (whose values are unchanged): 'outside_reads_blocked' (permissions.blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories refused the path), 'memory_paused' (memory is paused for the session), 'classifier_transcript_too_long' (the auto-mode classifier's transcript exceeded its context window). Absent for every other reason; new values are additive."),decision_reason:o().optional().describe("Human-readable reason from the deciding component, when available."),message:o().describe("The rejection message returned to the model in the tool_result."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when a tool call is auto-denied without an interactive permission prompt (e.g. auto-mode classifier, dontAsk mode, headless-agent auto-deny, or a deny rule). With a permission prompt surface (stdio/SDK canUseTool), the 'ask' path surfaces via a can_use_tool control_request and this event covers the 'deny' short-circuit. Without one (bare -p / SDK query() with no canUseTool), 'ask' decisions are terminal, so this event also covers those implicit denials. Best-effort advisory: in rare races a denial can book without a frame or a frame can lack a booking twin \u2014 result.permission_denials is the authoritative record. Denials that resolve before canUseTool runs \u2014 PreToolUse hook denies, deny-rule overrides of hook allow/ask decisions, and file-tool calls (Read, Edit, Write) refused by a path-scoped deny rule \u2014 are not covered here, and neither is the MCP --permission-prompt-tool surface (the prompt tool is the host there).")),oW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("permission_check_status"),tool_use_id:o(),agent_id:o().optional().describe("Subagent ID when the tool call originated inside a subagent, as on permission_denied."),status:F(["checking","done"]).describe("'checking': the call has waited about four seconds on its check and is still waiting. 'done': that wait is over."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A tool call has been waiting on its automatic permission check (the auto-mode classifier) for longer than usual. It lets a host say that the step is being checked, where it would otherwise show a silent pause that looks like a running tool. Most checks answer within about four seconds and send nothing. The wait can include the rest of the model's streaming reply, or a dialog the host is showing about the check: prefer those indicators. Every 'checking' is followed by a 'done' for the same tool_use_id. 'done' does not say whether the call was allowed: the tool's result, permission_denied or a can_use_tool request does. One call can send more than one pair. Display-only and best-effort: a frame can be lost, so also stop showing it at any other frame for this tool_use_id or at the end of the turn. Emitted on the output stream of -p/SDK sessions.")),aW=f(()=>u({type:T("prompt_suggestion"),suggestion:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Predicted next user prompt, emitted after each turn when promptSuggestions is enabled.")),d8=f(()=>u({type:T("attachment"),attachment:oe().describe("Internal Attachment discriminated union (at-mentioned files, IDE selections, pasted images, structured output, deferred tool-use). Wire shape pending a dedicated SDKAttachment schema."),timestamp:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when the engine yields an AttachmentMessage into the turn stream. Carries user-attached content (at-mentioned files, IDE selections, pasted media) and loop-attached data (structured output, deferred tool-use payloads). SDKResultMessage.structured_output and .deferred_tool_use are derived from these frames. From internal QueryEvent 'attachment'.")),c8=f(()=>u({type:T("tombstone"),message:oe().describe("The internal Message being tombstoned. Wire shape pending a dedicated schema."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a previously-yielded message is superseded or removed from the transcript (e.g., streaming\u2192non-streaming fallback removes a partial orphan). Consumers that render or persist the stream should remove the referenced message. From internal QueryEvent 'tombstone'.")),iW=f(()=>u({type:T("conversation_reset"),new_conversation_id:p(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),trigger:F(["clear","plan_mode_exit","fresh_session","onboarding"]).optional().describe("What discarded the conversation: 'clear' is the /clear command (or its /reset and /new aliases), 'plan_mode_exit' is leaving plan mode with the clear-context option, 'fresh_session' is a flow that starts a fresh session to implement an approved plan, 'onboarding' is an onboarding flow re-run inside an existing session. Informational: a consumer should reset on every conversation_reset frame whatever this says, and treat an absent (older emitter) or unrecognized value as an unspecified reset."),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Only with trigger 'clear': the uuid of the user message whose /clear was executed (the client's own uuid for that message when it came from a Remote Control or stream-json client, otherwise the uuid the CLI assigned to the typed command). Lets a consumer match this frame to a /clear message it has already seen, wiping once whichever arrives first, instead of relying on arrival order. Absent for the other triggers, when that uuid is not a canonical UUID, and from older emitters."),timestamp:o().optional().describe('When the reset happened, as an ISO 8601 string in UTC read from the clock of the process that performed it. Meant for display, such as the time on a "conversation cleared" row, not for ordering frames. Absent from older emitters; a consumer can fall back to the time it received the frame.')}).describe("Emitted by /clear, plan-mode exit, fresh-session, and onboarding flows. The surface should mount a fresh transcript under new_conversation_id and reset any cached session title. From internal QueryEvent 'conversation_reset'.")),h8=f(()=>u({type:T("api_metrics"),event:Br("type",[u({type:T("start"),ttft_ms:E().int(),id:o().optional(),message_id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("end"),output_tokens:E().int(),id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("content_block_start"),id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("thinking_progress"),estimated_tokens_delta:E().int(),id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("thinking_signature"),chars:E().int(),id:o().optional()})]).describe("Per-API-call OTPS/TTFT lifecycle event. Optional id correlates parallel subagent start/end; serial callers omit it."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a subagent's API call reports TTFT or output_tokens for OTPS (output-tokens-per-second) metering. From internal QueryEvent 'api_metrics' (ApiMetricsLifecycleEvent).")),u8=f(()=>u({type:T("os_notification"),message:o(),notification_type:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a tool (PushNotificationTool, the Computer Use wrapper) or turn-end cleanup requests a native OS notification. The surface dispatches to its platform notification channel (iTerm2/Kitty/Ghostty/bell in the terminal; native IPC for desktop/IDE). From internal QueryEvent 'os_notification'.")),p8=f(()=>u({type:T("apply_flag_settings"),settings:fe(o(),oe()).describe("Shallow-merge flag-settings patch \u2014 same shape as SDKControlApplyFlagSettingsRequest.settings."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Output-direction counterpart to SDKControlApplyFlagSettingsRequest. Emitted when slash commands that toggle flag settings request a batched write that the surface applies to its AppState. From internal QueryEvent 'apply_flag_settings'.")),lW=f(()=>u({type:T("command_lifecycle"),command_uuid:o().describe("The queued command's uuid \u2014 the client-supplied uuid on the inbound message. Commands enqueued without a uuid (e.g. the one-shot `-p \"prompt\"` string path) emit no lifecycle events. Renamed from Engine 'uuid' to avoid collision with the universal message uuid field."),state:F(["queued","started","completed","cancelled","discarded","refused"]),ran_ahead:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Only on a repeated 'queued' frame, written only to the VS Code panel: the plugin hooks of this `immediate` command, typed during a turn, have run and answered ahead of its turn. The command is still queued, and its own turn prints the answer."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fate of a queued command (slash command or queued user prompt). 'queued' when the inbound message enters the command queue; 'started' when it drains into a turn; then exactly one terminal state: 'completed' \u2014 the turn that consumed it ended cleanly; 'cancelled' \u2014 removed by cancel_async_message (the host's own, or \u2014 with Remote Control enabled (remote_control) \u2014 a remote client withdrawing a prompt that a remote client queued: such a uuid was never sent by the host, so there is nothing to resend and the frame is informational), swept by an interrupt with cancel_queued:true, caught by a pending cancel just before dispatch, or consumed into a turn that was aborted (interrupt) or died on a hard failure (model_error, prompt_too_long, ...) \u2014 cancelled-over-completed is deliberate dup-over-loss for exactly-once resenders, so a fold answered earlier in a failed turn may be re-sent (a per-fold ledger refinement is tracked separately) \u2014 but resenders must not blindly resend on 'cancelled': user-requested removals arrive as the same state, so correlate against your own cancel_async_message responses AND against any interrupt receipt's `cancelled` list (interrupt_cancel_queued_v1) and resend only cancels neither source accounts for; 'discarded' \u2014 the session ended (end_session) with the command still queued, or with a peer message still held by the receive-side policy (not preceded by 'queued' then); 'refused' \u2014 declined by the session's receive-side policy before entering the queue (outright, or after being held): not preceded by 'queued', and it will not run in this session. Known gap: folds consumed by turns ending via max_turns / hook_stopped / tool_deferred / background_requested report 'completed' even though their content may only be answered on continuation/resume. 'completed' means the consuming turn ended, not that the result frame was delivered. Ordering relative to the result frame is per-path: a command that starts a fresh turn emits 'completed' AFTER that turn's result frame (later still when the result is held back for background tasks); a command folded into an already-in-flight turn emits 'completed' BEFORE that turn's result frame. Not a strict pairing: a terminal state may arrive for a command_uuid that never emitted 'started' (control-request ACKs, duplicate deliveries); internally-enqueued commands (cron triggers, teammate shutdown prompts, deferred-turn resume) mint a fresh uuid at enqueue and emit started/terminal without 'queued'; a turn that fails by throwing can leave 'started' without a terminal state; and a peer message the queue's own admission guard drops after acceptance (rate limit, duplicate collapse, loop guard, queued-peer cap) currently leaves 'queued' without a terminal state \u2014 on process exit a wrapper should synthesize 'discarded' for uuids it has not seen reach a terminal state. The exactly-one-terminal guarantee is per worker process, not per uuid lifetime: after CCR redelivery a 'discarded' uuid legitimately re-emits queued\u2192\u2026\u2192terminal on the next worker. Emitted on the stdout stream in -p/SDK sessions; remote transports (mobile/desktop bridge) receive the equivalent signal via delivery ACKs instead. A host driving a cloud-hosted session through the headless `--cloud` client also receives 'cancelled' (and only that state) after its interrupt with cancel_queued:true, synthesized from the session's receipt: for a uuid the host sent and did not cancel itself it means the cloud session dropped that command \u2014 it will not run; do not resend it; the frames follow the interrupt's success response, are not ordered against the interrupted turn's result, and unknown uuids (another client's prompt) may appear. From internal QueryEvent 'command_lifecycle'.")),hWo=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("code_change_published"),provider:o().describe("Forge classification derived from the URL's shape: 'github', 'github-enterprise', 'gitlab', or 'bitbucket' for pull/merge requests, 'gerrit' for a change on a googlesource.com Gerrit host, and other values for other review systems in internal builds. A naming hint, not host trust \u2014 an unrecognized host with a PR-shaped path classifies as 'github-enterprise'. Open set; treat an unknown value as a valid provider you don't recognize, never as an error."),url:o().describe("Web URL of the published change, as printed in the command output. Unverified \u2014 do not route authenticated requests to this host without your own validation."),repo:o().describe("Repository path from the URL (owner/name on GitHub; may carry more segments on GitLab). Shape-validated (forge path segments only; exactly owner/name except on GitLab) but not verified against any forge \u2014 on GitLab a leading segment can be a nested group, so never interpret it as a host."),identifier:o().describe("Provider-native change identifier \u2014 the PR/MR (or other change) number as a string."),action:F(["created","edited","merged","commented","closed","reopened","ready","draft","auto-merge-enabled","auto-merge-disabled","pushed","checked-out","started"]).optional().describe("What the session did that produced this announcement: the flag-aware gh pr verb it ran ('created', 'closed', 'auto-merge-enabled', \u2026), 'pushed' for a push to a branch that has a PR (or a push that opened or updated a Gerrit change), 'checked-out' for gh pr checkout, or 'started' for the open change on the branch the session's working directory was on when a Claude Desktop session started or resumed (sent once, at the first initialize) \u2014 always sent by current producers, absent only from older ones."),branch:o().optional().describe("The session's working branch when it produced the change. Sent for providers whose changes have no head branch of their own ('gerrit'), so a host can place the change on that checkout, and with 'created' on any provider: the branch the create was opened from (its --head / --source-branch flag, else the working branch), so a host can show the new change before its own forge lookup answers. Absent otherwise."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A code change from this session went out for review (a pull/merge request, or another provider's change in internal builds). Fires when the harness sees the change published or links the session to a PR \u2014 on creation, and also when the session contributes to an existing one (gh pr edit/close/ready, gh pr checkout, a push to a branch that has an open PR) \u2014 so bind on every event, not just the first; re-emission for the same URL is possible and idempotent. Provenance: values are scraped from the command's captured output (the last PR-shaped URL printed) or a gh pr view lookup. Captured output is not only the forge CLI's own text \u2014 hook output or files printed by the same command can contribute \u2014 so treat the fields as a binding hint: display them, but verify against the forge with your own credential-scoped lookup before routing authenticated requests or trusting the host. Best-effort, not exhaustive: a crash before the link, gh printing no URL to a piped capture (gh pr merge), or an unrecognized forge means no event \u2014 keep your provider-API lookup as the source of truth and treat this as the trigger.")),yWo=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("vcs_state_changed"),kind:F(["commit","push","merge","rebase"]).describe("What class of mutation was observed. New kinds may be added \u2014 treat an unrecognized kind exactly like a recognized one (something changed, go look)."),cwd:o().describe("The directory the shell was in when the command finished (so an inner cd is reflected; a git -C target is not) \u2014 a hint about where to look, never proof of what moved: confirm against the repository itself."),branch:o().optional().describe("The branch a commit landed on (from the commit summary line git prints per commit) or a push updated (from the ref-update line git prints per pushed ref, e.g. `HEAD -> other`). Commit and push events carry it; a command that pushed several branches emits one push event per branch. A best-effort hint: absent whenever attribution is uncertain (a push whose output was redirected still emits, with no branch; a push that also updated a ref whose name could not be carried emits a nameless event beside the named ones), and never a required key."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A harness-observed shell command mutated repository state (git commit/cherry-pick, push, merge, rebase \u2014 dry runs excluded). A cache-invalidation signal with a deliberately minimal payload: beyond classification it carries the branch acted on and the directory the shell finished in, both hints about where to look, so consumers still re-read state (head, PR status) from the repository instead of decoding the event. Derived from the same detection as the tool result's structured gitOperation data, so the two agree on what happened; only a push's `branch` is attributed more strictly here (from the push's own output section) than in that data. A compound command can emit more than one kind (a merge and a rebase in one command collapse to the latter), and a push of several branches emits one push event per branch; coalesce freely. Best-effort, not exhaustive: only foreground mutations run through the Bash/PowerShell tools are observed \u2014 a backgrounded command whose confirming output had not printed at capture time emits nothing. A foreground push whose ref lines were redirected or silenced still emits, on the invoking command's zero exit code alone (so a compound that swallows a failed push's code also emits; the event is a prompt to look, never a claim).")),dW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("dev_intent"),kind:F(Yjr).describe("What kind of development the evidence shows. 'ios_app' from the conversation (in the main thread or a subagent): Claude ran one of Apple's tools for an iOS SDK, an iOS destination or the Simulator (xcodebuild, xcrun or swift for an iOS SDK, destination or target, simctl, devicectl), wrote iOS source into a Swift or Objective-C file (import UIKit, an import of a UIKit header, or an .iOS platform in a package manifest), or wrote or read build settings that name an iOS SDK or target iPhone or iPad; no Swift edit is needed for these. A command counts only where the shell would run it, so one that is quoted, in a here-document or in a comment does not. Weaker evidence (an iOS deployment target, any other TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY setting, iOS source written into another kind of file, an iOS tool named inside a quoted script) counts only beside a .swift file that Claude wrote or edited. A macOS-only app or server-side Swift package never qualifies. 'ios_app' from the project scan: the session's git repository has an Xcode project, at its root or in its ios/, iosApp/, macos/ or apple/ folder, whose build settings name an iOS SDK or target iPhone or iPad. 'android_app' from the conversation: Claude wrote or edited a .kt, .kts or .java file and something it wrote, read, or ran is Android-specific (a manifest, the Android Gradle plugin, an android.* import, adb, a Gradle variant task such as assembleDebug, the emulator), so a Kotlin server or a multiplatform module with no Android target never qualifies. 'android_app' from the project scan: the session's git repository has an AndroidManifest.xml, or the Android Gradle plugin in a build script or version catalog, at its root or in its android/ folder. More kinds will be added; ignore a kind you do not recognize."),trigger:F(XA).optional().describe("The evidence the detection fired on, for a host's own analytics. From the conversation, it is the first platform-specific evidence seen, whichever order it and a file edit the rule required arrived in; an 'ios_app' detection with no Swift edit names the first evidence that is enough alone. 'ios_app': 'uikit_import' (import UIKit or .iOS( in text Claude wrote), 'xcode_project' (iOS build settings written, or seen in a tool result such as a pbxproj or xcconfig read), 'ios_command' (an iOS SDK, an iOS destination or target, or the Simulator's tools, in a command Claude ran). 'android_app': 'android_import' (import android. in text Claude wrote), 'android_manifest' (a manifest path or body written, or seen in a tool result), 'gradle_plugin' (the Android Gradle plugin written, or seen in a tool result), 'android_command' (adb, the emulator, sdkmanager, avdmanager, react-native run-android or a Gradle variant task). The edit values 'swift_edit', 'kotlin_edit' and 'java_edit' are reserved for rules that need no other evidence; no rule sends them yet. 'project_scan', for any kind: a scan of the session's git repository. The CLI scans when the process starts and sends the result. After each conversation_reset, it sends the latest scan's result again. A conversation whose scan did not find every kind scans again, uncached, when its first message arrives, so a kind that scan missed can come after that message. A client that needs to tell project evidence from conversation evidence checks for 'project_scan'. More values will be added; ignore one you do not recognize. Absent from CLI versions that predate the field, which send only conversation evidence."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The conversation, or the git repository it runs in, shows a known kind of development work, for hosts that key tooling on it (Claude Code Desktop opens its iOS Simulator entry point on ios_app). Sent with trigger as its only other payload. Per conversation per process, each kind is sent at most once from conversation evidence and at most once from the project scan. Conversation evidence is sent when it first completes, or at startup when a resumed conversation already has it. Project evidence comes from a CLI process in print mode (-p), which is how the Agent SDK and Claude Code Desktop start it. It is sent at startup, after each conversation_reset, and at the first message of a conversation whose earlier scan did not find every kind. Either can arrive before system/init. A rewind or compaction never retracts it and only a conversation_reset starts over. Treat each kind as a sticky fact about the conversation: a client that needs only the kind can ignore repeats, and one that needs to know what set it off reads trigger.")),cW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("turn_preempted"),reason:o().describe("Why the turn was stopped. 'rapid_followup': the user's next message arrived before the running turn showed any output. More reasons may be added; treat an unknown one the same way."),preempted_by_uuid:o().describe("The client-supplied uuid of the queued user message the turn was stopped for. It runs next, together with any messages queued behind it."),preempted_message_uuids:C(o()).describe("The client-supplied uuids of the user messages the stopped turn was running (uuid-less members omitted; may be empty)."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The CLI itself stopped the running turn so a user's rapid follow-up message is answered at once, exactly as a priority 'now' message would have (running shell commands are backgrounded, not killed). Sent at the moment of the stop, so it precedes the stopped turn's result frame (terminal_reason aborted_streaming or aborted_tools) and its members' 'cancelled' command_lifecycle frames (it can also precede the follow-up's own 'queued' command_lifecycle frame): a host renders that turn as superseded by the follow-up rather than as interrupted, does not resend its messages, and treats only preempted_by_uuid as picked up. At most one per burst: the turn the follow-up starts is never preempted this way. Emitted on the stdout stream in -p/SDK sessions only, and only to a consumer that declared rapidFollowupPreempt on its initialize request, while the feature's rollout flag is on: without both, the turn is not stopped at all. Explicit 'now' and 'later' priorities never produce it.")),hW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("per_turn_effort_changed"),per_turn_effort_active:M().describe("Always false: a change to true is reported only by system/init."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe(`@internal The conversation stopped sending effort per turn, because the server refused its per-turn effort message or a role:"system" message. From the retried request on, an effort change rewrites the cached prefix, for every model, until a later system/init says otherwise. Sent once, when the conversation first turns per-turn effort off, before the retried request's output, on the output stream of -p/SDK sessions; sent again only if the server refuses again after the conversation's refusals are cleared (for example by /clear or a compaction). It can repeat a false the host already holds. Pairs with system/init's per_turn_effort_active.`)),uW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("file_attachments_missing"),message_uuid:o().describe("The uuid of the inbound user message whose file_attachments named the files."),sent_count:E().int().describe("How many well-formed file_attachments entries that message had."),missing:C(u({file_uuid:o().describe("The file_uuid of the message's file_attachments entry."),reason:o().describe("Why, as a short code. 'download': it could not be downloaded. 'too_large': the file is over the size a session accepts, so sending it again cannot help. 'not_found': the files route answered 404 or 410: the file is most likely deleted, or is not this session's to read, so sending it again rarely helps. 'write': it was downloaded and could not be saved. 'no_token': the session had no credential to download with. 'not_finished': the step that fetches a message's files was cut short or threw, so all its files are listed, though some may have been saved. 'count_cap': the message named more files than a session fetches, and this one was over the limit. More codes may be added; read an unknown one as 'did not arrive'.")})).describe("The files Claude was told did not arrive, each once. Never empty."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Some files a user message sent by reference (file_attachments) did not arrive where Claude can read them, and Claude was told so in that message's text or in a later note; the turn runs on without them. A host can use it to tell the person who sent them; a session with Remote Control on also sends it to its Remote Control clients itself. Sent once the message is queued, or later when a download or a save that runs beside its turn fails, or both; only for a message that reached the session through Claude Code Remote or over Remote Control, never for input a local host writes; whenever Claude is told, and for the same files. Queued counts as told: a message that a Stop then clears from the queue, or that a UserPromptSubmit hook then blocks, still sends it, though Claude never reads that message. Nothing is sent when every file arrives, when the message is refused or has no uuid (over Remote Control, one not in UUID form counts as none), for another session's message that was first held for approval, when the only files lost are those over the count limit, or for a picture that reached Claude inside the message and whose saved copy alone was lost; on claude-code-server the same holds for a text file whose copy was to be written late, at a use of the tool container. A file whose entry carries a sha256 (another session's transfer) is reported to Claude by name instead, and is listed here only under 'not_finished'. Each event states the whole list for its message: a save lost after the message was queued, or a delivery to a new worker, states it again, so keep the last one per message_uuid. If its files all arrive that time nothing is sent, and the earlier event stands. One exception: a worker that takes over a turn already begun may fetch and save the message's files again, except pictures and files with a sha256. Its event lists only those it could not fetch or save, so it can leave out a picture an earlier event listed, and can list a file the earlier worker did save.")),pW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("cloud_session_delta"),seq:E().int().positive().describe("Rises by one with each of these frames this client writes (1 for the first); never reset by an init. A reader keeps the highest it has applied and drops a lower one."),changed:C(o()).describe('The top-level keys of `cloud_session` whose value differs from the last block this client wrote (on an init or one of these frames), e.g. ["serving"] or ["calls", "connection"]; never empty. A hint for what to redraw: the block below is complete either way.'),cloud_session:My().describe("The whole block exactly as the next init would carry it (see SDKSystemMessage.cloud_session): replace the held copy, do not merge."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Written by the headless stream-json client of a cloud-hosted session (the same client that puts `cloud_session` on its init frames), and by nothing else: the session's status changed between two inits \u2014 for instance this machine's serving word or its reason, its stream to the session (`connection`), a call it serves starting, waiting on the session's approval or ending (`calls`), a serving notice said (`notices`). Only after the first init \u2014 except from a serve-only helper (`--attach-serve`), whose stream to the session carries no init: it writes these from the start, its first (`seq` 1) listing every key of the block in `changed`, so a host that has read no init takes that frame's block as its first snapshot. At most a few per second (changes within a quarter second are written as one frame carrying the latest block); none when nothing differs. An init is always a complete snapshot, so a host that (re)attaches resynchronises from the first init it reads and applies these on top. Display-only; ignore it if you do not draw session status.")),m8=f(()=>u({type:T("set_expanded_view"),expanded_view:F(["none","tasks","teammates"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Hint to expand a side panel. Enum is a superset of AppState.expandedView for back-compat; do not narrow.")),Ro=f(()=>u({type:T("active_goal"),value:u({condition:o(),iterations:E().int(),set_at:E().int(),tokens_at_start:E().int(),last_reason:o().optional()}).nullable(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when the user's /goal Stop hook reports met (clears) or not-yet-met (bumps iterations + last_reason). Any surface with a goal indicator re-renders from this. value is null when the goal is cleared. From internal QueryEvent 'active_goal'.")),vo=f(()=>u({type:T("autocompact_state"),value:u({enabled:M().describe("Whether auto-compact is enabled on the worker (autoCompactEnabled setting + DISABLE_AUTO_COMPACT / DISABLE_COMPACT env; DISABLE_COMPACT also disables manual /compact)."),effective_window:E().int().describe("The worker's resolved auto-compact window in tokens \u2014 resolveAutoCompactWindow output clamped to the model window, honoring /autocompact, CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW, clientdata and experiment overrides."),threshold:E().int().describe("The token count where threshold-triggered compaction fires on the worker \u2014 effective_window minus the summary buffer, lowered further by CLAUDE_AUTOCOMPACT_PCT_OVERRIDE when set."),enforced:M().describe("Whether threshold-triggered compaction will actually fire at `threshold`. False when the worker defers to the API's prompt-too-long (reactive mode) or context collapse owns headroom \u2014 clients then show % of window used instead of a countdown to a compaction that won't happen."),source:F(["env","settings","clientdata","experiment","model-default","unknown-model","auto"]).describe("Which input decided the window (resolveAutoCompactWindow precedence order).")}),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe(`@internal Worker-resolved auto-compact state, emitted by CCR workers at boot, whenever the resolved state changes (/autocompact, model switch, settings change), re-checked at each turn start, and re-emitted after a conversation reset. Thin clients adopt it so the "% until auto-compact" indicator counts down to the worker's real compaction trigger instead of re-resolving against client-local state. Turn-scoped divergence is accepted: a turn running under a skill/command frontmatter model override compacts against that model's window while the frame keeps the resting model's (the local indicator shares this limitation). From sessionState.onAutocompactInputsChanged.`)),_8=f(()=>u({type:T("set_in_progress_tool_use_ids"),op:u({action:F(["add","remove"]),ids:C(o())}),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when tool execution adds/removes tool_use ids from the mid-execution set (after permission grant, before result). Surfaces use this to show which tools are running. From internal QueryEvent 'set_in_progress_tool_use_ids'.")),g8=f(()=>u({type:T("hint_clears"),ids:C(o()),content_by_id:fe(o(),o()),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when the server-side context-hint reject path reports cleared tool_use ids after a retry/fallback. The surface re-runs clearToolResultsById on its message list so subsequent turns match the API's view. From internal QueryEvent 'hint_clears'.")),f8=f(()=>u({type:T("open_message_selector"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted by /rewind to open the message-selector overlay. Fire-and-forget \u2014 the user's selection returns through a separate channel. From internal QueryEvent 'open_message_selector'.")),b8=f(()=>u({type:T("compact_progress"),event:Br("type",[u({type:T("hooks_start"),hook_type:F(["pre_compact","post_compact","session_start"])}),u({type:T("compact_start"),hint_text:o().nullable().optional()}),u({type:T("compact_end")})]).describe("In-progress compaction lifecycle event."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted while compaction is running (hook phase, compact start, compact end). Distinct from system/compact_boundary, which reports the post-compaction transcript boundary after completion. From internal QueryEvent 'compact_progress' (CompactEvent Delta-track arm).")),E8=f(()=>u({type:T("stream_mode"),mode:F(["tool-input","tool-use","requesting","responding","thinking"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when the engine's spinner phase changes during compaction. From internal QueryEvent 'stream_mode' (CompactEvent Delta-track arm).")),S8=f(()=>Br("op",[u({type:T("response_length"),op:T("add"),delta:E().int(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}),u({type:T("response_length"),op:T("reset"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})]).describe("@internal Emitted to drive the streaming-output character counter in the spinner ('add' accumulates, 'reset' zeroes on compaction-boundary swap). From internal QueryEvent 'response_length' (Delta-track).")),T8=f(()=>Br("phase",[u({type:T("refusal_continuation"),phase:T("begin"),salvage_text:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}),u({type:T("refusal_continuation"),phase:T("end"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})]).describe("@internal Emitted when a silent refusal-continuation retry begins ('begin' with salvage_text to keep visible in the streaming preview) or ends ('end'). From internal QueryEvent 'refusal_continuation'.")),w8=f(()=>u({sessionId:o().describe("Unique session identifier (UUID)."),summary:o().describe("Display title for the session: custom title, auto-generated summary, or first prompt."),lastModified:E().int().describe("Last modified time in integer milliseconds since epoch."),fileSize:E().int().optional().describe("File size in bytes. Only populated for local JSONL storage."),customTitle:o().optional().describe("User-set session title via /rename."),firstPrompt:o().optional().describe("First meaningful user prompt in the session."),gitBranch:o().optional().describe("Git branch at the end of the session."),cwd:o().optional().describe("Working directory for the session."),tag:o().optional().describe("User-set session tag."),createdAt:E().int().optional().describe("Creation time in integer milliseconds since epoch, extracted from the first entry's timestamp.")}).describe("Session metadata returned by listSessions and getSessionInfo.")),ko=f(()=>Ue([mn(),pn(),sF(),uF(),pF(),mF(),_F(),gF(),SF(),TF(),wF(),AF(),RF(),vF(),kF(),CF(),zF(),OF(),NF(),PF(),MF(),DF(),ZF(),xF(),eW(),UF(),jF(),QF(),JF(),IF(),tW(),sW(),aF(),nW(),rW(),aW(),EF(),fF(),iW(),lW(),hWo(),yWo(),LF(),GF(),BF(),HF(),KF(),pW(),dW(),VF(),qF(),cW(),yF(),hW(),oW(),YF(),$F(),XF(),bF(),uW()]).describe("Every conversational and informational message the CLI emits on its output stream, discriminated by type (and subtype for system/result messages). Consumers should ignore types and subtypes they do not recognize: the set grows over time.")),$t=f(()=>F(["off","cooldown","on"]).describe("Fast mode state: off, in cooldown after rate limit, or actively enabled.")),Xt=f(()=>F(["free","preference","extra_usage_disabled","network_error","unknown","not_first_party","disabled_by_env","model_not_allowed","sdk_opt_in_required","pending"]).describe("Why fast mode can't serve right now. Absent when nothing blocks it (a request may still choose standard speed). A paused-after-rate-limit run is not here; it rides fast_mode_state as 'cooldown'.")),Co=f(()=>u({text:o().describe("The label to show \u2014 already sanitized to a single line of plain text and capped at 6 columns, exactly as the terminal footer renders it after its \u25C6 glyph.")}).describe('@internal The session indicator (the CLAUDE_CODE_FOOTER_INDICATOR environment variable, else the footer_indicator_<text> capability of the main model, else bootstrap client_data.footer_indicator) that the terminal renders as a "\u25C6 <text>" pill in the prompt footer \u2014 an opaque status note operators set per cohort (e.g. to prove a test config reached the session). Carried on `system/init` and the `initialize` response so a host UI (Claude Desktop, IDE webviews) can render the same pill. Absent when nothing is configured; hosts should then render nothing. The client_data source is read from the CLI\'s cached bootstrap data, so a label configured there after that cache was last written first appears on a later `system/init`.'));var Fy=f(()=>oe().describe("A JSON-RPC 2.0 message as defined by the Model Context Protocol (request, notification, or response object).")),mW=f(()=>u({matcher:o().optional(),hookCallbackIds:C(o()).describe("Opaque ids chosen by the client, one per hook function it registered for this matcher. When the hook fires the CLI sends a hook_callback control request carrying one of these ids as callback_id; the client maps it back to its function."),timeout:E().optional()}).describe("Configuration for matching and routing hook callbacks.")),q7n=f(()=>u({subtype:T("initialize"),hooks:fe(uy(),C(mW())).optional(),sdkMcpServers:C(o()).optional(),sdkMcpServerConfigs:fe(o(),uo().omit({type:!0,name:!0})).optional().describe("Settings for the SDK-hosted MCP servers named in sdkMcpServers, keyed by server name. Sent as a separate field so a CLI that predates it ignores it; entries whose name is not in sdkMcpServers, and values that do not match this shape, are ignored rather than rejected. timeout and disableAutoBackground apply when the server is first registered; tools (the per-tool permission policy) adds its ask and deny rules on every initialize that names the server. An initialize never removes a rule; mcp_set_servers replaces them."),sdkMcpServerManifests:fe(o(),u({initializeResult:fe(o(),oe()).describe("The server's verbatim JSON-RPC `initialize` result object (protocolVersion, capabilities, serverInfo, instructions, ...), exactly as the in-process server produced it when the host initialized it with no client capabilities \u2014 not re-serialized or schema-parsed by the host."),toolsListResult:fe(o(),oe()).optional().describe("The server's verbatim JSON-RPC `tools/list` result object. Omit when the initialize result does not advertise the tools capability, when the listing is paginated (nextCursor present), or when it could not be captured \u2014 the CLI then lists over the control channel as before.")})).optional().describe("Optional, keyed by sdk server name (each key should also appear in sdkMcpServers; other keys are ignored). Unlike sdkMcpServerConfigs \u2014 host-declared settings the CLI keeps for the server's lifetime, same shape inline on mcp_set_servers \u2014 this is a one-shot cache of the servers' own handshake output: sent on initialize only, consumed by the connect that follows it, never retained. MCP handshake results the host already obtained from its in-process servers by delivering initialize + notifications/initialized (+ tools/list) to them itself before writing this request. For each such server the CLI answers its own MCP client's initialize and first tools/list from these results and skips the notifications/initialized round trip, so registering N in-process servers costs no mcp_message control round trips before the first turn; tools/call and everything after the handshake still flow as mcp_message exactly as before. The host MUST keep answering mcp_message for every server as if this field were absent: a CLI that predates the field ignores it and performs the full per-server handshake over the control channel, and a newer CLI does the same for any server whose entry is missing or malformed, whose initializeResult.protocolVersion differs from the MCP protocol version the CLI's client requests, or that the CLI had already connected. Entries apply only to the connect that follows this initialize; they are never retained for later reconnects. Over a remote session transport the CLI uses the field only when sdkMcpServerManifestsOrigin is set (see that field); a host on the CLI's own stream-json stdio transport needs no marker. sdk_mcp_manifests_parked in the response reports what became of each entry. Absent (older hosts, the Python SDK, browser clients): unchanged behaviour."),sdkMcpServerManifestsOrigin:F(["host_per_send"]).optional().describe("The host's statement that it composed this request's sdkMcpServerManifests for this very send, from its servers' current state. Needed only by a host that reaches the CLI over a remote session transport, where an unmarked sdkMcpServerManifests is not used (a recorded registration could be handed to a later worker). The CLI also refuses a marked manifest whose session-stream frame is more than five minutes old, so capture the manifests when writing the request, not earlier; and it may still decline them when remote manifests are switched off in that CLI, or on any session where the session service has not told the CLI in the last five minutes that only the account owning the session can send events to it. sdk_mcp_manifests_parked in the response says what became of each entry. A value this CLI does not know counts as no marker. A host on the CLI's own stream-json stdio transport may omit this field; a host writing to the stdin of a remote-session worker cannot use manifests at all."),jsonSchema:fe(o(),oe()).optional(),systemPrompt:C(o()).optional(),appendSystemPrompt:o().optional(),systemPromptSnapshot:M().optional().describe("Record the conversation's system prompt once and reuse it verbatim on every later request and resume. Omitted or true (the default): the prompt is rendered on the first request, systemPrompt or appendSystemPrompt included, and the record is sent as-is afterwards \u2014 even when a later launch passes different text \u2014 until compaction. false: never record; the prompt is rendered fresh every request. No effect where system-prompt recording is not yet enabled."),planModeInstructions:o().optional().describe("Custom workflow body for the plan-mode system reminder. Replaces the default code-implementation phases; the CLI still wraps it with the read-only enforcement preamble and the ExitPlanMode protocol footer."),appendSubagentSystemPrompt:o().optional().describe("@internal Additional system prompt appended to every Task-tool subagent (and propagated to nested subagents). Gated by CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_APPEND_SUBAGENT_PROMPT."),toolAliases:fe(o(),o()).optional().describe("Map of tool-name aliases applied before name resolution. When the model emits a tool_use whose name is a key in this map, the tool execution path resolves the mapped name instead. Single-hop (no chains). See Options.toolAliases."),codeRunningMcpTools:fe(o(),C(o())).optional().describe("@internal MCP tools that run code the model writes, keyed by server name as registered, each with its tool names. In auto mode no allow rule, skill, hook or plugin approves a call to one on its own: the classifier judges it, unless set_mcp_permission_mode_override pins its server to default. Declaring any also stops every PermissionRequest hook allow from changing the permission mode (its setMode update is dropped). Give names without the mcp__ prefix: a server or tool name that starts with mcp__ is skipped as malformed. Read from the first initialize only; its response lists what was declared in code_running_mcp_tools_applied. See Options.codeRunningMcpTools."),excludeDynamicSections:M().optional().describe("When true, omit per-user dynamic sections (working directory, auto-memory path) from the cached system prompt and re-inject them as the first user message. Lets cross-user prompt caching hit on a static system prompt prefix. Tradeoff: the model sees this context slightly later in the prompt, so steering on the working directory and memory location is marginally less authoritative. Has no effect when a custom (non-preset) system prompt is in use."),agents:fe(o(),Ey()).optional(),title:o().optional().describe("Custom session title. When provided, the session uses this title and skips automatic title generation. Has no effect on the persisted title when resuming an existing session."),skills:C(o()).optional().describe('When provided, only skills whose names match an entry are loaded into the main session system prompt, matching the exact canonical name (e.g. "my-plugin:my-skill") or a ":name" suffix of it. Display names and aliases do not match. Omit to load every discovered skill. Applies to the main session only; subagents use AgentDefinition.skills, which additionally resolves display names and aliases.'),webSearchIsolationExemptMcpServers:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Additional MCP server names exempt from the web search / connector isolation latch. Unioned with the built-in infra-server list."),promptSuggestions:M().optional(),agentProgressSummaries:M().optional(),forwardSubagentText:M().optional(),supportedDialogKinds:C(o()).optional().describe("Dialog kinds (request_user_dialog `dialog_kind` values) this consumer's onUserDialog can actually render. The CLI treats ABSENCE as 'cannot display' and fails closed: without the kind declared here, a dialog-gated flow degrades to its no-dialog behavior (for 'refusal_fallback_prompt', the classic refusal error) instead of parking a dialog the consumer may mishandle. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),rendersChromeSetupOffer:M().optional().describe("@internal A client capability: this consumer renders the OfferChromeSetup tool's permission request as a Claude in Chrome setup card and answers it with updatedInput.outcome 'connected' or 'not_now'. It lets that tool join the tool list once a Claude in Chrome tool call has reported the browser extension is not connected and the session's experiment flag is on. On, never off: a later initialize may be another client's; absence leaves it as it was. Kept across worker restarts."),perTaskStopAffordance:M().optional().describe("Declares that this consumer renders a per-task stop control wired to the `stop_task` control request, so the user can stop an individual background task. When declared, an interrupt on an open-input (interactive stream-json) session spares running background agents/workflows (Stop only aborts the turn). Closed-input exception: a one-shot run (string prompt / -p closes stdin) still kills hold-back tasks at the held-result release regardless of the declaration \u2014 with stdin closed, a stop_task control could never be delivered, so the fail-closed kill stands. ABSENCE also fails closed: the interrupt kills background tasks, since the user would otherwise have no way to stop a runaway one. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),forwardNestedSubagentToolCalls:M().optional().describe("@internal Asks Claude Code to also send the tool_use and tool_result messages of sub-agents that other sub-agents start, and of skills a sub-agent forks, each with `parent_tool_use_id` set to the call that started it. That call is itself a tool_use in the messages of the parent sub-agent, so a consumer can place every sub-agent under the one that started it and list its tool calls. Their text and thinking stay unsent unless `forwardSubagentText` is set, which already sends all of it. ABSENCE sends none of them, as before. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),rapidFollowupPreempt:M().optional().describe("@internal Declares that this consumer renders the `system/turn_preempted` frame and expects Claude Code to stop a turn on its own: when a person's follow-up message arrives while the running turn has shown nothing yet, the turn is stopped as an interrupt would stop it (its messages get `command_lifecycle` 'cancelled' and its result is an aborted one) and the follow-up runs next, announced by `turn_preempted`. Honored only while the rollout flag is on. ABSENCE fails closed: the follow-up waits its turn exactly as before, because a consumer that does not know the frame would see a message it never withdrew reported cancelled. Messages arriving over the Remote Control bridge never stop a turn, whatever was declared here. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),plugins:C(lZe()).optional().describe("Plugins to load for the session, in the same shape as the SDK `plugins` option: the stdin form of one --plugin-dir flag per entry (--plugin-dir-no-mcp when skipMcpDiscovery is set), so the launch command line does not grow with the plugin count. Loaded only by a CLI launched with --await-initialize, which reads this request during startup before any plugin work. Without that flag, on a repeated initialize, or over a remote session transport the field loads nothing; plugins_applied in the response reports whether the listed plugins are in fact loaded."),workspaceTrust:u({accepted:M().describe("Only send true after showing the user a trust dialog for `directory` \u2014 the same attestation set_cwd carries as trust_accepted."),directory:o().describe("Absolute path of the directory the user accepted, as it exists on the machine the CLI runs on. Honored only when it resolves to the trust key of the session working directory (the canonical git root when inside a repository, so a repository root and its linked worktrees agree); a network or obfuscated spelling, a path that is not a directory here, or one that resolves elsewhere records nothing. The grant covers that whole repository, exactly as the terminal trust dialog and set_cwd do, so a host whose folder sits inside a repository should name the repository root in its dialog.")}).optional().describe("@internal Host attestation that the user explicitly accepted a trust dialog for the session's working directory before launching it \u2014 the launch-time twin of set_cwd's trust_accepted/trusted_directory, for hosts whose dialog runs before the CLI is spawned and possibly on another machine than the ~/.claude.json this process reads (Claude Desktop's Code tab over SSH or WSL). Honored once, on the session's first initialize from the process that owns the CLI's stdin: the CLI records the working directory as trusted in the same store the interactive trust dialog writes (the home directory is trusted for the session only and never persisted), so the persisted-trust gates read the same answer they do in a checkout the user trusted at a terminal \u2014 project permission rules and hooks re-derive in the running process, the open-change announcement that follows this initialize already sees it, and readers evaluated at launch (project .mcp.json approval, project plugins, agent-frontmatter hooks and MCP servers) see it from the next launch on. A re-sent initialize, one arriving over a remote session transport, or one sent to a parked spare (--await-claim; send workspace_trust with claim_session instead) records nothing. workspace_trust_recorded in the response reports the outcome."),attachServeRequest:u({workId:o(),environmentId:o()}).optional().describe("@internal Claude Desktop's serve-only helper launch (`--attach-serve`) only: the tool-use work item the helper was started for and the served folder's environment id, exactly as the host polled them. Read from the opening `initialize` on the helper's own stdin and nowhere else; the helper signs its link to the session over both. Every other launch ignores it.")}).describe("Initializes the SDK session with hooks, MCP servers, and agent configuration.")),_W=f(()=>u({minTimeBeforeFeedbackMs:E(),minTimeBetweenFeedbackMs:E(),minTimeBetweenGlobalFeedbackMs:E(),minUserTurnsBeforeFeedback:E(),minUserTurnsBetweenFeedback:E(),hideThanksAfterMs:E(),onForModels:C(o()),probability:E(),lastSurveyShownTime:E().nullable()}).describe("@internal Session feedback-survey configuration for host UIs (VS Code webview, Claude Desktop) that run the survey trigger logic themselves: the same GrowthBook-driven pacing/probability values the terminal survey uses, plus the cross-surface last-shown time the host can't read. Survey responses are proxied back as tengu_feedback_survey_event log_event notifications.")),gW=f(()=>u({kind:F(["post","bundle","share","disabled"]).describe("How a submit_feedback report from this session is handled: 'post' sends it to Anthropic; 'bundle' writes it, redacted, to a zip on this machine for the user to pass on (a third-party provider or gateway, or no Anthropic credentials) and the response carries its path; 'share' is the internal-build upload; 'disabled' refuses it for a static cause (an environment variable or the nonessential-traffic setting). An org-policy refusal is not reported here: it is read late and can flip, and the per-turn system/init product_feedback_disabled carries it.")}).describe("@internal The resolved feedback mode (getFeedbackMode) for host UIs that offer a feedback dialog, so they can say up front that a report is refused or saved on this machine, instead of learning either only after the user has written and sent it.")),fW=f(()=>u({commands:C(Ft()),agents:C(mo()),output_style:o(),available_output_styles:C(o()),user_output_styles_dir:o().optional().describe("@internal Absolute path of the user-level output-styles directory this CLI lists styles from: the output-styles folder of the config home as the CLI resolves it after the settings env blocks are applied, so a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set in user or managed settings is honored. A host that writes a user-level style file writes it here; absent on older CLIs, where the host falls back to its own reading of CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR."),models:C(un()),unavailable_models:C(un()).optional().describe("@internal Models the account can see but not select (disabled: true, reason folded into description \u2014 e.g. a model the org's Zero Data Retention setting excludes). Disjoint from `models`, which stays selectable-only so consumers without disabled rendering are unaffected. Populated only for allowlisted 1P hosts that render these rows (currently the VS Code extension \u2014 UNAVAILABLE_MODELS_HOST_ENTRYPOINTS); empty for every other consumer. Omitted when empty."),account:by(),current_model:o().optional().describe("@internal The CLI's active model at connect time. Remote Control clients (web/mobile) sync their model dropdown TO this value on connect instead of sending set_model with their own default \u2014 without it, connecting from a phone silently switches the terminal's model (CC-2659)."),current_permission_mode:de().optional().describe("@internal The CLI's active permission mode at connect time, for the same connect-time sync as current_model."),inbound_read_receipts:M().optional().describe("@internal Remote Control hosts only: true when this host reports a client message read when it leaves the command queue for a turn \u2014 the message's `processing` delivery status \u2014 and finished when the turn ends (`processed`). Absent (an older CLI, or a host that acks at receipt): a delivery status means the host received the message, not that it read it. Read per initialize; the kill switch can turn it off for a session already answered."),session_state:F(["idle","running","requires_action"]).optional().describe("@internal Session state at connect time (same values as the session_state_changed message). Lets a host that attaches to an already-running CLI tell whether a turn is in progress without having seen the transition. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 unknown."),hooks_applied:M().optional().describe("Whether the `hooks` this initialize carried were registered: true on a session's first initialize, and on a repeated initialize from the process that owns the CLI's stdin (its set replaces the one registered earlier); false when a repeated initialize's hooks were ignored (a client joining a remote session another client configured). Absent when the request carried no hooks, and on CLIs that predate the field \u2014 those ignored `hooks` on every repeated initialize."),code_running_mcp_tools_applied:fe(o(),C(o())).optional().describe("@internal The MCP tools the first initialize's codeRunningMcpTools declared, keyed by server name with tool names as sent, each once. Names are not checked against the registered servers, so a misspelled one is listed but covers nothing. Malformed entries, and servers left with no tool, are omitted. Absent when that initialize carried no codeRunningMcpTools, on a repeated initialize, and on older CLIs: treat a tool as declared only when it is listed here."),plugins_applied:M().optional().describe("Whether every plugin this initialize listed is loaded: true when each one is among the plugins the process loaded at launch (from `plugins` under --await-initialize, or from --plugin-dir), so a re-sent initialize naming the launch set also reads true; false otherwise. The `plugins` field never loads anything after launch. Absent when the request listed no plugins, and on CLIs that predate the field (those never read `plugins`)."),host_worded_asks:M().optional().describe("@internal Desktop host only: true when this session reads permissionDisplay on a PreToolUse hook's ask (coreSchemas.ts HookPermissionDisplaySchema) and sends its asks to the host as can_use_tool requests. Absent otherwise. Send permissionDisplay only when this is true, and check each can_use_tool request for the words: the CLI may send an ask without them."),workspace_trust_recorded:M().optional().describe("@internal Outcome of this initialize's `workspaceTrust` attestation: true when the attested directory resolved to the session working directory's trust key and that key is now persisted as trusted (or already was); false when nothing durable was recorded \u2014 the directory was a network or obfuscated spelling, is not a directory on this machine, resolved to another key, the grant could not be written durably, or the working directory is the home directory (trusted for this session only); false too, whatever is already persisted, when the directory, the working directory or that key carries an invisible or format character (refused as set_cwd refuses such a spelling). Absent when the request carried no accepted attestation, on a repeated or remote-transport initialize, on a parked spare's initialize (--await-claim), and on CLIs that predate the field."),sdk_mcp_manifests_parked:fe(o(),F(["parked","already_connected","protocol_version_mismatch","malformed","not_honoured"])).optional().describe("What became of each entry of this initialize's sdkMcpServerManifests, keyed by sdk server name, for the entries whose name is in sdkMcpServers. 'parked': kept for the connect that follows this reply, which answers the server's MCP initialize and first tools/list from it (a later failure to replay shows up as live mcp_message frames, as when no entry was sent). 'already_connected': this CLI already had a live client for the server. 'protocol_version_mismatch': initializeResult.protocolVersion is not the version this CLI's client requests. 'malformed': the entry is not of the documented shape, reported whether or not manifests were honoured (every name in sdkMcpServers gets this when the field itself is not an object); or an honoured entry is past the limits this CLI sets on a live answer. 'not_honoured': the CLI did not use the entry: manifests or remote manifests are switched off in this CLI, the session service has not told this remote-session worker in the last five minutes that only the account owning the session can send events to it, or the request reached such a worker other than over its session stream (nothing for the host to change in any of these cases); or this CLI had read as many tool lists as it allows in a minute (send it again later); or it came over the session stream without sdkMcpServerManifestsOrigin or on a frame too old to trust (the host's to fix). Absent when the request carried no sdkMcpServerManifests or named no sdkMcpServers, and on CLIs that predate the field."),permission_mode_from_default_fallback:M().optional().describe('@internal True when current_permission_mode is auto because no settings source configured a mode and the gated auto default applied. IDE hosts only; drives the one-time "auto mode is now the default" disclosure.'),auto_default_nudge:de().optional().describe("@internal The user-settings defaultMode the one-shot auto-default nudge should offer to replace with auto. IDE hosts only; absent when the user isn't eligible."),pid:E().optional().describe("@internal CLI process PID for tmux socket isolation"),claude_code_version:o().optional().describe("Claude Code version of the process that runs this session's turns (the same value its system/init carries). Absent on CLIs that predate the field, and when the responder is not that process and does not know its version."),fast_mode_state:$t().optional(),fast_mode_disabled_reason:Xt().optional(),footer_indicator:Co().optional().describe(`@internal The terminal's configured "\u25C6 <text>" footer pill at connect time \u2014 see SDKFooterIndicator. Absent when nothing is configured.`),feedback_survey_config:_W().optional().describe("@internal Present only when the feedback-survey surface is enabled for this host (GrowthBook gate, privacy level, and org policy all allow it). Absent means the host must not show the survey."),feedback_mode:gW().optional().describe("@internal How this session handles a submit_feedback report (see SDKFeedbackMode), read once at init like the other host gates here; the org-policy half of 'disabled' stays on the per-turn system/init product_feedback_disabled boolean. Absent on an older CLI, and when the only refusal is the org policy \u2192 the host behaves as it does without the field."),analytics_disabled:M().optional().describe("@internal True when the CLI has analytics/telemetry disabled for this session (privacy level, DISABLE_TELEMETRY / DO_NOT_TRACK from any settings tier, third-party provider, gateway) \u2014 the same verdict the system/init message carries. IDE hosts fold it into the gate for their own direct telemetry. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 unknown."),remote_control_auto_enable:M().optional().describe("@internal Whether the CLI resolver says Remote Control should auto-enable at session start (explicit setting \u2192 policy default \u2192 GB rollout), so IDE hosts can mirror TUI behavior. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 treat as false."),remote_control_auto_on_by_default:M().optional().describe("@internal True when remote_control_auto_enable is true because of the org/GB default rather than an explicit remoteControlAtStartup setting \u2014 mirrors replBridgeAutoOnByDefault so IDE hosts can render the same disclosure notice."),remote_control_auto_connect_default:M().optional().describe('@internal The org/GB auto-connect default alone (getCcrAutoConnectDefault, under the same remote-environment guard as remote_control_auto_enable), with every explicit copy of remoteControlAtStartup left out. IDE hosts show it for their toggle only once they have seen the settings key cleared while the session runs (/config "default" clears the legacy ~/.claude.json copy in the same step); at rest they show remote_control_auto_enable. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 hosts keep their previous display behavior.'),claude_in_chrome_at_startup:M().optional().describe(`@internal Whether an IDE host that connects Claude in Chrome itself should connect it when the session starts. True when the "Enabled by default" setting (claudeInChromeDefaultEnabled) is on, this session did not connect the server itself, and the gates the terminal applies to the setting at startup pass (claudeInChromeDefaultForHost). Sent only to a host that owns the session's startup (isVscodeHostOwnedStartupSession). Absent (older CLI, other hosts) \u2192 treat as false.`),remote_control_available:M().optional().describe("@internal Whether Remote Control can be offered at all in this deployment (isRemoteControlDeploymentAvailable: not hard-disabled by managed settings, not nested in a remote environment, first-party provider). Only host-lifetime-stable conditions; transient ones (auth state, the GrowthBook rollout gate, the async org-compliance verdict) are deliberately excluded since hosts latch this from one initialize response \u2014 see the helper docstring. Lets IDE hosts hide their Remote Control affordance where it can never work. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 treat as available."),ide_rc_auto_enable_gate:M().optional().describe("@internal IDE-side rollout kill-switch for RC auto-enable (tengu_ide_rc_auto_enable), independent of remote_control_auto_enable. Carried on the init response (not experimentGates) because the host reads it at init time, before the first-prompt-triggered gate refresh. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 treat as false."),capabilities:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Members of system/init.capabilities that are already settled at connect time, said here so a host need not wait for the first turn to read them. The same literals under the same conditions; a subset, so a member missing here may still arrive on init. Absent when there is none to say, and on older CLIs.")}).describe("Response from session initialization with available commands, models, and account info.")),Io=f(()=>u({subtype:T("interrupt"),reason:o().optional().describe("@internal Why the turn was interrupted, forwarded to the turn's AbortSignal.reason. Tool implementations branch on it to distinguish a user-driven cancel (which suppresses error output) from other aborts. Known values: `interrupt` (user Esc/Ctrl+C), `user-cancel`, `remote-cancel`, `consumer-error`, `workflow-abort`, `stalled`, `recovery-timeout`. Open set \u2014 consumers must treat unknown values as a generic abort."),cancel_queued:M().optional().describe("When true, the interrupt also cancels every uuid-stamped main-thread command still in the queue or already dequeued for the imminent turn but not yet reachable by the abort (the first-command prewait window) \u2014 the same set the response would otherwise list under `still_queued`. Each is closed with a terminal 'cancelled' lifecycle and listed on the response's `cancelled` field. `still_queued` is then empty, except that a client driving a hosted session lists there what it can no longer recall (a send already in flight to that session, or the first prompt the session was created with) and, when the session's own sweep then cancels one of those or a send it had already delivered, follows up with a command_lifecycle 'cancelled' frame for it. (The isFoldInFlight guard cancel_async_message uses does not apply here: this request also aborts the running turn, so a fold-in-flight uuid is never delivered and is swept with the rest. A fold-in-flight uuid's queued_command attachment may already appear in the aborted turn's transcript if the abort landed after the fold's attachment yield \u2014 pre-existing leave-queued semantics; it never runs as its own turn.) Uuid-less commands (task notifications) still in the queue are also dequeued but cannot be listed; a uuid-less command already in the prewait window is unreachable by either cancel leg \u2014 the first-command prewait latch covers it (this request latches exactly like a plain interrupt; see still_queued): its turn starts aborted. When false or absent, queued commands survive the interrupt and are listed under `still_queued` \u2014 the interrupt_receipt_v1 contract is unchanged. A Stop-means-stop-everything client (a remote UI's Stop button) sets this true so one round-trip halts the session; a wrapper that wants per-uuid control leaves it false and follows up with cancel_async_message. Advertised by the `interrupt_cancel_queued_v1` capability on system/init; older CLIs ignore the field and behave as if false."),send_now:M().optional().describe("@internal True when the request is a client's Send now on a message that is waiting, not its Stop: it asks that the waiting messages reach the model now, as the terminal's send-now key does. It is not a Stop: it makes no later turn start aborted, cancels nothing that is queued, and ends no background task, subscription or other thing outside the running turn that a Stop ends. What it does depends on what the CLI holds when it reads the request, and the response's send_now says which. Without message_uuid every waiting user message counts, which may be another than the one pressed. With no user message waiting at the CLI it does nothing: a message the running turn has already taken in, or was started for, is being answered. With one waiting that is not in the queue yet (received and still being admitted, or already taken for the next turn), that the running turn is taking in at that moment, or with no turn under way, it also does nothing: the message runs when its place comes. With a person's message in the queue behind a turn under way, the CLI chooses how that turn makes way: it moves what the turn waits on to the background so that the turn takes the message in (and, if that fails, stops the turn later as a priority 'now' message does, without a marker), or it aborts that turn at once as a plain interrupt does (running tools are ended, the interruption marker is written, the result is an error_during_execution); what was queued ahead of the message still runs before it. Either way a sender learns that the turn has ended from the turn's result, not from this response. The message must already be at the CLI, or the request finds nothing waiting: a sender that still holds it delivers it first, or sends it with priority 'now', or sends a plain interrupt instead. Only the boolean true counts, and with cancel_queued:true the request is a Stop. Advertised by interrupt_send_now_v1 on system/init; a CLI without it handles the request as a plain interrupt."),message_uuid:o().optional().describe("@internal Read only beside send_now:true: the uuid of the user message that Send now was pressed on, in the very form in which that message reached the CLI (a session service may deliver it in lower case): it is compared as an exact string, as cancel_async_message compares. With it only that message counts as waiting. If the running turn has already taken it in or was started for it, or the CLI does not know it or does not count it as a person's, the request does nothing and answers 'nothing_waiting', whatever else waits. Once the request acts, it acts as without the name: everything queued decides between the move to the background and the abort, and goes along with the named message. An empty string is read as no name, and so is a null, which this schema does not admit but an encoder may write; any other value that is no string names nothing."),for_user_message_uuids:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal One to eight uuids (case ignored) of user messages whose turn this Stop is for; any other value is read as absent. The current turn is the latest one dequeued for a client's message (it may be over, or not yet started), with every turn started and message taken in since. A Stop that names one of its messages is handled as a plain interrupt (if latched, see still_queued, only for pending work that it names or that answers no client's message), and so is any Stop while the CLI knows of no current turn. Other Stops do nothing and get turn: 'spared', except a few that name queued work (sparesCurrentTurn). Always include the message that opened the turn (user_message_uuid on its first frames, before any re-run): after a restart the CLI knows no other. A turn handed over by turn_handoff has no name: any Stop may stop it, so send a plain one. The uuids are only compared: never stored, logged, sent back or shown to the model. Not read beside cancel_queued:true or send_now:true, nor by older CLIs, which answer without turn."),worker_epoch:E().int().min(1).optional().describe("@internal The worker life (system/init's worker_epoch) that this interrupt is for, as a JSON number. Written only on an interrupt that a service sends by itself and that must not outlive the worker it was meant for. Never written on a person's Stop, not even as a null or a 0 for 'unset': a Stop must hold across a restart. Read by a claude-code-server tenant alone, before every other member and whatever they say. A worker of another life takes none of an interrupt's steps (it still counts as a person's presence, as every interrupt does), marks the request processed, so that no later worker is served it, and answers with a stale_worker_epoch error; take the new number from the next system/init, not from the error's text. Any other value that is present (a null, a 0, a fraction, a string such as '7') is refused the same way with an invalid_worker_epoch error, never read as absent. Both errors carry the token as error_code and list no still_queued: they are no receipt. The worker of the stated life, every other host that handles an interrupt, and an older tenant handle the request as if the member were absent, and no capability says which a worker is; a client that relays its host's Stop to a hosted session drops the member.")}).describe("Interrupts the currently running conversation turn.")),bW=f(()=>u({still_queued:C(o()).describe("Uuids of async user messages that survive this interrupt: commands still in the queue, plus any batch already dequeued for the imminent turn but not yet reachable by the abort. An interrupt \u2014 plain or cancel_queued:true \u2014 that lands during the FIRST-command prewait window (before the first turn of the session has armed a controller) is additionally LATCHED, scoped to the user-intent work pending at that instant \u2014 the batch already dequeued and parked for the imminent turn, plus the user-intent main-thread commands then in the queue (the work this list enumerates): the first turn to arm that carries any of that doomed work starts already aborted, exactly once, so the listed prewait batch is delivered into an immediately-aborted turn, its frames and result flowing through the normal abort path, instead of running to completion. A turn carrying none of it arms live and leaves the latch waiting: a system delivery turn (for example a replayed host event), or a prompt enqueued after the interrupt \u2014 post-interrupt work is never coalesced with the doomed work and never dies to the latch, so the Stop kills exactly what this receipt listed. The latch is released when the doomed work is retired without arming: if a parked prewait batch is entirely cancelled, the latch is released even when other queued commands remain (those arm and run normally); with nothing parked, it is released once none of the doomed commands remains queued \u2014 work enqueued after the interrupt neither holds it up nor is aborted by it. Survivors that ride any later turn run normally. These WILL run (subject to that latch) unless cancelled first (or unless the request set cancel_queued:true, in which case every uuid-stamped survivor this process holds is removed, emitted a terminal `cancelled` synchronously, and listed under `cancelled` instead \u2014 leaving here only what a client driving a hosted session can no longer recall: a send already in flight to that session, or the first prompt the session was created with; a send that client still holds on its own machine behind a send gate (it waits there until that session is ready to take it) has not gone out, so it is withdrawn and listed under `cancelled` like a queued one, and cancel_async_message can withdraw it too, while a plain interrupt leaves it held and lists it here). Cancellation granularity: uuids still in the queue are individually cancellable via cancel_async_message (the re-run of an interrupted turn that also answers for other client messages only if this interrupt was for it; see the response to that request); once a batch is dequeued and coalesced into one turn, cancelling a NON-representative member uuid is a no-op (its content still runs), while cancelling the batch-representative uuid drops the WHOLE coalesced batch \u2014 in both cases the cancel response reports cancelled:false because the message was no longer in the queue. Coverage caveats: only uuid-STAMPED messages appear (a message enqueued without a uuid still runs but is never listed, so [] does not mean \"nothing will run\"); only main-thread messages are listed (subagent-addressed messages are out of scope); and the list may include internally-enqueued uuids the client never sent (cron triggers, auto-resume continuations) \u2014 ignore unknown uuids rather than treating them as an error. Ordering: on a clean interrupt this receipt is written before the interrupted turn result; a turn that crashes during interrupt handling emits its error result on a direct-write path that may precede the receipt. Snapshot is taken synchronously with abort processing \u2014 probing the queue after the interrupted result instead always loses the race against the drain loop, which starts the next queued turn immediately."),send_now:F(["stopped","delivering","nothing_waiting"]).optional().describe("@internal Present only when a CLI that advertises interrupt_send_now_v1 itself answers a send_now (a client that relays to a hosted session answers without it). 'nothing_waiting': no user message waited (with message_uuid, the named one did not), and nothing was done. 'delivering': one waits and this request stopped no turn, for one of these reasons: no turn is under way and the message runs when its place comes; the message is not in the queue yet, or the running turn is just taking it in; the CLI is moving what the turn waits on to the background, and may still stop the turn later if that fails; the turn is already being stopped; or the process is on its way out, and the message will not run in it. 'stopped': the running turn was aborted for what waits, and its result follows."),turn:F(["stopped","idle","spared"]).optional().describe("@internal Present only when the answering CLI read the request's for_user_message_uuids. 'stopped': handled; the turn under way was aborted, or the interrupt was latched for pending work. 'idle': handled; no turn was under way and it latched for nothing. 'spared': it named no message of the current turn, so nothing was done. A named message listed in still_queued still waits, whatever the value."),cancelled:C(o()).optional().describe("Present only when the request set cancel_queued:true \u2014 uuids of main-thread commands cancelled by this interrupt: every survivor that would otherwise have appeared under `still_queued`, including any uuid that was mid-fold at the interrupt instant (this request also aborts, so the fold never delivers it). Each listed uuid has been removed (queue-resident) or marked cancel-pending (the first-command prewait window, closed by the drain loop's backstop) and emits a terminal 'cancelled' lifecycle synchronously at the first such interrupt (a repeat interrupt over the same parked batch re-lists the uuid idempotently without re-emitting); none will run. Where one of them is the re-run of an interrupted turn that also answered for other client messages whose re-sent copies had been set aside for it, those messages' uuids are listed behind the commands' too: they were in no queue and never under `still_queued`, each has its terminal 'cancelled' lifecycle, and a repeat interrupt does not list them again. Same coverage caveats as `still_queued` (uuid-stamped main-thread only; internally-enqueued uuids may appear). Advertised by the `interrupt_cancel_queued_v1` capability.")}).describe("Result of an interrupt operation. Advertised by the interrupt_receipt_v1 capability on system/init; older CLIs send an empty success response with no still_queued field.")),Po=f(()=>u({subtype:T("can_use_tool"),tool_name:o(),mcp_server:He().optional(),input:fe(o(),oe()),permission_suggestions:C(Kt()).optional(),blocked_path:o().optional(),decision_reason:o().optional().describe(`Human-readable reason the ask escalated, for the consent line of the host's dialog. For decision_reason_type "subcommandResults" (compound bash), this is the NESTED safety check's warning text \u2014 the wrapper itself has no text \u2014 preferring a check that requires manual approval (classifier_approvable false); treat it with the same display/policy care as a "safetyCheck" reason. May carry ANSI escapes; sanitize before rendering.`),decision_reason_type:F(Iwt).optional().describe(`Structured discriminator for why auto-mode escalated. Lets SDK hosts make policy (e.g. auto-deny safetyCheck) without parsing decision_reason text. For compound bash commands this is "subcommandResults" even when a safetyCheck is nested inside \u2014 check classifier_approvable for that case, and see decision_reason: for this variant it carries the nested safety check's warning text.`),decision_reason_code:F([...eer,"org_ask_ceiling"]).optional().describe("@internal A closed-set code for a reason a host can act on, beside decision_reason_type (whose values are unchanged, so type-keyed host policy decides as before): 'outside_reads_blocked', 'memory_paused', 'classifier_transcript_too_long', 'org_ask_ceiling' (the MCP tool's ceiling, toolPermissions, is 'ask', and an ask rule or the ceiling itself raised the ask: type 'rule' or 'other'). Absent for every other reason; new values are additive."),decision_reason_rule_source:o().optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'rule': the source of the rule in the decision reason (a PermissionRuleSource word). It names the first rule that matched, not the strongest, and says nothing of what that rule hides. For the host's logs: a host should not decide by it."),decision_reason_hook_event:F(["PreToolUse","tool.check"]).optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'hook': the event of the hook that asked. 'PreToolUse': the terminal's dialog names the hook 'PreToolUse:' followed by tool_name. 'tool.check': it names it 'tool.check'. A host can write the same, as plain text. Absent for any other hook. For display only: never answer or hide a prompt because of it."),decision_reason_hook_source:o().optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'hook': where the hook that asked comes from: 'plugin:<name>', 'plugin', 'skill:<name>', 'skill', or 'settings' for any other hook, such as one the SDK host registered. This CLI sends and passes on no other shape. It is absent when the CLI does not know it, when several plugins' check hooks asked together, or when the name is not 1 to 64 ASCII letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-' that start with a letter or digit. When several hooks ask on one call it is absent, or names the one whose reason decision_reason carries. The name is chosen by the plugin's or skill's author: joined words can spell a message, and it can look like a domain. Set it apart as a name, in quotation marks or in brackets as the terminal's label does. Show it in plain text, not through Markdown or an autolinker, and never as your own words. Untrusted, for display only: check and escape it, never put it in a URL, and never answer or hide a prompt because of it."),decision_reason_hook_plugin_id:o().optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'hook', for one plugin's hook: the plugin's id: its name, an at sign, then its marketplace, each a name as in decision_reason_hook_source. It can look like an e-mail address. The marketplace is a real one or a reserved word for how the plugin was loaded, such as 'synced' (delivered from claude.ai: on for the person's account, or attached to the session by its configuration), 'inline' (--plugin-dir), 'skills-dir' (.claude/skills/) or 'builtin'. Same care as decision_reason_hook_source; either may be sent without the other."),server_policy_ask_only:M().optional().describe("@internal Sent with decision_reason_type 'rule', and for an MCP tool's ask that has none. true = when the frame was built, nothing asked for a person on this call but the default prompt for a tool that no rule allows and, at most, a tool policy that the host itself pushed (permission_policy 'always_ask'): no other ask rule that the permission check applies to the call, no hook's or plugin's ask, no PermissionRequest hook, no organization ceiling, no plan-mode floor, no safety check, no ask or veto on a standing grant of the tool's own, no auto-mode classifier active for the call. false = something else did, or may have. It says nothing of WHY the host pushed that policy, which only the host knows; nothing of whose server the tool is (see mcp_server), so a host answers only for a tool that it holds a person's grant for; and nothing of what changes after the frame is sent. Absent on other reason types, on another tool's ask without a reason type, wherever a request is listed, mirrored or passed on again (pending_permission_requests), and from older versions."),classifier_approvable:M().optional().describe("Set when a safetyCheck is present anywhere in the decision reason (including nested inside subcommandResults for compound bash). false = at least one safety check requires manual approval (e.g. Windows path bypass, dangerous rm); true = all safety checks MAY be classifier-approved (e.g. sensitive-file paths). Absent when no safetyCheck is involved."),suppress_always_allow_rule:M().optional().describe(`True when the dialog must not offer the persistent "don't ask again" row for this ask: accepting it would write a whole-tool allow rule broader than the ask's own verb (PermissionAskDecision.suppressAlwaysAllowRule). Hosts rendering approve options should omit any persistent-rule affordance when set.`),default_to_no:M().optional().describe("True when the ask must not be approvable by a single stray keystroke (PermissionAskDecision.defaultToNo): a terminal-style prompt opens on its decline option and takes no digit shortcut. Hosts rendering approve options should not pre-select approve when set."),matched_ask_rule:u({source:o(),tool_name:o(),rule_content:o().optional()}).optional().describe("Set when a user-configured ask RULE (permissions.ask) forced this prompt but the ask carries the tool's own decision_reason \u2014 the ask-rule substitution keeps the richer tool-minted ask, so the rule rides here instead of decision_reason_type 'rule'. Hosts making policy on decision_reason_type (e.g. auto-deny safetyCheck) or running host-side auto-approval should treat asks carrying this field as rule-forced: the user's stated intent is a human prompt. Values are producer-authored but render-unsafe like decision_reason; sanitize before display."),title:o().optional(),display_name:o().optional(),tool_use_id:o(),agent_id:o().optional(),plugin:o().optional().describe("@internal The plugin whose `$` call (`$.ui.ask`, `$.tool.call`, `$.agent.spawn`) started the run this ask belongs to, by the name it is loaded under: who the terminal's dialog says the ask is from. The engine's word, which no plugin sets. Empty on a run resumed from an agent a plugin steered, whose name was not kept (the terminal says `from a plugin`). Absent for the model's own calls. Untrusted text; escape before display."),description:o().optional(),requires_user_interaction:M().optional().describe("True when one-tap Approve/Deny must not be offered: the tool's approval card IS the user-interaction surface (Tool.requiresUserInteraction(): the user responds on the card itself), OR the pending ask is localDisplayOnly (its consent disclosure cannot ride this wire and only the local dialog renders it), OR the auto-mode classifier blocked the call and it was handed to a person (decision_reason_type 'classifier' on a person-only ask), which no stored grant or automatic approval may answer, OR the SDK host worded the ask (title and description are its consent copy, which a push cannot carry whole). In each case the user has to open the session to answer."),server_prompt:o().optional().describe("@internal A marker the session service puts on an ask it worded itself, naming which of its prompts this is (an open set). Nothing on this frame proves who wrote it: the marker is the service's word only on a stream served by the service, which removes the key from anything a session's own container wrote; anywhere else it is a claim. A host keeps showing the tool name and input whatever it says."),computer_folder:u({path:o(),computer_name:o()}).optional().describe("@internal Beside server_prompt, and as trustworthy as it is: a folder on one of the person's computers and that computer's name. Untrusted text; escape before display."),provenance_prompt_line:F(["one_at_a_time","all_at_once"]).optional().describe("@internal Only on the URL prompt that WebFetch raises mid-call, when the session's web-fetch proxy refuses an address. Says whether the session's line of such prompts was on for this call. 'one_at_a_time' = on: the call took a turn in the line, so no other prompt of that line was up when this one was raised. 'all_at_once' = off: the call joined no line, and its prompt was raised without waiting. For the host's logs: nothing in the CLI decides by it, and a host should not either."),tool_kind:F(["browser"]).optional().describe("@internal Structured tool category so SDK hosts can route to a specialized permission UI without name-matching tool_name. 'browser' = a Claude-in-Chrome MCP tool under any of its registered server names: the mcp__claude-in-chrome__* extension server, the mcp__Claude_in_Chrome__* Cowork desktop server, the Claude Desktop pane under mcp__Claude_Preview__* / mcp__Claude_Browser__* (both pane names are live during its rename rollout), or the mcp__remote-devices__* spellings bridged through Claude Code Remote (the bridged forms of the current names; the retiring Claude_Preview name has no bridged form). Open for future kinds.")}).describe("Requests permission to use a tool with the given input.")),Mo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_permission_mode"),mode:de(),ultraplan:M().optional().describe("@internal CCR ultraplan session marker."),from_settings:M().optional().describe("@internal The mode is the default of the creating person's settings, not a choice of the launch.")}).describe("Sets the permission mode for tool execution handling.")),EW=f(()=>u({mode:de().optional().describe("The permission mode now in effect. Headless CLIs echo it; other hosts may acknowledge with an empty object.")}).describe("Success payload answering set_permission_mode.")),Do=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_model"),model:o().nullable().optional().describe("Model to switch to. Omitted, null, or 'default' resets to the session default model."),system_prompt:o().min(1).optional().describe("@internal Replaces the custom system prompt (the --system-prompt / initialize systemPrompt slot). The session keeps sending the system prompt it has recorded, so the new text is first sent after the next compaction; under systemPromptSnapshot: false it is sent from the next turn on. The tool definitions already sent do not change. Applied only when the model request is accepted; must be non-empty (there is no revert-to-built-in form); re-send the current model for a prompt-only update; sending the text already in effect changes nothing. The CLAUDE_CODE_SYSTEM_PROMPT_GB_FEATURE per-turn read, where configured, still wins. Transports that do not implement it, and older builds, ack success without applying it."),only_if_model_is_current:M().optional().describe("@internal When true, the request does not change the model. `model` must name a model: if it is absent, null, empty or 'default', the request fails with error_code 'invalid_request'. `system_prompt` is required: without it the request fails the same way. If `model` names the session's model, the request succeeds, and `system_prompt` replaces the custom system prompt as that member describes. The request makes no server check of the model, runs no PreModelSwitch hook, and sends no model report to the host. For a model id that this build knows, two names are one model if they resolve to one model id: an alias resolves to the id that it stands for, every dated id of a version resolves to the undated id, and a [1m] tag is ignored. An id that a behaves_as mapping points at another model is a different model. If the session's model is another model, the request fails with error_code 'model_not_current' and changes nothing. The request also fails that way while the session's records of its model disagree. A fallback that changes the session's model can cause that state until its turn ends, or longer. A session whose model setting is 'opusplan', 'opusplan[1m]' or 'haiku' runs a different model in plan mode. For such a session the request always fails with 'model_not_current'. A value that is not a boolean fails with 'invalid_request'. `claude -p` and a claude-code-server worker implement this member. Remote Control refuses every value but false. A build without this member ignores it and applies the request as a plain set_model.")}).describe("Sets the model to use for subsequent conversation turns.")),xo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_max_thinking_tokens"),max_thinking_tokens:E().int().nullable().optional(),thinking_display:F(iZe).nullable().optional()}).describe("Sets the maximum number of thinking tokens for extended thinking. When max_thinking_tokens is null, thinking resets to the session default: any mid-session budget override is cleared (back to the spawn-time budget, if one was set), and thinking stays off for sessions that have it disabled. When max_thinking_tokens is omitted, the budget is left as it is, so a request that only changes thinking_display can leave the field out. thinking_display optionally sets the thinking display mode for the rest of the session: a value replaces the session display mode, null clears that override so Claude Code's default display handling applies again, and when omitted the display mode from session start (--thinking-display) is kept. 'highlights' returns one short title per stretch of thinking instead of a prose summary. The API accepts it only from Claude Code sessions that Anthropic hosts; if the API rejects it, the session sends 'omitted' (no thinking text) in its place from then on. A request for 'highlights' gets an error reply after such a rejection, on Amazon Bedrock, Google Vertex AI or another provider to which Claude Code sends no first-party-only beta features, when experimental betas are off (CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_BETAS or organization policy), or on a Claude 3 model (except on Microsoft Foundry). The error names which of these it was; the display stays as it was, and max_thinking_tokens still applies when the request has it.")),Lo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("rename_session"),title:o(),source:F(["remote","host"]).optional().describe("Who chose the title: 'remote' (the default) for a rename made on claude.ai and relayed to this process, 'host' for one the user made in the hosting application (an IDE), which the CLI counts as a user rename."),session_id:o().optional().describe("The session the title is for. When given and this process has since moved to another session (/clear, an in-session resume), the request is refused instead of naming the new session.")}).describe("Sets the user-facing title for the current session.")),Uo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_color"),color:o()}).describe('Sets the session accent color. Accepts an agent color name or "default" to reset.')),Vo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("mcp_status")}).describe("Requests the current status of all MCP server connections.")),SW=f(()=>u({mcpServers:C(po())}).describe("Response containing the current status of all MCP server connections.")),jo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("file_suggestions"),query:o()}).describe("Requests at-mention file autocomplete suggestions for a partial path prefix. Returns the same fuzzy-matched results the TUI shows.")),TW=f(()=>u({suggestions:C(u(