ven: pass `-o` and `-Dmaven.repo.local=` with the full path of the user's `.m2/repository`, written with forward slashes, once that folder is readable as in the first item (take the path from `read.allowWithinDeny` above, or ask the user; `~` in a command is not the user's home). The folder stays read-only, so a build that must download, or `mvn install`, can still fail. .NET: in the sandbox, never pass `--no-restore` on a project restored outside it: the build can report success yet delete package DLLs from `bin`, and `dotnet test --no-build` then fails on a missing DLL, in the user's own terminal too, until a plain `dotnet test` there rebuilds it.",Kt='`mvn` from Git Bash can loop forever on `cd: ...: Permission denied` without starting Java. Kill it and run it as `MAVEN_BASEDIR="$PWD" mvn ...`.',Sn="The sandbox creates 0-byte placeholder files, such as `.mcp.json` and `.claude/settings.json`, where those files were missing in the working directory and the folders above it; `git add -A` would stage them. A 0-byte one belongs to the sandbox: do not stage, commit, move or delete it. If git says it is in the way, tell the user. Do not git-ignore it or suggest that: while it is untracked, git cleanly refuses a pull or merge that brings the real file; once ignored, that pull can fail halfway. One with content is the user's file.";dt.push("On Windows, sandboxed commands run as a separate account, not as the user: inside the sandbox `%USERPROFILE%` is that account's own home and `$HOME` is not the user's either. Other failures to recognize:",[...be==="relaxed"?[]:[y?`\`read.denyOnly\` above does not tell the whole story. Sandbox set-up normally makes the user's profile folder (usually \`C:\\Users\` plus the user's name) readable to sandboxed commands, except credential stores (such as \`.ssh\`, \`.aws\`, \`.claude\`, browser profiles and password managers), which set-up closes to them wherever their permissions let it; never read one from a sandboxed command, even where 