The session is bound to exactly one project (set by the harness when the session started). You never pass a project ID \u2014 every method operates on that project. There is no project discovery in this tool; if the user wants a different project, they restart the session.

Methods (dispatch on \`method\`):

- \`project_info\` \u2014 project name, description, custom instructions, doc list, file-upload list (PDFs, images), and knowledge-base stats. Call this first.
- \`project_read\` \u2014 read one doc or file upload by \`path\`. For a text doc or a document-kind file upload (PDF, docx), small text returns inline and large text is written to a local file whose path is returned (read it with the ${Qe} tool). Image and other non-document uploads (spreadsheets, binaries) are downloaded whole: the original bytes are written to a local file whose path is returned \u2014 open it with file-appropriate tooling.${r?` When a document-kind upload's text does not carry what matters (layout, figures, scanned pages), pass \`original: true\` to download it that way too; the ${Qe} tool shows a PDF's pages.`:""}
- \`project_search\` \u2014 query the project's knowledge base. Returns RAG hits with snippets and source paths. Prefer this over reading every doc when answering a question about the project.
- \`project_write\` \u2014 create or replace a doc. ${{here:"Pass `path` plus exactly one of `content` (inline text) or `local_path` (a file inside the working directory; the tool reads, encodes, and uploads it directly so its contents never enter your context \u2014 use this for anything you have on disk).",elsewhere:`Pass \`path\` plus exactly one of \`content\` (inline text) or \`local_path\` (in the main conversation only: the absolute path of a text file of at most 1 MiB that you have read or written in full in this session and that has not changed since, under ${kMe}, under ${jln}, or under the working directory (not if the working directory is your home directory); the tool uploads it directly, so you need not write its text out again; for any other file, pass \`content\`).`,none:"Pass `path` and `content` (inline text); `local_path` is not available in this session, so put the text in `content` even for a file you have."}[e]} Writing to a path that already exists replaces it in place.${s?" Other people and other sessions write to the same docs. Every `project_read` and `project_write` of a text doc returns its `version`: when you replace a doc, pass the `version` that came with the copy you worked from as `if_version`. If the doc has changed since, the write is refused and changes nothing; read the doc again, make your change in that text, and write with the new `version`. Copy the value exactly and never invent one. Omit `if_version` only for a new doc, or when a refusal tells you to.":""} Writing a *new* bare filename defaults into the \`claude/\` namespace (\`project_write("notes.md")\` \u2192 \`claude/notes.md\`) so agent-written docs are distinguishable from user uploads; pass an explicit nested path to override. Set \`present_to_user: true\` only when the doc is the file the user needs to see \u2014 the deliverable they asked for or must act on; leave it unset (default false) for routine saves, notes, and bulk writes.
- \`project_delete\` \u2014 delete a text doc by \`path\`. File uploads are read-only via this tool; remove them from the project in claude.ai.
${n?`- \`project_memory_list\` \u2014 list the project's memory files (what Claude has remembered for this project across chats) with sizes and dates. Memory is separate from the docs above and this tool cannot write it.
- \`project_memory_read\` \u2014 read one memory file by \`path\` (as listed by \`project_memory_list\`). Small files return inline; large ones are written to a local file whose path is returned (read it with the ${Qe} tool). Read memory on demand when it is relevant, not every turn. Memory is read once per session: a file changed by another chat after that is not seen until a new session.`:`- \`project_memory_list\`, \`project_memory_read\` \u2014 this tool does not serve project memory in this session, so do not call them. ${y_o}`}

